WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.280
- So, welcome everybody

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.063
to the Public Board of NHS England.

00:00:10.687 --> 00:00:14.040
A break with habit actually is,

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.320
as individuals speak for the first time,

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.890
it's a request we've had from
people watching the webcast.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.390
Just for the first time we speak,

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:22.910
if you could just introduce yourselves,

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.260
by which I don't mean the full bio.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.713
Actually, just who you are

00:00:26.713 --> 00:00:27.683
and are you a non-executive director,

00:00:27.683 --> 00:00:30.270
are you an executive director, et cetera.

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:32.343
That would be very helpful.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.000
Richard Meddings, Chair of NHS England.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.840
Apologies for absence for this meeting.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.087
Vin Diwakar, who's with us this morning,

00:00:43.087 --> 00:00:44.880
unable to be with us this afternoon.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.560
Sarah-Jane Marsh is away,

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.590
and also actually Wol Kolade
and David Behan, again,

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.267
who were in the private
board this morning,

00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:54.210
are unable to join us this afternoon.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.760
And also Mark Bailey who was
dialling in this morning,

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.850
again, unable to be
with us this afternoon.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.807
Can I just test whether
for the public board

00:01:01.807 --> 00:01:04.440
there are any new declarations of interest

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.203
that any of the board need to make?

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:09.603
Thank you very much.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.600
And can I just get approval
from board colleagues

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.573
for the minutes of the
16th of May meeting?

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:21.180
Can I adopt those? Thank you.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.473
Thank you very much.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:28.080
Well, let me just share some
opening remarks from the chair.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.203
And since we last met,
clearly a lot has happened.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:34.230
Very notably a general election

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:36.003
and a change of government.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:40.290
We've also had actually a cyber attack,

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:42.780
which has been impacting our services

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.930
certainly in southeast London.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.940
We had a global IT outage,

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:51.450
which impacted I think,
starting last Friday.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.670
And where the impacts
are, not just in the NHS,

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.850
but generally across
the commercial sector,

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.980
continue to have some impact.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.290
And we also continue to see strike action.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:04.560
So we had further strike action in June.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:05.730
I raise those,

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.010
not because I want to be
particularly depressed

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:09.474
about the continuing challenges,

00:02:09.474 --> 00:02:13.230
because I want to thank
staff across the NHS

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.030
for their very hard work actually

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:18.120
in keeping vital services
going for patients

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.780
through each of these incidents.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:26.460
Now, I'd also just like to
thank two of our colleagues.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:29.190
One actually who has
apologised for this meeting,

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.050
but that's Sir David Behan.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:35.160
And David is leaving the
Board of NHS England,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:39.990
timed out effectively as
a non-executive director.

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:44.990
He has been a really
superb member of the board.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:50.190
And he was originally the Chair of HEE,

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:54.450
Health Education England, from 2018.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:58.020
On the NHS England Board, he served both

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:01.050
as Chair of the People
and Remuneration Committee

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:04.290
and also the Workforce Training
and Education Committee.

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:08.400
And also as a member of
the Quality Committee.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.677
And the Oversight group,

00:03:10.677 --> 00:03:15.677
the committee that oversaw
the merger of essentially four

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:18.540
of the NHS England head office entities,

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:20.340
if I call them, head office entities,

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.477
chaired by Wol Kolade,

00:03:21.477 --> 00:03:24.870
and David also served very,
very diligently on that.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.382
And so I'm sure the board will join me

00:03:27.382 --> 00:03:30.270
actually in wishing David huge success

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.280
and as he continues now

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.130
as Chair of King's College
NHS Foundation Trust.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.630
And just to record in the minutes,

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:38.760
a huge thanks for all the work

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.310
that he's done for NHS England.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:42.143
And I'd also like,

00:03:42.143 --> 00:03:43.617
slightly more awkward
because I think you're here.

00:03:43.617 --> 00:03:46.260
Yes, you are here actually, Helen,

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:48.150
so it's always slightly more awkward,

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:49.470
slightly embarrassing for you I think.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:52.410
But Professor Dame Helen Stokes-Lampard

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:54.360
is going to be leaving us

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.640
and she's leaving us to become

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.120
the Chief Medical Officer
for Health in New Zealand.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:01.217
As I said this morning actually,

00:04:01.217 --> 00:04:02.820
I mean the great agreement is that

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:04.230
I think you are going to stay with us

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:05.610
almost until the very last day

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:08.250
before you get on a plane
to go to New Zealand,

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:09.210
and we welcome that.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:11.070
And while you've been with us on the board

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:13.050
for a relatively short time,

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:16.560
you've been pivotal in
our work and our piloting,

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.360
for instance, in primary care,

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:21.030
and also in our quality agenda.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:22.890
And actually, you've been a force for good

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:25.860
more broadly within the NHS
for the whole of your career

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.890
as a practising GP and a serving academic,

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:31.080
and also as a President of, I think,

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:32.037
Royal College of GP's

00:04:32.037 --> 00:04:35.460
and the Academy of Medical Royal Colleges.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.950
So, thank you very much
and we will miss you,

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:39.117
although you are probably
going to be with us

00:04:39.117 --> 00:04:41.880
for at least a few more weeks, I think.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:43.350
- Thank you.
- Now,

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.570
with those two departures
of the board referenced,

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:47.347
I just wish to,

00:04:47.347 --> 00:04:49.299
so we are looking to undertake

00:04:49.299 --> 00:04:53.430
a non-executive director
recruitment process.

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:55.680
It got slightly delayed
because of the election,

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.120
wholly understandably.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.790
And we're now engaged with our
colleagues in the department

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:03.000
in finalising on the
timeline of essentially

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.993
a new advert which will kick
off that further recruitment.

00:05:08.447 --> 00:05:09.570
This morning,

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:11.730
can I just reflect a little
bit on the board meeting,

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:14.700
the private board meeting that we had?

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:16.650
We changed the format, which was helpful,

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:20.014
on committee approvals and
what's quite often missed

00:05:20.014 --> 00:05:23.460
is actually the scale of work
that colleagues on the board

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:26.130
do outside this boardroom.

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:28.590
And it's also actually outside committees.

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:31.620
But just as a matter of
interest that, you know,

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:33.510
we had I think very good feedback

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:36.060
and conversation around topics covered

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:39.630
since the last board at
various of our committees.

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:43.500
So the Digital Data and
Technology Committee

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:47.760
gave a good report, audit and
risk and assurance, obviously,

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:52.760
the the CFO Advisory
Group Committee, NOMSCO,

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:56.310
we also had Quality Committee feedback

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:58.713
where we are looking very much, Robert,

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:02.730
under your chairmanship
taking over from Simon.

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:04.770
But we are, as we agreed
at the board this morning,

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:06.720
looking at how should we reshape

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.210
and focus that committee's agenda.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.430
And I think that's a very sensible step.

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:14.520
And then clearly we have, I'll
call the People Committee's,

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:17.100
two of those, the People
of Remuneration Committee,

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:21.300
and the Workforce Training
and Education Committee,

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:23.250
both of which David
gave very good feedback

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:27.870
to the board on in his
valedictory remarks.

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:29.490
And then the other topics
covered this morning

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:30.330
that I will mention,

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:33.300
there were several that I won't mention,

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:35.010
but actually a very good conversation

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:38.340
to an approach to frailty.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:40.410
So how do we scale our work

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:43.470
in serving patients with frailty?

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:47.010
Very good discussion, only
actually an hour or so ago,

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:49.740
on innovation and how do we encourage

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:53.790
and catalyse and scale innovation,

00:06:53.790 --> 00:06:57.390
and also actually a review
on long-term infrastructure

00:06:57.390 --> 00:06:59.340
requirements and long-term capital needs.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:01.650
So I think a very rich agenda this morning

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:03.870
in the private board.

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:06.000
Now since the last time we met,

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.780
I've been on several
visits across England,

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:12.570
so including Nottingham
University Hospital,

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:14.580
St. George's NHS Trust.

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:17.910
I went to an extremely,
really, really impressive,

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:19.080
and many of them are so,

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.227
but really impressive
primary care practise

00:07:21.227 --> 00:07:23.040
in London Fields in Hackney,

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.660
which was the Richmond Road Medical Centre

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:28.800
wonderfully embedded in its community.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:33.800
And actually it really was a
truly outstanding visit for me.

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:38.670
And also I went to see Shropshire,
Telford, and Shrewsbury

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:40.320
and during my time on these visits,

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:41.700
I did have the opportunity to speak

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:44.874
to clearly frontline
staff and to patients,

00:07:44.874 --> 00:07:47.130
as well as actually seeing
some innovative ways

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:49.380
to improve services.

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:50.550
Whilst I was in Nottingham,

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:54.840
I heard directly from the team
regarding the improvements

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:56.850
that are occurring within their maternity

00:07:56.850 --> 00:08:00.180
and neonatal services following
their independent review.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:01.740
And at St. George's it was really great

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:04.050
to hear directly from a patient

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:09.050
who had undergone a
major trauma and surgery.

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:10.230
And actually what was interesting to see

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:12.240
how her virtual reality handset was

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:15.930
that she's substantially
improving the pace of her recovery

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:18.840
and her recovering
mobility in a way actually

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:21.480
that without that virtual
reality engagement

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:24.030
was probably going to be
just much more difficult

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:24.963
to achieve.

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:33.920
When I went to Shrewsbury
and Telford, a couple things,

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:38.670
we had a really interesting
and tough conversation

00:08:38.670 --> 00:08:41.760
given some of the pressures
that are being seen

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:45.150
in that system and in the hospital.

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:47.940
But I also had the chance
to see actually the progress

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:49.650
that was being made on the enabling works

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:52.080
in the construction actually
for what I think will be

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:55.890
a very good, new and improved building

00:08:55.890 --> 00:09:00.003
and facility for the
hospital in Shrewsbury.

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:08.386
We're going to go through the agenda,

00:09:08.386 --> 00:09:10.230
we'll have a couple of
normal items to begin with.

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:12.990
So we'll have a performance update, Emily,

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:15.450
which I know you'll lead for us,

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:18.870
and Julian, you'll also
talk to us clearly about

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:21.303
the financial position
that we currently see.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.420
But actually it was interesting

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:27.540
to see published earlier this week,

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:31.590
actually right at the beginning
this week, the NAO report.

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:36.590
And I would actually say it
was a very important report

00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:40.167
on the financial sustainability of the NHS

00:09:40.167 --> 00:09:44.370
and it referenced a
combination of longstanding

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:46.800
as well as recent issues that we face,

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:49.680
including the state of our estate

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:51.630
plus inflationary pressures,

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:54.570
and also actually the cost
of post-pandemic recovery,

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:56.550
and all of those
contributing factors actually

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:58.530
to the pressures that we see.

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:00.930
And there are others as
we've discussed those,

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:03.003
again, this morning.

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:09.540
The NHS has delivered,

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:10.860
and this is important reflection

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:12.780
in that NAO report actually,

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:14.970
and it's a phrase I've often used that

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:17.730
the NHS is delivering and
has delivered record levels

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:21.060
of activity in many key areas.

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:25.470
And that's certainly true
in the last year or two.

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:28.710
And if you look at A&amp;E attendances,

00:10:28.710 --> 00:10:30.630
if we look at hospital admissions,

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:33.570
if we look at the number
of GP appointments,

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:38.220
we are clearly providing
record levels of care.

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:40.020
But, and it's important,

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:43.050
but waiting times and
crucially experience,

00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:46.770
I think it's fair to say is
well below what patients expect

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:49.170
and also what our staff want to provide

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:51.030
and attempt to provide.

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:52.980
And it shouldn't come
as any surprise given

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:55.710
the pressure that the system's under,

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:58.710
and yet the scale of this activity

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:02.070
that our latest monthly data shows

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:07.050
that across the board staff
are under huge pressure.

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:11.010
And whilst they are delivering
so much for patients,

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:12.600
they also know that there needs

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:16.803
to be major improvements
actually in how we're operating.

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:23.990
The waiting list we
saw rose by over 30,000

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:27.543
and the overall backlog
now at 7.6 million,

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:33.381
with 6.3 million people
underlying that 7.6 million

00:11:33.381 --> 00:11:34.950
on the list.

00:11:34.950 --> 00:11:37.410
So 6.3 million people waiting

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:41.760
for 7.6 million treatments
or interventions.

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:44.010
And despite the hard works
and efforts of staff,

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:46.050
we know this isn't again, good enough,

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:48.270
for our patients who when
they're on the waiting list

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:53.270
are waiting either in pain
and/or with significant anxiety.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:56.520
And again, I referenced
this morning actually,

00:11:56.520 --> 00:12:00.660
which again was a recent publication,

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:01.623
and it's actually,

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:07.290
it's the rate of increase
in diagnosis of dementia

00:12:07.290 --> 00:12:09.330
and we're seeing more people than ever

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:11.250
before diagnosed with dementia,

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:14.910
and we have one of the highest
diagnosis rates in the world.

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:17.640
And we know that whilst diagnosis is key

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:20.280
so we can ensure people
get the right support,

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:22.560
it does add extra demand pressure

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:25.740
and pressure on staff to
deal with some of these areas

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:28.323
where we're seeing
surging levels of demand.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:31.110
I would say, I know not just me,

00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:34.470
but the board welcomes
the Lord Ara Darzi review.

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:38.130
He was actually a member
of the NHS England Board

00:12:38.130 --> 00:12:41.130
three years or so ago, I think.

00:12:41.130 --> 00:12:43.230
But we welcome that review announced

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:45.600
by government two weeks ago

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:47.370
and various teams across the NHS

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:49.980
will be working with Lord Darzi

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:51.870
in terms of providing him with information

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:55.650
to enable him to produce his review.

00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:57.690
And clearly it's sort of a springboard

00:12:57.690 --> 00:13:01.109
to the also announced ten year plan

00:13:01.109 --> 00:13:03.540
and that ten year plan should
enable us all, I think,

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:08.540
to build an NHS that's fit for the future.

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:10.590
And then just coming very quickly

00:13:10.590 --> 00:13:12.570
and back to sort of diary management,

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:15.870
and forgive me some of the
meetings I should also mention

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:18.270
that I've had, or engagements I've had,

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:20.490
since the last board were with

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:22.920
the Dean of the Royal
College of Psychiatry,

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:26.250
a very good strategy session
with Genomics England.

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:28.200
So, genomics strategy.

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:31.660
I had a really good
engagement with Lord Bird

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:34.650
who's a founder of the Big Issue

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:36.390
and actually a passionate advocate

00:13:36.390 --> 00:13:39.000
for really addressing poverty

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:41.610
and how do we alleviate poverty.

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:44.580
A very good meeting with Steve's support

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:47.250
with the medical Royal College presidents,

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:49.650
and then meetings with
Alzheimer's Research UK

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:51.600
and with the UK Sepsis Trust,

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:53.370
and then a couple of
meetings with Nuffield Trust,

00:13:53.370 --> 00:13:58.020
one on communication
challenges across the NHS

00:13:58.020 --> 00:14:01.590
and the second actually was
to exchange views with them

00:14:01.590 --> 00:14:05.250
on their recent research into primary care

00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:09.360
and how do we think about transforming

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:11.340
and supporting primary care.

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:15.750
And then I've made a series
of presentations or speeches.

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:19.080
NHS Confederation
Conference as an induction

00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:21.570
for national chairs and
non-executive directors,

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:23.640
the quarterly ICB Chairs meeting.

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:25.050
So every quarter we have a meeting

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:26.973
with all the chairs of the ICBs.

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:31.113
I attended the Genomics
England Research Summit,

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:35.910
an NHS England Clinical
Fellows Insight Day.

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:37.680
I spoke last week, I think,

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:40.140
at the King's Fund
Digital Health Conference.

00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:42.480
And earlier on this week I was delighted

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:43.863
to be able to go to,

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:48.210
it's a lobby group called 50:50

00:14:48.210 --> 00:14:49.870
and it lobbies and promotes

00:14:51.210 --> 00:14:54.240
that 50% of the members
of the House of Commons

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:55.530
should be female.

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:57.630
And I think it was a brilliant evening,

00:14:57.630 --> 00:15:00.060
the focus of the evening
actually was on women's health,

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:03.990
and I attended that and was
delighted to be able to go.

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:07.980
So that would be a, that's
all for me, I think,

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:10.440
in terms of a chair's summary.

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:13.140
I'm happy to take any
questions or reactions

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:15.750
but I suspect we'll be able to move on

00:15:15.750 --> 00:15:17.250
to the better discussion,

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:18.930
which will be the chief executives report.

00:15:18.930 --> 00:15:19.920
Amanda?

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:21.393
- No, not at all Richard.

00:15:22.290 --> 00:15:24.810
So can I just start by echoing

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:26.490
what Richard, you've just said,

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:29.910
in relation to David Behan
and to Helen Stokes-Lampard

00:15:29.910 --> 00:15:32.550
who've been two hugely
valuable members of the board,

00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:33.990
and will be greatly missed.

00:15:33.990 --> 00:15:35.730
And thank you to both you, Helen,

00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:37.380
and David, in his absence,

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:39.930
for everything that
you have brought to us.

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:42.960
In a more positive announcement though,

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:44.760
I would also like, if I may,

00:15:44.760 --> 00:15:48.480
to welcome Duncan Burton to the board

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:52.230
in his capacity now officially as of today

00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:55.140
as the Chief Nurse for England,

00:15:55.140 --> 00:15:59.031
replacing Ruth May who
has retired after 40,

00:15:59.031 --> 00:16:04.031
incredibly impressive and dedicated years

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:05.700
of service to the NHS.

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:09.060
So, we wish Ruth all the
very best for her retirement.

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:12.270
She goes with an enormous, I think,

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:14.970
load of gratitude and
thanks from colleagues

00:16:14.970 --> 00:16:17.850
not just in this room but across the NHS.

00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:20.850
But Duncan, welcome,
we're absolutely delighted

00:16:20.850 --> 00:16:24.750
to have you now officially
as our Chief Nurse,

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:27.810
and I know you bring
a wealth of experience

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:30.960
from across local,
regional, national roles,

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:32.700
a real track record of delivery,

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:36.510
and actually a real record
of values-based leadership

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:37.920
to the role.

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:41.280
And then it is equally, I think,

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:45.150
on me to welcome and also thank

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:48.810
Dr. Michael Gregory and Robert
Cornell who have stepped up

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:51.120
as Interim Regional
Directors for, respectively,

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:53.160
the Northwestern and the
Northeastern Yorkshire

00:16:53.160 --> 00:16:55.680
following Richard Barker's retirement,

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:58.620
who again, is someone
who's given huge service,

00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:03.240
a real public servant to
the NHS over many decades.

00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:05.400
So, we thank Richard, we
wish him all the best,

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:09.030
and we thank Michael and
Robert for stepping up.

00:17:09.030 --> 00:17:11.430
But it is a good time, Duncan,

00:17:11.430 --> 00:17:14.400
to be joining us because it is a,

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:16.770
as you said Richard,

00:17:16.770 --> 00:17:20.670
an exciting time now with
a change of government,

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:21.750
and a busy time.

00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:23.430
So I would just like to thank colleagues,

00:17:23.430 --> 00:17:26.340
particularly in NHS England,

00:17:26.340 --> 00:17:27.270
for all the work that they have done

00:17:27.270 --> 00:17:29.400
to help prepare for a change of,

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:30.690
or a potential change of government,

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:34.200
which has now become an
actual change of government.

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:38.400
We have had really
positive early engagement

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:41.340
with the new Secretary of
State and ministerial teams

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:46.340
and the focus on greater
joint working between us.

00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:50.520
Like for example, the
inclusion in a town hall event

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:52.200
that the Secretary of State hosted,

00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:53.817
not just for the
Department of Health staff,

00:17:53.817 --> 00:17:56.820
for other colleagues in other ARBs

00:17:56.820 --> 00:17:59.080
and for NHS England was, I think,

00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:02.520
very welcome indeed and a real signal

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:05.220
of wanting to work very closely with us.

00:18:05.220 --> 00:18:06.723
So, hugely welcome.

00:18:07.620 --> 00:18:11.010
Early announcements have
focused, as you've said Richard,

00:18:11.010 --> 00:18:16.010
on the review that Lord Ara Darzi is doing

00:18:16.143 --> 00:18:18.823
on current challenges faced by the NHS

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:23.580
and that will feed into the development

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:25.740
of a new 10 year plan.

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:28.230
Importantly, and again
we've touched on this,

00:18:28.230 --> 00:18:32.430
that will be for Health,
not just for the NHS.

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:33.810
That's really important

00:18:33.810 --> 00:18:37.050
because not only is it
a very different lens,

00:18:37.050 --> 00:18:40.393
but of course it is the
sustainability of the NHS

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:47.220
is dependent, or so dependent really,

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:48.510
on the health of the nation.

00:18:48.510 --> 00:18:50.190
And that is one of the issues, I think,

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:53.370
that did come through
the NAO report this week.

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:54.510
And indeed it's one of the things

00:18:54.510 --> 00:18:56.250
that was very much, I think,

00:18:56.250 --> 00:19:01.050
a feature of the COVID
Inquiry Module 1 report.

00:19:01.050 --> 00:19:03.660
So huge amount of work
that will take place

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:07.980
over the next weeks and months
on both Lord Darzi's review

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:11.673
and those first steps in
developing the 10 year plan.

00:19:12.810 --> 00:19:17.070
But I'm very pleased that already

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:20.700
the engagement has started
with colleagues across the NHS,

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:24.480
visits are already underway,
I know, from Lord Darzi,

00:19:24.480 --> 00:19:28.350
and it's very clear that
the team that are coming in

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:30.150
to support the Secretary of State,

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:32.760
which we're obviously also feeding into,

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:34.320
will be seeing this

00:19:34.320 --> 00:19:36.690
as a really important engagement exercise

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:38.520
with colleagues across the NHS,

00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:41.910
much wider engagement
also with stakeholders,

00:19:41.910 --> 00:19:44.520
and with the public themselves

00:19:44.520 --> 00:19:47.850
on their experiences and what
they want from the future.

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:49.500
So it's a very exciting opportunity

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:51.963
I think for us all to be involved in that.

00:19:53.520 --> 00:19:58.520
I do just want to highlight
as part of this though,

00:19:58.650 --> 00:20:01.410
the importance of the three shifts

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:04.470
that the government talked about

00:20:04.470 --> 00:20:07.253
as part of the run-up to the election,

00:20:07.253 --> 00:20:10.410
and the thing that I think
is particularly encouraging

00:20:10.410 --> 00:20:11.790
from this board's perspective,

00:20:11.790 --> 00:20:14.040
is these are themes that
we have been talking about

00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:16.290
ourselves for some time.

00:20:16.290 --> 00:20:19.260
So the importance of that shift,

00:20:19.260 --> 00:20:24.260
moving care out of hospital
into community and primary care,

00:20:25.230 --> 00:20:28.830
moving from an analogue to
a digital way of working.

00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:31.830
So, better utilising technology and data.

00:20:31.830 --> 00:20:33.450
And of course that focus on a shift

00:20:33.450 --> 00:20:35.190
from treatment to prevention.

00:20:35.190 --> 00:20:36.990
All of those things,

00:20:36.990 --> 00:20:39.210
improving experience for patients,

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:40.860
improving value for money,

00:20:40.860 --> 00:20:43.500
improving how we use the facilities,

00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:45.630
the assets that we have
within the health service.

00:20:45.630 --> 00:20:48.420
All of them really ambitious,

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:50.040
really difficult to deliver,

00:20:50.040 --> 00:20:51.120
but all of them right,

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:52.920
and all of the things that I think

00:20:52.920 --> 00:20:54.630
we would strongly recognise as the things

00:20:54.630 --> 00:20:56.221
that we've been working away at

00:20:56.221 --> 00:20:59.160
over the past few years as well,

00:20:59.160 --> 00:21:00.900
and delighted to have the opportunity

00:21:00.900 --> 00:21:05.043
to really turbocharge the
focus on those things.

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:07.620
Just talking about early policies,

00:21:07.620 --> 00:21:10.560
there have been some very specific things

00:21:10.560 --> 00:21:13.440
that again, were flagged
clearly in manifestos,

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:18.360
which we are now very, very much working

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:19.770
with colleagues at the
Department of Health

00:21:19.770 --> 00:21:22.770
and Social Care to operationalize,

00:21:22.770 --> 00:21:25.590
thinking particularly
in these first few days

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:27.180
about elective activity.

00:21:27.180 --> 00:21:31.980
So, the pledge to step up
elective activity as a first step

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:35.160
is something we have been planning on now

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:36.480
in the run-up to the election

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:38.819
and are now in the sort of final stages

00:21:38.819 --> 00:21:42.540
of putting the detail around that.

00:21:42.540 --> 00:21:43.373
In delivering that,

00:21:43.373 --> 00:21:46.440
and of course anything else
that we are talking about today,

00:21:46.440 --> 00:21:51.150
we do rely on having the
staff around to do the work.

00:21:51.150 --> 00:21:53.490
So we've been really pleased

00:21:53.490 --> 00:21:57.480
to see talks resuming with junior doctors.

00:21:57.480 --> 00:22:01.710
We do also have the
forthcoming intention by GP's

00:22:01.710 --> 00:22:03.870
to take collective action as part

00:22:03.870 --> 00:22:06.600
of their contractual dispute.

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:08.430
So just to note that we did write

00:22:08.430 --> 00:22:11.520
to the NHS earlier this week

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:15.360
to ask colleagues to
assess readiness for action

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:18.180
that might be taken by contract holders.

00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:21.600
Obviously, it's not going
to be the same as the action

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:24.690
that's been taken by junior doctors,

00:22:24.690 --> 00:22:28.290
but we do know, we've talked
about it a lot as a board,

00:22:28.290 --> 00:22:30.900
general practise primary care

00:22:30.900 --> 00:22:33.690
is the absolute bedrock of the NHS.

00:22:33.690 --> 00:22:35.580
So any action taken in primary care

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:37.920
won't just have an impact on primary care.

00:22:37.920 --> 00:22:40.980
It is the knock on
impact to other services,

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:45.150
secondary care elective
and urgent emergency care.

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:46.860
So there's a lot of work to do to prepare

00:22:46.860 --> 00:22:50.133
for a potential impact if that goes ahead.

00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:53.940
Three other things, Chair,

00:22:53.940 --> 00:22:54.900
I just wanted to make sure

00:22:54.900 --> 00:22:57.690
that I had highlighted to the board.

00:22:57.690 --> 00:23:01.230
So, just linked with the
point I've just made about

00:23:01.230 --> 00:23:03.750
where we are with
industrial relations issues,

00:23:03.750 --> 00:23:06.090
colleagues will be aware
that we're expecting

00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:09.390
the outcome of the pay
review body processes

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:13.290
for the current financial
year and future years shortly.

00:23:13.290 --> 00:23:16.500
As ever we will be and
are working very closely

00:23:16.500 --> 00:23:18.840
with colleagues at the Department
of Health and Social Care

00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:22.290
and beyond to support
assessment of affordability.

00:23:22.290 --> 00:23:26.880
Julian's finance update is
going to give us a picture later

00:23:26.880 --> 00:23:29.280
of just how important that is in a year

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:31.170
we knew was going to be very stretching,

00:23:31.170 --> 00:23:33.676
and is proving to be.

00:23:33.676 --> 00:23:35.820
Second thing I just wanted to say is,

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:39.180
we are again sort of
getting slightly ahead

00:23:39.180 --> 00:23:42.324
of what Emily is going to be
talking to us about shortly,

00:23:42.324 --> 00:23:47.190
getting ready to set out our expectations

00:23:47.190 --> 00:23:49.800
and support for colleagues across the NHS

00:23:49.800 --> 00:23:52.560
in relation to planning for winter.

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:57.560
So that will build on much
of the really detailed work

00:23:59.940 --> 00:24:01.680
that was done to put together

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:04.320
the urgent emergency care recovery plan,

00:24:04.320 --> 00:24:06.480
high impact interventions,

00:24:06.480 --> 00:24:09.690
but we've talked a lot about
really wanting to make sure

00:24:09.690 --> 00:24:12.750
that we are putting a
focus on patient safety

00:24:12.750 --> 00:24:16.470
and patient experience at
the heart of our approach

00:24:16.470 --> 00:24:18.210
to winter this year,

00:24:18.210 --> 00:24:21.360
at both trust and system level.

00:24:21.360 --> 00:24:24.360
And I think any of us who watched,

00:24:24.360 --> 00:24:25.590
and I'm sure we all did,

00:24:25.590 --> 00:24:28.740
the Dispatches documentary a few weeks ago

00:24:28.740 --> 00:24:32.760
shows what happens when
we don't get that right.

00:24:32.760 --> 00:24:34.643
And we have to, I think,

00:24:34.643 --> 00:24:37.350
have at the heart of
our approach for winter

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:40.080
a recognition that whilst we cannot solve,

00:24:40.080 --> 00:24:41.730
to your point Richard,

00:24:41.730 --> 00:24:44.550
all the challenges that are
facing the NHS by this winter,

00:24:44.550 --> 00:24:45.720
we shouldn't be, you know,

00:24:45.720 --> 00:24:47.100
under any illusion about that.

00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:48.930
There are huge challenges

00:24:48.930 --> 00:24:51.570
and huge pressures in the system.

00:24:51.570 --> 00:24:53.400
When we do end up in a situation

00:24:53.400 --> 00:24:55.680
where patients are not in the right place

00:24:55.680 --> 00:24:57.750
or they're waiting too long,

00:24:57.750 --> 00:25:00.900
the onus on ensuring that
the care that they're getting

00:25:00.900 --> 00:25:02.820
is as good as it possibly can be,

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:05.040
and as safe as it possibly can be

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:07.260
is absolutely at the heart

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:10.890
of how we need to support
colleagues in the NHS

00:25:10.890 --> 00:25:12.990
to meet the needs of patients.

00:25:12.990 --> 00:25:16.200
And that will be true
for this winter, sadly.

00:25:16.200 --> 00:25:18.840
But it is something that
we need to make sure sits,

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:20.880
I think, at the heart
of what we'll be talking

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:24.000
to colleagues across the
NHS about over the next few,

00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:25.500
well, days I think, possibly,

00:25:25.500 --> 00:25:27.570
because we are looking
to get something out

00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:30.240
very soon on that.

00:25:30.240 --> 00:25:34.230
Final thing is just on how we work.

00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:37.080
So, colleagues know
that we've been through

00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:39.750
an enormous restructure within NHS England

00:25:39.750 --> 00:25:43.080
over the course of the
past year, 18 months.

00:25:43.080 --> 00:25:44.970
There's still a massive amount of work now

00:25:44.970 --> 00:25:46.710
going on internally to ensure

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:48.420
that we really do reap the benefits

00:25:48.420 --> 00:25:50.400
of being the new NHS England

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:52.980
with all of the advantages
of having colleagues

00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:54.390
from what used to be HEE

00:25:54.390 --> 00:25:56.730
and what used to be NHS Digital

00:25:56.730 --> 00:25:59.730
as part of this national organisation.

00:25:59.730 --> 00:26:02.730
Equally of course that's
replicated at regional level.

00:26:02.730 --> 00:26:06.900
We also have the huge opportunity
created over the last,

00:26:06.900 --> 00:26:11.900
what, now nearly three
years, of ICS's, ICB's,

00:26:12.270 --> 00:26:14.040
42 systems across the NHS,

00:26:14.040 --> 00:26:16.770
as well as all of the structures
that were there before.

00:26:16.770 --> 00:26:21.770
We continue to focus on, under
Steve Russell's leadership,

00:26:22.290 --> 00:26:24.030
but with input from a huge number

00:26:24.030 --> 00:26:25.950
of other colleagues as well,

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:29.610
how we best work as NHS England

00:26:29.610 --> 00:26:31.020
with colleagues in regions,

00:26:31.020 --> 00:26:33.720
with colleagues in ICB's, ICS's,

00:26:33.720 --> 00:26:34.920
with colleagues in trust's,

00:26:34.920 --> 00:26:38.790
so that the collective whole
is as effective as it can be.

00:26:38.790 --> 00:26:40.980
That feels to me to be something

00:26:40.980 --> 00:26:44.490
that just needs to remain
on all of our radars

00:26:44.490 --> 00:26:46.350
as we go through the next weeks and months

00:26:46.350 --> 00:26:49.800
because we will be, I think,

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:52.650
just continuing to iterate
our operating model

00:26:52.650 --> 00:26:53.997
so that we put quality and safety

00:26:53.997 --> 00:26:56.190
at the heart of everything that we do,

00:26:56.190 --> 00:26:58.230
so that we are able to respond

00:26:58.230 --> 00:26:59.820
to the immediate operational pressures,

00:26:59.820 --> 00:27:02.700
be that winter, be that elective recovery,

00:27:02.700 --> 00:27:04.470
be that continuing to deliver

00:27:04.470 --> 00:27:06.480
on our financial responsibilities,

00:27:06.480 --> 00:27:09.580
but that we are also able to get ourselves

00:27:11.040 --> 00:27:14.760
ready to input in a really constructive

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:16.550
and with a level of ambition

00:27:16.550 --> 00:27:20.130
that I know we all have into
the plans for the longer term.

00:27:20.130 --> 00:27:22.410
And that we're then in the
strongest possible place

00:27:22.410 --> 00:27:26.010
to support colleagues across
the NHS to deliver now,

00:27:26.010 --> 00:27:30.600
but also to be ready to
deliver a very exciting

00:27:30.600 --> 00:27:34.800
and ambitious set of
things for our patients

00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:36.750
and for our staff over the longer term.

00:27:38.400 --> 00:27:39.510
- Thank you.

00:27:39.510 --> 00:27:44.510
Any comments or questions for
Amanda on the CEO's update?

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:47.250
And the only thing I would add

00:27:47.250 --> 00:27:50.550
before we move on then to
the operational performance,

00:27:50.550 --> 00:27:52.770
Amanda, just to say thank you very much

00:27:52.770 --> 00:27:56.340
on behalf of the board just
for the pace and diligence

00:27:56.340 --> 00:27:58.950
and rigour of the engagement.

00:27:58.950 --> 00:28:01.080
It's always been a strong engagement,

00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:02.550
but actually in the
last two to three weeks

00:28:02.550 --> 00:28:03.383
with the new government,

00:28:03.383 --> 00:28:05.850
I know how hard you and the executives

00:28:05.850 --> 00:28:07.980
have been working in
providing briefings to

00:28:07.980 --> 00:28:10.517
and engaging in meetings
with the new government,

00:28:10.517 --> 00:28:12.570
and thank you for all that effort,

00:28:12.570 --> 00:28:14.400
which has been extremely good.

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:16.803
Very good, strong engagement.

00:28:18.150 --> 00:28:21.660
Okay Emily, can I ask
you to give us an update

00:28:21.660 --> 00:28:23.010
on operational performance?

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:24.713
- Thank you.

00:28:26.574 --> 00:28:29.730
So the paper covers the
NHS's operational performance

00:28:29.730 --> 00:28:31.200
across May and June,

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:34.701
with published data varying
depending on the source.

00:28:34.701 --> 00:28:36.960
And the performance story this month is,

00:28:36.960 --> 00:28:39.920
as I mentioned last time
and I think the time before,

00:28:39.920 --> 00:28:43.410
one of a combination of
impressive achievement

00:28:43.410 --> 00:28:44.790
at the same time as many areas

00:28:44.790 --> 00:28:46.560
where we clearly need to do better.

00:28:46.560 --> 00:28:48.510
And I'll pull these out as we go along.

00:28:50.732 --> 00:28:52.410
A few weeks ago, Amanda, Julian and I met

00:28:52.410 --> 00:28:54.720
with all 42 systems over the
period of a couple of weeks

00:28:54.720 --> 00:28:57.000
to test their plans for this year,

00:28:57.000 --> 00:28:59.130
and it was impressive and encouraging

00:28:59.130 --> 00:29:01.110
to see the work that's gone
in to develop stretching

00:29:01.110 --> 00:29:04.830
and realistic plans across
the country for 24/25.

00:29:04.830 --> 00:29:07.440
And the work that ICSs have
done to balance the delivery

00:29:07.440 --> 00:29:09.600
of all three critical
components of delivery.

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:12.603
So financial, quality, and
operational performance.

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:15.240
We are now in Q2,

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:18.120
although the data I'll report
on here will refer to Q1.

00:29:18.120 --> 00:29:20.820
So we're focusing on delivery
mode ahead of the challenges

00:29:20.820 --> 00:29:23.970
that are always placed
on the system in winter.

00:29:23.970 --> 00:29:25.380
That includes pressure, obviously,

00:29:25.380 --> 00:29:27.960
on financials, quality and performance.

00:29:27.960 --> 00:29:30.720
And we are particularly
focused on the headline areas

00:29:30.720 --> 00:29:31.770
of UEC and elective,

00:29:31.770 --> 00:29:36.360
but the pressure's across all
aspects of system delivery.

00:29:36.360 --> 00:29:37.950
Looking ahead to those challenges,

00:29:37.950 --> 00:29:40.740
we are thinking through our
operational planning for winter

00:29:40.740 --> 00:29:42.960
and will shortly be
communicating with the system

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:44.580
to set out our approach to winter

00:29:44.580 --> 00:29:46.470
drawing on the UEC recovery plan

00:29:46.470 --> 00:29:48.563
and the system operational plans,

00:29:48.563 --> 00:29:51.420
and obviously including
the links into social care,

00:29:51.420 --> 00:29:54.060
which is critical for local delivery.

00:29:54.060 --> 00:29:55.800
We know we're making progress

00:29:55.800 --> 00:29:57.420
and there are some really
good examples of that

00:29:57.420 --> 00:29:58.740
in the report and we'll cover some

00:29:58.740 --> 00:30:01.320
in the cancer paper later in the meeting.

00:30:01.320 --> 00:30:04.200
But there is a long way to go
to make the strides required

00:30:04.200 --> 00:30:06.120
to deliver constitutional standards

00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:08.010
and to consistently provide the quality

00:30:08.010 --> 00:30:10.590
and quantity of care
that we all want to see.

00:30:10.590 --> 00:30:12.750
And I've spoken to various systems,

00:30:12.750 --> 00:30:15.420
including all the ICS
leads a couple of days ago,

00:30:15.420 --> 00:30:19.980
about the impact that
the Dispatches programme

00:30:19.980 --> 00:30:22.530
has given all of us on
the focus on respect

00:30:22.530 --> 00:30:24.900
and care as it's delivered locally,

00:30:24.900 --> 00:30:26.300
particularly under pressure.

00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:30.030
We have had very constructive
early engagement,

00:30:30.030 --> 00:30:32.100
as Amanda's mentioned,
with the new government,

00:30:32.100 --> 00:30:34.770
on what we need to do to
return to performance standards

00:30:34.770 --> 00:30:38.497
and I'll be able to update the
board on that in due course.

00:30:38.497 --> 00:30:40.440
And as Amanda has already mentioned,

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:42.120
on top of business-as-usual challenges,

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:45.840
the NHS has continued to face
disruptive industrial action.

00:30:45.840 --> 00:30:48.720
The junior doctor strike
between Thursday 27th of June

00:30:48.720 --> 00:30:50.640
and Tuesday the 2nd of July

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:55.640
saw 61,989 inpatient and
outpatient appointments rescheduled

00:30:56.430 --> 00:30:59.190
and 23,001 staff were absent from work

00:30:59.190 --> 00:31:01.620
due to strikes at the peak of the action.

00:31:01.620 --> 00:31:05.010
So we recognise that on
those strike days, as always,

00:31:05.010 --> 00:31:07.260
there is an extraordinary
effort going in from colleagues

00:31:07.260 --> 00:31:11.400
to ensure safe, effective
and timely patient care.

00:31:11.400 --> 00:31:14.280
And the places where I'm saying
we've got a long way to go

00:31:14.280 --> 00:31:16.500
are not in any way a comment on the effort

00:31:16.500 --> 00:31:17.970
that's given every day at the frontline,

00:31:17.970 --> 00:31:21.570
but more on what it takes
to deliver day after day

00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:24.573
across whole pathways
and across whole systems.

00:31:25.530 --> 00:31:27.570
If I then pull out some of the highlights

00:31:27.570 --> 00:31:28.590
of the performance data,

00:31:28.590 --> 00:31:29.910
or the key points of performance data,

00:31:29.910 --> 00:31:33.060
which are in the document,
starting with UEC,

00:31:33.060 --> 00:31:36.303
which refers to data for June, 2024.

00:31:37.290 --> 00:31:42.120
In UEC we saw high pressure
in June including hot weather,

00:31:42.120 --> 00:31:45.030
which included several
yellow heat health warnings

00:31:45.030 --> 00:31:47.490
and four days of strikes,
as just discussed.

00:31:47.490 --> 00:31:50.490
And all of that creates extra
pressure for frontline teams,

00:31:50.490 --> 00:31:52.320
different cases and different intensities

00:31:52.320 --> 00:31:53.613
of people arriving in ED.

00:31:54.600 --> 00:31:59.600
We saw 3.3% higher ED attendances
in June '24 than in '23

00:32:00.090 --> 00:32:01.620
and ambulance services responded

00:32:01.620 --> 00:32:03.990
to more category one and two incidents

00:32:03.990 --> 00:32:07.020
in June '24 than in June '23,

00:32:07.020 --> 00:32:09.101
which is a 3.2% increase,

00:32:09.101 --> 00:32:14.100
and a 2.6% increase in
incidents respectively.

00:32:14.100 --> 00:32:15.510
Despite that pressure,

00:32:15.510 --> 00:32:18.120
there was an improvement four
hour performance in June,

00:32:18.120 --> 00:32:22.170
up 0.6% on the previous month of 74.6%,

00:32:22.170 --> 00:32:25.893
and above the 73.3% seen in June 2023.

00:32:26.760 --> 00:32:28.380
On category two response times,

00:32:28.380 --> 00:32:30.870
unfortunately there was
decline in performance

00:32:30.870 --> 00:32:33.570
up to 34 minutes and 38 seconds in June,

00:32:33.570 --> 00:32:35.880
almost two minutes above
the figure for May,

00:32:35.880 --> 00:32:38.460
which we put down to increased demand

00:32:38.460 --> 00:32:41.940
and longer ambulance
handover delays at trusts.

00:32:41.940 --> 00:32:44.340
Clearly, we need to
see performance improve

00:32:44.340 --> 00:32:46.110
as we work towards our overall target

00:32:46.110 --> 00:32:48.210
to achieve an average
response time of 30 minutes

00:32:48.210 --> 00:32:49.743
across 24/25.

00:32:50.820 --> 00:32:52.260
Colleagues will also be aware

00:32:52.260 --> 00:32:54.210
that there are ongoing challenges

00:32:54.210 --> 00:32:56.370
with the number of patients
waiting 12 hours or more

00:32:56.370 --> 00:32:58.920
to be admitted, transferred or discharged.

00:32:58.920 --> 00:33:02.790
And June for example saw
43% more 12 hour delays

00:33:02.790 --> 00:33:04.680
than June '23.

00:33:04.680 --> 00:33:06.540
So we are very conscious
that these metrics

00:33:06.540 --> 00:33:08.310
that aren't always reported as a headline

00:33:08.310 --> 00:33:10.560
are ones that we need to pay attention to

00:33:10.560 --> 00:33:13.050
in terms of improving
clinical quality of care

00:33:13.050 --> 00:33:15.120
and patient experience.

00:33:15.120 --> 00:33:17.520
Overall, the work everybody's
done to improve UEC

00:33:17.520 --> 00:33:18.930
particularly since the publication

00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:20.940
of the UEC plan in January '23,

00:33:20.940 --> 00:33:23.220
and bearing in mind all the
actions people have taken

00:33:23.220 --> 00:33:24.660
in response to the year two letter,

00:33:24.660 --> 00:33:26.700
which was published in April,

00:33:26.700 --> 00:33:28.140
means that there's a very clear focus

00:33:28.140 --> 00:33:29.970
on improvements in the system

00:33:29.970 --> 00:33:32.010
and lessons learned from things

00:33:32.010 --> 00:33:34.140
which were trialled in year one,

00:33:34.140 --> 00:33:35.850
which we've taken forward into this year

00:33:35.850 --> 00:33:38.790
and will reiterate in the winter letter.

00:33:38.790 --> 00:33:41.640
But as I said, there's a long way to go.

00:33:41.640 --> 00:33:43.710
We need to make sure we make
the most of the improvements

00:33:43.710 --> 00:33:46.410
that every individual
organisation has put in place

00:33:46.410 --> 00:33:50.130
and make sure we can scale
those quickly and appropriately.

00:33:50.130 --> 00:33:51.120
We need to work effectively

00:33:51.120 --> 00:33:52.620
across organisational boundaries,

00:33:52.620 --> 00:33:55.191
particularly locally with social care,

00:33:55.191 --> 00:33:57.150
but also with ambulance services.

00:33:57.150 --> 00:33:59.550
And we need to ensure
everyone across acute trusts

00:33:59.550 --> 00:34:02.250
takes responsibility for
their role in A&amp;E performance,

00:34:02.250 --> 00:34:04.378
IE, not just the people in A&amp;E.

00:34:04.378 --> 00:34:07.140
This is a whole system requirement

00:34:07.140 --> 00:34:09.570
if we're really going to improve.

00:34:09.570 --> 00:34:10.950
I'll keep going with the other topics

00:34:10.950 --> 00:34:12.480
and then obviously we can
pick up whichever ones

00:34:12.480 --> 00:34:14.280
people are interested in.

00:34:14.280 --> 00:34:15.633
If we look at electives,

00:34:17.610 --> 00:34:19.320
this remains a key focus

00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:21.750
because we need to treat
people as speedily as possible

00:34:21.750 --> 00:34:23.370
and we've been making
progress on our goals

00:34:23.370 --> 00:34:25.260
in elective recovery despite the impacts

00:34:25.260 --> 00:34:26.940
from industrial action.

00:34:26.940 --> 00:34:29.100
In May, the total waiting list increased

00:34:29.100 --> 00:34:31.920
for only the second time
since October, 2023,

00:34:31.920 --> 00:34:36.420
with an increase of 31,249
patients since April '24,

00:34:36.420 --> 00:34:38.670
standing at 7.61 million.

00:34:38.670 --> 00:34:41.820
That equates to an estimated
6.38 million people

00:34:41.820 --> 00:34:43.530
waiting for treatment.

00:34:43.530 --> 00:34:45.660
We look at the key metrics
including long waits,

00:34:45.660 --> 00:34:49.230
which has been the focus of
the elective recovery plan.

00:34:49.230 --> 00:34:53.993
78-week waits decreased in
May from 5,013 to 4,597,

00:34:56.010 --> 00:35:00.270
although 65-week waits increased by 11%

00:35:00.270 --> 00:35:04.843
from 50,397 to 55,955.

00:35:06.180 --> 00:35:08.670
We've seen an increase in
65-week waits in recent months,

00:35:08.670 --> 00:35:10.980
which obviously is not
what we're heading for,

00:35:10.980 --> 00:35:12.600
but it's worth noting that

00:35:12.600 --> 00:35:14.400
that's increased since
the turn of the year.

00:35:14.400 --> 00:35:18.055
In January, we were
looking at 92,213 people,

00:35:18.055 --> 00:35:20.100
meaning we've seen a reduction overall

00:35:20.100 --> 00:35:21.810
since the turn of the year.

00:35:21.810 --> 00:35:24.930
What we've also seen in unpublished data

00:35:24.930 --> 00:35:27.450
is that the cohort of people waiting,

00:35:27.450 --> 00:35:30.120
who will have waited
65-week waits by September,

00:35:30.120 --> 00:35:32.880
has been coming down
over this period as well.

00:35:32.880 --> 00:35:35.340
In terms of the published data, again,

00:35:35.340 --> 00:35:38.730
the data shows us that
50% of all 65-week waits

00:35:38.730 --> 00:35:41.970
are located in 18 providers
across the country.

00:35:41.970 --> 00:35:43.950
So it's clear where we
need to focus our efforts

00:35:43.950 --> 00:35:46.380
and the elective team
has those trusts in sight

00:35:46.380 --> 00:35:47.820
and is working closely with them

00:35:47.820 --> 00:35:50.490
to make sure they have the
operational support required

00:35:50.490 --> 00:35:52.350
so that we can move towards the target

00:35:52.350 --> 00:35:55.863
to virtually eliminate 65-week
waits by September, 2024.

00:35:57.030 --> 00:36:00.150
And noting that the data
on 52-week waits means

00:36:00.150 --> 00:36:01.710
we still have a long way to go.

00:36:01.710 --> 00:36:06.050
Those waits increased by
4,911, which is 1.62%,

00:36:06.930 --> 00:36:11.133
to 307,500 at the end of the month.

00:36:12.570 --> 00:36:16.470
In diagnostics, we continue
to deliver record number

00:36:16.470 --> 00:36:20.190
of diagnostics checks, tests and checks.

00:36:20.190 --> 00:36:22.860
In May, there were 2.42
million patients checked,

00:36:22.860 --> 00:36:26.460
which is up more than a fifth
since before the pandemic.

00:36:26.460 --> 00:36:28.920
Despite the major increase
in activity, however,

00:36:28.920 --> 00:36:31.270
the overall waiting list
has continued to grow.

00:36:32.280 --> 00:36:34.920
The latest published data shows
community diagnostic centres

00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:37.230
have delivered over 9.58 million tests

00:36:37.230 --> 00:36:40.590
since the first CDC was
launched in July, 2021.

00:36:40.590 --> 00:36:45.420
We now have 170 CDCs approved
with 165 operational,

00:36:45.420 --> 00:36:48.243
108 of which are now in
their permanent sites.

00:36:50.040 --> 00:36:52.410
In terms of cancer, which
we'll come to later,

00:36:52.410 --> 00:36:54.820
but just to give the
operational performance

00:36:55.890 --> 00:36:57.450
at this point in the meeting,

00:36:57.450 --> 00:37:00.060
we improved performance
against all three standards

00:37:00.060 --> 00:37:03.390
when comparing May '24 to May '23.

00:37:03.390 --> 00:37:05.040
The faster diagnostic standard was met

00:37:05.040 --> 00:37:06.510
for the third time in four months

00:37:06.510 --> 00:37:10.410
with 76.4% of patients
receiving a cancer diagnosis,

00:37:10.410 --> 00:37:12.690
or having cancer ruled out within 28 days

00:37:12.690 --> 00:37:14.490
of being urgently referred.

00:37:14.490 --> 00:37:17.400
Performance against the 31-day
combined treatment standard

00:37:17.400 --> 00:37:20.372
was 91.8%.

00:37:20.372 --> 00:37:21.240
And in May performance against

00:37:21.240 --> 00:37:23.160
the cancer 62-day urgent referral

00:37:23.160 --> 00:37:26.490
to first treatment standard was 65.8%,

00:37:26.490 --> 00:37:28.680
which is below where we want to see it.

00:37:28.680 --> 00:37:30.720
The NHS has set the
ambition to improve this

00:37:30.720 --> 00:37:33.000
to 70% to March 2025

00:37:33.000 --> 00:37:34.517
as a first step in recovering performance

00:37:34.517 --> 00:37:36.840
to the 85% standard.

00:37:36.840 --> 00:37:38.640
And this month, which is an improvement

00:37:38.640 --> 00:37:39.990
over the same time last year,

00:37:39.990 --> 00:37:43.440
represents a positive
step in terms of doing so.

00:37:43.440 --> 00:37:46.620
Finally, on mental health we've
seen pressures remain high

00:37:46.620 --> 00:37:48.360
across the sector with increased demand,

00:37:48.360 --> 00:37:50.520
as with the other areas I've spoken about,

00:37:50.520 --> 00:37:52.800
and the recent industrial
action has also shone a light

00:37:52.800 --> 00:37:55.680
on mental health pressures
across providers.

00:37:55.680 --> 00:37:58.740
The urgent and emergency
mental health pathway continues

00:37:58.740 --> 00:38:00.690
to be under significant pressure

00:38:00.690 --> 00:38:04.320
with very high bed occupancy
of between 95 and 97%

00:38:04.320 --> 00:38:06.600
since May, 2022.

00:38:06.600 --> 00:38:08.820
Continued focus remains
to improve patient flow

00:38:08.820 --> 00:38:10.110
and work towards eliminating

00:38:10.110 --> 00:38:12.060
the inappropriate, out-of-area placements,

00:38:12.060 --> 00:38:15.570
which tend to result from
very high occupancy locally.

00:38:15.570 --> 00:38:17.100
On talking therapies,

00:38:17.100 --> 00:38:19.560
the 24/25 NHS planning guidance committed

00:38:19.560 --> 00:38:23.460
to increasing the number
of adults and older adults

00:38:23.460 --> 00:38:26.100
completing a course of treatment
with NHS talking therapies

00:38:26.100 --> 00:38:31.100
to 700,000 with at least 67%
achieving reliable improvement

00:38:31.230 --> 00:38:34.560
and 48% achieving reliable recovery.

00:38:34.560 --> 00:38:38.730
In May, 2024, 56,960
adults and older adults

00:38:38.730 --> 00:38:40.500
completed a course of treatment,

00:38:40.500 --> 00:38:43.110
with reliable improvement of 67.4%

00:38:43.110 --> 00:38:47.430
and reliable recovery of 47.9%.

00:38:47.430 --> 00:38:49.710
Access to the individual
placement of support services

00:38:49.710 --> 00:38:52.980
has significantly improved with
over 36,000 people reported

00:38:52.980 --> 00:38:56.280
to have accessed the service
in the 12 months to March '24

00:38:56.280 --> 00:38:59.583
compared to only 26,000
in the year to March '23.

00:39:00.450 --> 00:39:04.560
The dementia diagnosis rate
is 65% as of June, 2024

00:39:04.560 --> 00:39:07.170
against the national ambition of 66.7%.

00:39:07.170 --> 00:39:09.843
So, steady improvements since January '23.

00:39:10.830 --> 00:39:13.500
Access to specialist community
perinatal mental health

00:39:13.500 --> 00:39:15.900
and maternal mental health
services continues to increase,

00:39:15.900 --> 00:39:18.540
although we are still short of the target.

00:39:18.540 --> 00:39:20.352
In the 12 months to April '24,

00:39:20.352 --> 00:39:24.540
58,898 women accessed these services.

00:39:24.540 --> 00:39:25.380
And finally,

00:39:25.380 --> 00:39:28.110
around 1 in 10 people are
waiting for a second contact

00:39:28.110 --> 00:39:29.760
from adult community
mental health services

00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:32.340
have been waiting at least 116 weeks

00:39:32.340 --> 00:39:34.290
as of April, 2024,

00:39:34.290 --> 00:39:38.100
which is clearly an unacceptable
quantity of long waits

00:39:38.100 --> 00:39:40.500
and which have a negative
impact on care for people

00:39:40.500 --> 00:39:41.940
with serious mental illness

00:39:41.940 --> 00:39:43.980
and those seeking support for self-care

00:39:43.980 --> 00:39:46.530
and neurodevelopmental conditions.

00:39:46.530 --> 00:39:48.660
So we are looking with some urgency

00:39:48.660 --> 00:39:51.568
on the management of longer wait lists

00:39:51.568 --> 00:39:54.570
and looking at how we create the same

00:39:54.570 --> 00:39:56.100
or similar standards in mental health

00:39:56.100 --> 00:39:58.470
as we have currently in elective.

00:39:58.470 --> 00:40:02.160
So I hope that that long list
of statistics gives the sense

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:04.350
that I said at the beginning where

00:40:04.350 --> 00:40:06.030
it's incredibly important to recognise

00:40:06.030 --> 00:40:08.817
that operational delivery is
an incredibly complex system

00:40:08.817 --> 00:40:11.310
and a very complex set of patients.

00:40:11.310 --> 00:40:12.420
And so every day,

00:40:12.420 --> 00:40:15.330
there are extraordinary
positive improvements being made

00:40:15.330 --> 00:40:17.070
at the same time as there are still

00:40:17.070 --> 00:40:19.620
incredibly challenging targets
that we have yet to meet,

00:40:19.620 --> 00:40:21.927
but which we absolutely
think we should be meeting.

00:40:21.927 --> 00:40:23.430
And the progress towards those

00:40:23.430 --> 00:40:25.800
needs to be carefully tracked and managed.

00:40:25.800 --> 00:40:26.670
- Thank you.

00:40:26.670 --> 00:40:29.220
So, on operations and current performance,

00:40:29.220 --> 00:40:31.470
any comments or questions?

00:40:31.470 --> 00:40:32.400
Mark?

00:40:32.400 --> 00:40:34.680
- Well, it is great to have the numbers.

00:40:34.680 --> 00:40:37.770
I mean the report notes the
pressure on GP services,

00:40:37.770 --> 00:40:39.120
which everyone knows about,

00:40:40.170 --> 00:40:42.570
but it also notes the
launch of Pharmacy First,

00:40:42.570 --> 00:40:44.430
with over 90% of pharmacists signed up,

00:40:44.430 --> 00:40:47.070
which is very good news.

00:40:47.070 --> 00:40:48.780
I guess there are sort
of two questions there.

00:40:48.780 --> 00:40:53.780
One is, are the missing
10% in areas where,

00:40:54.510 --> 00:40:55.890
I suppose it's how they mapped

00:40:55.890 --> 00:40:58.143
the areas of unmet need actually,

00:40:59.460 --> 00:41:01.680
and it would be a pity if the
10% that weren't signed up

00:41:01.680 --> 00:41:03.300
were mainly in the areas of unmet need,

00:41:03.300 --> 00:41:05.640
which seems an unlikely outcome.

00:41:05.640 --> 00:41:08.040
And I guess the sort
of related question is,

00:41:08.040 --> 00:41:11.580
do we know whether the people
that take up Pharmacy First

00:41:11.580 --> 00:41:13.440
are people who would otherwise

00:41:13.440 --> 00:41:15.180
have used general practise services?

00:41:15.180 --> 00:41:18.030
In other words, we ought
to see a slight reduction

00:41:18.030 --> 00:41:20.703
in pressure going with people
using this new service.

00:41:22.890 --> 00:41:24.534
- [Meddings] Should we try and gather

00:41:24.534 --> 00:41:27.980
two or three questions?
- Sure, I'll make a note.

00:41:27.980 --> 00:41:28.813
- Andrew, do you have a-

00:41:28.813 --> 00:41:30.893
- [Morris] My name is
Andrew Morris, Deputy Chair.

00:41:32.237 --> 00:41:33.486
- [Meddings] Sorry. You are, Mark?

00:41:33.486 --> 00:41:36.351
- [Walport] Sorry, Mark
Walport, Non-Executive Director.

00:41:36.351 --> 00:41:39.660
- I've just got a couple of
points around the winter plan.

00:41:39.660 --> 00:41:44.280
So, recognising that activity's
probably going to increase

00:41:44.280 --> 00:41:46.620
by about 4%.

00:41:46.620 --> 00:41:51.110
We've got the stretch
of trying to achieve 78%

00:41:51.110 --> 00:41:53.460
of people going through
the emergency department

00:41:53.460 --> 00:41:55.320
within four hours.

00:41:55.320 --> 00:41:58.200
And unlike last year
and the previous year,

00:41:58.200 --> 00:42:01.080
we've made some attempts
to increase capacity

00:42:01.080 --> 00:42:05.130
across the system and this
year. for financial reasons,

00:42:05.130 --> 00:42:08.940
predominantly, we haven't
got that flexibility.

00:42:08.940 --> 00:42:12.960
And whilst Emily and Sarah-Jane,

00:42:12.960 --> 00:42:14.490
you've done some really fantastic work

00:42:14.490 --> 00:42:18.930
around improving flow on the
ICB level and trust level,

00:42:18.930 --> 00:42:21.150
everyone's focused on doing that.

00:42:21.150 --> 00:42:24.990
I still feel this feels
really, really tight

00:42:24.990 --> 00:42:26.170
for this coming winter

00:42:27.390 --> 00:42:30.690
and we do want to improve
on an annual basis,

00:42:30.690 --> 00:42:32.553
but it is really, really tight.

00:42:33.780 --> 00:42:36.780
And if I go back a couple of years,

00:42:36.780 --> 00:42:40.173
I can recall the chancellor,
or the then chancellor,

00:42:41.250 --> 00:42:46.250
earmarked about 67 billion
to improve social care.

00:42:46.350 --> 00:42:47.460
And I'm just thinking,

00:42:47.460 --> 00:42:51.030
have we seen any benefit
from that investment

00:42:51.030 --> 00:42:54.060
or is that investment
just focused on preserving

00:42:54.060 --> 00:42:56.943
the status quo in terms of
buying the existing capacity?

00:42:57.900 --> 00:42:59.700
- [Meddings] So, can you take one more,

00:42:59.700 --> 00:43:01.110
and then have a go at some of those?

00:43:01.110 --> 00:43:02.736
So Jane, you were-

00:43:02.736 --> 00:43:03.720
- Thank you.

00:43:03.720 --> 00:43:05.400
Jane Ellison, Non-Executive Director.

00:43:05.400 --> 00:43:06.800
So just two points.

00:43:06.800 --> 00:43:08.400
One, I mean you know,

00:43:08.400 --> 00:43:11.340
great reduction in the long
waiters, very long waits,

00:43:11.340 --> 00:43:13.740
but I just wondered if
you could just unpack

00:43:13.740 --> 00:43:15.390
for us a little bit what you found

00:43:15.390 --> 00:43:18.120
in terms of the complexity
of the remaining people

00:43:18.120 --> 00:43:19.290
on that list.

00:43:19.290 --> 00:43:21.960
And then the second
point is actually more,

00:43:21.960 --> 00:43:25.563
it's in the area of
screening and vaccination,

00:43:26.640 --> 00:43:29.760
obviously very much
welcome our commitment,

00:43:29.760 --> 00:43:33.870
NHS England's commitment
to the global elimination

00:43:33.870 --> 00:43:35.940
of cervical cancer which is within reach,

00:43:35.940 --> 00:43:39.060
and obviously HPV vaccine
is critical to that.

00:43:39.060 --> 00:43:40.710
Been a lot of coverage recently

00:43:40.710 --> 00:43:45.480
about the Department of
Education having a real focus

00:43:45.480 --> 00:43:47.460
on children who are out of school

00:43:47.460 --> 00:43:51.060
and I'm imagining there's
quite a strong relationship

00:43:51.060 --> 00:43:52.920
there in terms of children out of school

00:43:52.920 --> 00:43:54.600
and children who were struggling to access

00:43:54.600 --> 00:43:57.090
in terms of some of the
school-based vaccinations.

00:43:57.090 --> 00:44:00.150
So I just wondered if
there's an opportunity there

00:44:00.150 --> 00:44:03.753
sort of for some strategic
joined-up thinking in that area.

00:44:04.710 --> 00:44:06.450
- [Meddings] So, do
you want to take those,

00:44:06.450 --> 00:44:08.587
or even allocate any of
those if you want to your-

00:44:08.587 --> 00:44:11.910
- I was going to see if Steve
wants to come in on HPV,

00:44:11.910 --> 00:44:14.970
but I'll give him a couple of minutes.

00:44:14.970 --> 00:44:15.900
Sorry, I should have introduced.

00:44:15.900 --> 00:44:17.800
Emily Lawson, Chief Operating Officer.

00:44:18.930 --> 00:44:20.250
So just on Pharmacy First,

00:44:20.250 --> 00:44:22.860
so I had absolutely been
asking that question

00:44:22.860 --> 00:44:25.890
about the sign-up as you would expect.

00:44:25.890 --> 00:44:28.333
And there is not a correlation

00:44:28.333 --> 00:44:31.623
with areas of particular
need, it's quite spread out.

00:44:32.970 --> 00:44:35.160
And of course one of the
benefits of Pharmacy First

00:44:35.160 --> 00:44:40.110
is that pharmacy disobeys
the access to care rules

00:44:40.110 --> 00:44:42.690
and tend to be in areas
which are underserved

00:44:42.690 --> 00:44:44.220
by GP's generally.

00:44:44.220 --> 00:44:45.360
So there is a high concentration

00:44:45.360 --> 00:44:46.193
in areas which are underserved.

00:44:46.193 --> 00:44:48.240
So one of the good things
about Pharmacy First

00:44:48.240 --> 00:44:50.160
is it's broadly increased access

00:44:50.160 --> 00:44:52.350
made it more convenient for people.

00:44:52.350 --> 00:44:55.410
In terms of the reduced pressure,

00:44:55.410 --> 00:44:58.170
I have to say I haven't heard
this back from the system

00:44:58.170 --> 00:44:59.700
and at the moment we wouldn't have data

00:44:59.700 --> 00:45:00.780
that would necessarily show that

00:45:00.780 --> 00:45:03.090
we can see all of the
interactions that are going on

00:45:03.090 --> 00:45:06.060
in Pharmacy First. Although,
I have to say at the moment,

00:45:06.060 --> 00:45:08.310
the data's a little bit
lagged for my liking,

00:45:09.180 --> 00:45:10.290
but I wonder,

00:45:10.290 --> 00:45:12.231
and there are plenty of
people in primary care

00:45:12.231 --> 00:45:13.860
around the table who
might want to comment,

00:45:13.860 --> 00:45:17.490
but I wonder if it's a bit
similar to what we've seen in UEC

00:45:17.490 --> 00:45:19.740
and linking to Andrew's question,

00:45:19.740 --> 00:45:21.570
which is the improvements enable us

00:45:21.570 --> 00:45:23.700
to absorb the increased pressure

00:45:23.700 --> 00:45:26.760
and increased need for access year on year

00:45:26.760 --> 00:45:29.550
rather than to actually
create additional capacity.

00:45:29.550 --> 00:45:30.870
I don't think I could,

00:45:30.870 --> 00:45:32.910
I'm 100% sure that the stats won't give me

00:45:32.910 --> 00:45:34.230
the ability to say that right now,

00:45:34.230 --> 00:45:36.300
but that would be, I guess,

00:45:36.300 --> 00:45:37.500
a question for us to look into.

00:45:37.500 --> 00:45:39.390
I don't know if any
colleagues in primary care

00:45:39.390 --> 00:45:41.123
want to come in on that, maybe want to-

00:45:43.336 --> 00:45:44.169
- When you asked the question, Mark,

00:45:44.169 --> 00:45:46.920
I was sort of grinning
to myself that, you know,

00:45:46.920 --> 00:45:50.010
we have so much unmet
need in general practise

00:45:50.010 --> 00:45:52.110
that what's happened is
that you've taken a bit

00:45:52.110 --> 00:45:53.640
of the unmet need and dealt with it

00:45:53.640 --> 00:45:55.380
in a safe and appropriate way.

00:45:55.380 --> 00:45:57.660
But all that's done is, one would hope,

00:45:57.660 --> 00:45:59.820
is allow those with
slightly more complex issues

00:45:59.820 --> 00:46:00.977
to get to the front of the queue

00:46:00.977 --> 00:46:03.930
to get to access what little is there.

00:46:03.930 --> 00:46:08.100
But, Amanda earlier mentioned
industrial action looming,

00:46:08.100 --> 00:46:09.720
and the risk of debating primary care,

00:46:09.720 --> 00:46:11.610
and we're already starting to see tension

00:46:11.610 --> 00:46:14.190
as general practise is pulling back

00:46:14.190 --> 00:46:15.960
from doing all the discretionary stuff.

00:46:15.960 --> 00:46:17.250
And I think that's one
thing we need to watch

00:46:17.250 --> 00:46:18.630
and it'd be interesting
to see other statistics

00:46:18.630 --> 00:46:20.467
evolve over that as well.

00:46:20.467 --> 00:46:21.480
I think it's going to be quite difficult

00:46:21.480 --> 00:46:22.890
to disentangle those statistics

00:46:22.890 --> 00:46:26.970
because it's so nuanced and difficult.

00:46:26.970 --> 00:46:28.560
It's difficult to pull out,
it's difficult to measure, Mark.

00:46:28.560 --> 00:46:31.980
So thank you. Thanks, Emily.

00:46:31.980 --> 00:46:33.780
- I think the other
thing to be aware of is

00:46:33.780 --> 00:46:36.150
that we are in year two of PCIP,

00:46:36.150 --> 00:46:38.638
the primary care improvement plan,

00:46:38.638 --> 00:46:41.220
but year one was a lot about hitting

00:46:41.220 --> 00:46:43.410
sort of foundational
implementation of things

00:46:43.410 --> 00:46:45.120
that will then deliver a benefit.

00:46:45.120 --> 00:46:46.440
And this year is about making sure people

00:46:46.440 --> 00:46:47.520
can actually get benefit,

00:46:47.520 --> 00:46:49.350
for example from digital telephony.

00:46:49.350 --> 00:46:51.480
Digital telephony doesn't
actually improve anything

00:46:51.480 --> 00:46:52.680
until you've got the triage

00:46:52.680 --> 00:46:54.420
and the people in the
surgery at the right time

00:46:54.420 --> 00:46:56.040
to treat patients appropriately.

00:46:56.040 --> 00:46:58.080
So that is what we need to focus on

00:46:58.080 --> 00:47:01.030
and what the primary care team
is focused on at the moment.

00:47:02.340 --> 00:47:04.623
Andrew, in terms of your
points about winter,

00:47:06.090 --> 00:47:09.270
they are obviously well
made and and well taken.

00:47:09.270 --> 00:47:11.700
And this is what, you
know, the team as you say,

00:47:11.700 --> 00:47:14.430
which is not just the team in NHS England,

00:47:14.430 --> 00:47:16.230
but teams across the country

00:47:16.230 --> 00:47:17.520
who've put in place improvements.

00:47:17.520 --> 00:47:18.870
So, over the last 18 months,

00:47:18.870 --> 00:47:20.640
but before that as well, obviously,

00:47:20.640 --> 00:47:21.790
in response to the UEC,

00:47:22.890 --> 00:47:25.233
the plan that was
published in February '23.

00:47:27.060 --> 00:47:29.010
We have made substantive improvements.

00:47:29.010 --> 00:47:31.410
Last winter was the first winter for,

00:47:31.410 --> 00:47:32.243
I can't remember how long,

00:47:32.243 --> 00:47:34.050
but somebody will remind me,

00:47:34.050 --> 00:47:36.810
where we actually improved
winter performance.

00:47:36.810 --> 00:47:37.830
Although we should also be aware

00:47:37.830 --> 00:47:40.440
we didn't have a deeply
problematic flu spike, right?

00:47:40.440 --> 00:47:44.160
So I don't want to put too much emphasis

00:47:44.160 --> 00:47:45.420
on the operational improvements.

00:47:45.420 --> 00:47:47.700
They clearly enabled the system to be

00:47:47.700 --> 00:47:49.953
more resilient last
winter than previously.

00:47:51.237 --> 00:47:53.190
And we've got a long way to go,

00:47:53.190 --> 00:47:55.620
and there are additional
beds put in last year

00:47:55.620 --> 00:47:57.570
that we've asked systems to
maintain for this winter,

00:47:57.570 --> 00:47:59.250
but there isn't additional money

00:47:59.250 --> 00:48:01.173
for yet more beds this winter.

00:48:02.640 --> 00:48:04.860
So we absolutely have to,

00:48:04.860 --> 00:48:07.770
we collectively as the
NHS, not just NHS England,

00:48:07.770 --> 00:48:10.020
work to put the productivity improvements,

00:48:10.020 --> 00:48:11.850
the flow improvements in place,

00:48:11.850 --> 00:48:13.160
or there won't be the capacity

00:48:13.160 --> 00:48:14.520
in the right place at the right time

00:48:14.520 --> 00:48:17.100
to be resilient in the
same way this winter.

00:48:17.100 --> 00:48:19.710
Which is why we're writing out now,

00:48:19.710 --> 00:48:21.090
not with a whole bunch of new stuff,

00:48:21.090 --> 00:48:23.610
because we took the time
to work with systems

00:48:23.610 --> 00:48:24.960
to write the plan in the spring

00:48:24.960 --> 00:48:26.673
and update the plan in the spring,

00:48:27.630 --> 00:48:30.120
but to just make sure
systems have got the support

00:48:30.120 --> 00:48:31.350
and the lead time

00:48:31.350 --> 00:48:34.020
to put in place these
improvements pre-winter

00:48:34.020 --> 00:48:35.520
and to identify which are the ones

00:48:35.520 --> 00:48:37.920
that will make the biggest
difference in my system.

00:48:37.920 --> 00:48:39.780
Because everybody is doing something

00:48:39.780 --> 00:48:42.450
but nobody is doing absolutely everything

00:48:42.450 --> 00:48:45.480
to the sort of maximum
point of implementation.

00:48:45.480 --> 00:48:47.580
So that's what we want to focus
on over the next few months.

00:48:47.580 --> 00:48:48.413
- Sorry, Emily, may I interrupt,

00:48:48.413 --> 00:48:50.430
because we had this conversation again

00:48:50.430 --> 00:48:53.160
briefly this morning in the board.

00:48:53.160 --> 00:48:55.290
And I have this sort
of rather tedious habit

00:48:55.290 --> 00:48:57.510
of reciting a number of
facts that I sort of learn.

00:48:57.510 --> 00:48:59.490
- Please do.
- And they were,

00:48:59.490 --> 00:49:03.420
what I call operational or
managerial interventions

00:49:03.420 --> 00:49:05.790
by you, Amanda and you,

00:49:05.790 --> 00:49:08.790
and the exec team down through the ICBs

00:49:08.790 --> 00:49:11.340
and into the trusts and so on.

00:49:11.340 --> 00:49:13.860
And engage with that
within local authorities.

00:49:13.860 --> 00:49:16.260
Whether it was 5,000 additional core beds,

00:49:16.260 --> 00:49:18.390
I think you've said that we would hope

00:49:18.390 --> 00:49:20.010
to lock in the same number

00:49:20.010 --> 00:49:21.570
but we haven't got funding

00:49:21.570 --> 00:49:25.830
for another step into a
greater core bed capacity.

00:49:25.830 --> 00:49:29.640
We did 12,700, I think,
square bracket the number,

00:49:29.640 --> 00:49:31.620
but virtual ward beds,

00:49:31.620 --> 00:49:34.410
which is after I think about
18 months of investment,

00:49:34.410 --> 00:49:35.940
we've grown virtual ward capacity

00:49:35.940 --> 00:49:38.517
which allows for step
up and step down care.

00:49:38.517 --> 00:49:41.340
And again, was really
powerful and very early.

00:49:41.340 --> 00:49:45.060
We'll hear about vaccinations
later on in this board.

00:49:45.060 --> 00:49:48.000
But there was a very proactive
vaccination programme

00:49:48.000 --> 00:49:51.150
for flu and for COVID prosecuted very,

00:49:51.150 --> 00:49:53.010
as I say, proactively.

00:49:53.010 --> 00:49:56.310
There was that 40 million
additional funding targeted

00:49:56.310 --> 00:49:59.910
into particular local
authorities where the prior year

00:49:59.910 --> 00:50:01.680
they faced particular challenges.

00:50:01.680 --> 00:50:05.610
And so they got additional
support to allow them

00:50:05.610 --> 00:50:07.980
to provide better
capacity in the community

00:50:07.980 --> 00:50:10.560
to enable the flow and the discharge.

00:50:10.560 --> 00:50:12.510
And there were a series of these steps.

00:50:12.510 --> 00:50:14.480
And the message I'm getting
back though, I think,

00:50:14.480 --> 00:50:18.300
is that this year we are
hoping to lock those in

00:50:18.300 --> 00:50:21.660
but actually we haven't stepped up again.

00:50:21.660 --> 00:50:25.110
We are having to use pretty
much broadly that same capacity.

00:50:25.110 --> 00:50:25.943
Is that right?

00:50:25.943 --> 00:50:28.550
- I think that's right, but
I wouldn't underestimate...

00:50:30.150 --> 00:50:32.190
Look, I'm really balancing
between wanting to say,

00:50:32.190 --> 00:50:33.720
look, I think it will
be hugely challenging

00:50:33.720 --> 00:50:35.730
and I don't want to be
overly optimistic with,

00:50:35.730 --> 00:50:37.200
I still think there is massive amounts

00:50:37.200 --> 00:50:39.030
of opportunity in the
system to do better, right?

00:50:39.030 --> 00:50:40.410
I think both these can
be true at the same time.

00:50:40.410 --> 00:50:41.970
So virtual wards for example-

00:50:41.970 --> 00:50:42.930
- [Meddings] We've got
to look at occupancy.

00:50:42.930 --> 00:50:47.760
- Most systems were not
at 75, 80% occupancy.

00:50:47.760 --> 00:50:49.747
Everybody has now got that
in place, that's improved.

00:50:53.460 --> 00:50:55.800
- I don't know who that was but...

00:50:55.800 --> 00:50:57.120
- [Lawson] But it was striking.

00:50:57.120 --> 00:50:58.489
- No, it was the microphone,

00:50:58.489 --> 00:51:00.990
whatever, the loudspeaker.

00:51:00.990 --> 00:51:04.518
- So we, a bit like I said about PCIP,

00:51:04.518 --> 00:51:08.640
but the first year...

00:51:08.640 --> 00:51:10.770
I'm pretty sure it's not me.

00:51:10.770 --> 00:51:13.284
Because I'm really staying
very still right now.

00:51:13.284 --> 00:51:15.219
- I think we all are.

00:51:15.219 --> 00:51:16.950
- The first year of the
primary care improvement plan

00:51:16.950 --> 00:51:18.900
was about putting
foundational aspects in place

00:51:18.900 --> 00:51:21.240
and this year is about ensuring
everybody has their capacity

00:51:21.240 --> 00:51:23.013
to really do something with them.

00:51:23.850 --> 00:51:25.230
The UEC plan a bit less so.

00:51:25.230 --> 00:51:28.200
People both put in place and
delivered against improvements

00:51:28.200 --> 00:51:29.033
last year.

00:51:29.033 --> 00:51:31.560
But as I said, there is no
system that is maximising

00:51:31.560 --> 00:51:33.870
all of the interventions
that have been laid out,

00:51:33.870 --> 00:51:36.630
and for some of them it may
not be the right intervention.

00:51:36.630 --> 00:51:38.640
But we laid out in the year two letter,

00:51:38.640 --> 00:51:40.650
and we will be clear in the winter letter

00:51:40.650 --> 00:51:42.210
we're sending out shortly to say, look,

00:51:42.210 --> 00:51:43.590
you really do need to have to have

00:51:43.590 --> 00:51:44.580
the following things in place

00:51:44.580 --> 00:51:46.710
because they work, you know?

00:51:46.710 --> 00:51:48.927
There is not a sort of not
invented here thing going on.

00:51:48.927 --> 00:51:50.010
- And did they look at community beds?

00:51:50.010 --> 00:51:52.830
Sorry, I was probably rather
disturbed by the sudden squawk

00:51:52.830 --> 00:51:54.600
that came from...

00:51:54.600 --> 00:51:56.363
Is that looking at the community beds?

00:51:56.363 --> 00:51:58.290
- We have an audit now of community beds,

00:51:58.290 --> 00:52:00.283
which we did for the
first time last winter.

00:52:00.283 --> 00:52:02.610
- Very good.
- We have less ability

00:52:02.610 --> 00:52:03.450
to control community beds

00:52:03.450 --> 00:52:06.690
because of course only half
of them are NHS funded,

00:52:06.690 --> 00:52:07.743
directly NHS funded.

00:52:08.940 --> 00:52:11.430
But the flow work is focused on acutes

00:52:11.430 --> 00:52:13.139
but also obviously affects community.

00:52:13.139 --> 00:52:15.120
- As far as we can.
- As far as we can.

00:52:15.120 --> 00:52:17.340
So there are lots of
opportunities to go further

00:52:17.340 --> 00:52:20.283
and it will be really
tight at the same time.

00:52:21.960 --> 00:52:25.560
And then on complexity, to your point,

00:52:25.560 --> 00:52:28.200
the last published data
we have on complexity,

00:52:28.200 --> 00:52:30.810
because it isn't something
that's routinely analysed,

00:52:30.810 --> 00:52:33.960
the long wait data is from August '22.

00:52:33.960 --> 00:52:35.580
And I think it's likely
to be different now

00:52:35.580 --> 00:52:38.700
because of the focus on reducing
long waits at this point.

00:52:38.700 --> 00:52:42.630
And at the point at which
we looked at 104-week waits,

00:52:42.630 --> 00:52:46.710
at that time, about 37% were what we call

00:52:46.710 --> 00:52:48.360
technically complex.

00:52:48.360 --> 00:52:50.430
Which means the case is not suitable to be

00:52:50.430 --> 00:52:52.050
moved to another provider, for example,

00:52:52.050 --> 00:52:56.250
because of the need for
combination of care at that site

00:52:56.250 --> 00:52:57.850
or by that particular clinician.

00:52:58.900 --> 00:53:01.170
I think given the focus on long waits,

00:53:01.170 --> 00:53:04.503
I wouldn't expect that
to have got much better.

00:53:05.490 --> 00:53:06.690
And certainly when I look at

00:53:06.690 --> 00:53:08.820
individual challenged trusts now,

00:53:08.820 --> 00:53:11.010
because we look at the ones
which are finding it hardest

00:53:11.010 --> 00:53:12.900
to reduce 65-week waits, for example,

00:53:12.900 --> 00:53:15.090
there are particular specialties,

00:53:15.090 --> 00:53:17.490
for example, complex gynaecological care,

00:53:17.490 --> 00:53:20.340
which do come up routinely
in individual trusts

00:53:20.340 --> 00:53:22.740
where it is the combination
of different clinicians

00:53:22.740 --> 00:53:24.600
all needing to be
present at the same time,

00:53:24.600 --> 00:53:26.910
which can be very hard
to schedule for patients.

00:53:26.910 --> 00:53:29.133
But I can't give you a statistic on it.

00:53:30.210 --> 00:53:31.260
Should we ask Steve about HPV?

00:53:31.260 --> 00:53:33.960
- You want to pass that question?

00:53:33.960 --> 00:53:35.789
- I'm scared to speak now
in case it comes back.

00:53:36.960 --> 00:53:39.480
So, hello, my name's Steve,
Chief Delivery Officer

00:53:39.480 --> 00:53:40.890
and National Director of
Vaccination Screening.

00:53:40.890 --> 00:53:43.470
So Jane, just to your point,

00:53:43.470 --> 00:53:46.530
first thing, just to say is,

00:53:46.530 --> 00:53:49.740
Amanda announced our ambition
around nine months ago

00:53:49.740 --> 00:53:51.210
to eliminate cervical cancer,

00:53:51.210 --> 00:53:53.733
which is absolutely as
you say, within reach.

00:53:54.990 --> 00:53:57.000
So, and colleagues will know this,

00:53:57.000 --> 00:53:58.080
but it's worth repeating

00:53:58.080 --> 00:54:03.080
that 99.8% of cases are preventable.

00:54:03.210 --> 00:54:07.620
There's about just over
3000 new cases each year

00:54:07.620 --> 00:54:08.550
across the UK.

00:54:08.550 --> 00:54:10.770
So, it is worth going after.

00:54:10.770 --> 00:54:14.550
And as you say, we are not that far away.

00:54:14.550 --> 00:54:19.550
We need to get to 90%
vaccination rate for HPV,

00:54:19.980 --> 00:54:23.730
we need to get to 70% of screening

00:54:23.730 --> 00:54:25.890
and 90% treatment.

00:54:25.890 --> 00:54:27.690
We are at 90% treatment

00:54:27.690 --> 00:54:30.390
and the other two are
within touching distance.

00:54:30.390 --> 00:54:33.190
But of course that means
they're the hard yards as well.

00:54:34.230 --> 00:54:35.910
So over the last year,

00:54:35.910 --> 00:54:38.280
we've done quite a number
of tactical things to try

00:54:38.280 --> 00:54:40.320
and improve vaccination
rates in particular

00:54:40.320 --> 00:54:42.123
with a number of catch-up campaigns.

00:54:42.960 --> 00:54:45.240
And to try and answer
your specific question,

00:54:45.240 --> 00:54:46.980
we were assisted in that in some respects

00:54:46.980 --> 00:54:49.143
by a change in the vaccine.

00:54:50.190 --> 00:54:53.013
It previously required two
doses, it now only requires one.

00:54:53.013 --> 00:54:54.960
That is good because it is more likely

00:54:54.960 --> 00:54:56.670
that people will become fully protected

00:54:56.670 --> 00:54:58.593
if it's a one dose rather than two.

00:54:59.460 --> 00:55:01.290
And the primary delivery is

00:55:01.290 --> 00:55:02.910
through school age immunisation services.

00:55:02.910 --> 00:55:05.160
That gave us some capacity.

00:55:05.160 --> 00:55:06.510
So we used that capacity,

00:55:06.510 --> 00:55:08.370
we left the funding in, quite rightly,

00:55:08.370 --> 00:55:11.310
and we used that capacity to run catch-up

00:55:11.310 --> 00:55:12.960
for people who hadn't been vaccinated

00:55:12.960 --> 00:55:16.080
and also to pilot some
out of school offers.

00:55:16.080 --> 00:55:18.420
Now, they were attached
to school services.

00:55:18.420 --> 00:55:20.580
So I think your point of,

00:55:20.580 --> 00:55:23.130
could we go further for
people who are not in school

00:55:23.130 --> 00:55:24.330
is absolutely right,

00:55:24.330 --> 00:55:27.720
and something that we will go
away and have a think about.

00:55:27.720 --> 00:55:29.310
We're hoping come the autumn,

00:55:29.310 --> 00:55:30.870
which will be the year anniversary,

00:55:30.870 --> 00:55:33.840
to share some really
interesting opportunities

00:55:33.840 --> 00:55:35.730
both in screening and vaccination

00:55:35.730 --> 00:55:38.190
that might get us closer to that ambition

00:55:38.190 --> 00:55:40.511
to eliminate cervical cancer.

00:55:40.511 --> 00:55:41.344
- Thank you very much.
- Thank you.

00:55:42.320 --> 00:55:43.350
Amanda, do you want to come in?

00:55:43.350 --> 00:55:45.630
- Yeah, so if I could just jump us back

00:55:45.630 --> 00:55:47.250
a second to the conversation
we were just having

00:55:47.250 --> 00:55:48.890
about winter, two things, Emily,

00:55:48.890 --> 00:55:52.020
as I listened to your
extremely clear report.

00:55:52.020 --> 00:55:54.900
One is, it strikes me as
you're talking about it,

00:55:54.900 --> 00:55:58.170
this is not going to
feel any easier for staff

00:55:58.170 --> 00:56:00.000
over the course of this winter

00:56:00.000 --> 00:56:03.333
because what we are describing
is a whole load of things

00:56:03.333 --> 00:56:05.370
that are going to be more mature

00:56:05.370 --> 00:56:08.250
and hopefully better
embedded in ways of working,

00:56:08.250 --> 00:56:09.810
but actually against a backdrop

00:56:09.810 --> 00:56:12.630
of continuing rising patient need

00:56:12.630 --> 00:56:14.370
and demand as a consequence.

00:56:14.370 --> 00:56:18.270
So, my question is whether or
not we have given any thought

00:56:18.270 --> 00:56:21.960
to what we could be
doing to support staff,

00:56:21.960 --> 00:56:23.430
particularly over what's going to,

00:56:23.430 --> 00:56:27.904
I think feel like a very,
very busy winter period.

00:56:27.904 --> 00:56:30.300
Whether there's any more
that we could do on that.

00:56:30.300 --> 00:56:31.770
Secondly, I think, again,

00:56:31.770 --> 00:56:34.050
I know you've thought
about this a lot already,

00:56:34.050 --> 00:56:35.820
but given that what we are trying to do

00:56:35.820 --> 00:56:39.907
is set really clear, sort of
evidence-based interventions

00:56:41.070 --> 00:56:42.960
that we're now asking, as you say,

00:56:42.960 --> 00:56:46.200
the right mix of those
to be applied locally,

00:56:46.200 --> 00:56:47.033
which is right,

00:56:47.033 --> 00:56:50.550
and that's where the
benefit of the assessment

00:56:50.550 --> 00:56:52.530
over the last two years plays in.

00:56:52.530 --> 00:56:55.620
But we're also doing that against

00:56:55.620 --> 00:56:57.720
a backdrop of saying like,

00:56:57.720 --> 00:57:01.260
this is tough and, you
know, hard yards asks.

00:57:01.260 --> 00:57:02.910
Again, second question,

00:57:02.910 --> 00:57:05.880
just your thoughts on how
we then best make sure

00:57:05.880 --> 00:57:08.700
that we are engaging with colleagues.

00:57:08.700 --> 00:57:10.680
I don't just mean within NHS England,

00:57:10.680 --> 00:57:13.637
I mean within ICB's, within trusts,

00:57:13.637 --> 00:57:15.060
within the whole system.

00:57:15.060 --> 00:57:17.130
Because it's an all-in ask, isn't it?

00:57:17.130 --> 00:57:19.530
Voluntary sector colleagues, as well.

00:57:19.530 --> 00:57:21.210
About how we then best make sure

00:57:21.210 --> 00:57:23.910
that we understand where some
of the biggest risks are,

00:57:23.910 --> 00:57:25.650
where the biggest opportunities are,

00:57:25.650 --> 00:57:27.330
and how we might work most effectively

00:57:27.330 --> 00:57:31.020
with colleagues in this next
sort of few weeks, months,

00:57:31.020 --> 00:57:34.170
to translate all of this good
guidance and good evidence

00:57:34.170 --> 00:57:36.780
and good planning into kind of our best

00:57:36.780 --> 00:57:38.973
and most resilient plans for this winter.

00:57:40.170 --> 00:57:42.690
- Thank you. I think
that was three things.

00:57:42.690 --> 00:57:44.246
- Sorry.
- That's all right.

00:57:44.246 --> 00:57:45.546
Just checking.

00:57:47.460 --> 00:57:51.480
So, you talked about
the increase in demand.

00:57:51.480 --> 00:57:53.730
The thing that I haven't
spoken about is obviously

00:57:53.730 --> 00:57:56.027
what we're trying to do is to
make sure people only end up

00:57:56.027 --> 00:57:58.950
in A&amp;E when they really
need to end up in A&amp;E.

00:57:58.950 --> 00:58:00.300
And there was some really,

00:58:00.300 --> 00:58:02.760
there is ongoing good
work in some systems,

00:58:02.760 --> 00:58:04.560
but again, not consistently
across the piece.

00:58:04.560 --> 00:58:08.880
So one of the major
interventions on flow this winter

00:58:08.880 --> 00:58:11.850
is to make sure everybody
is using clear criteria

00:58:11.850 --> 00:58:13.470
to admit pathway standards

00:58:13.470 --> 00:58:15.420
and that everybody who is
admitted has a care plan

00:58:15.420 --> 00:58:18.663
which includes the point to
which we'd expect them to exit.

00:58:19.500 --> 00:58:21.480
Which again, none of
these are rocket science,

00:58:21.480 --> 00:58:23.370
but they are inconsistently applied

00:58:23.370 --> 00:58:25.140
and we have evidence from
systems that are using them

00:58:25.140 --> 00:58:26.730
that they make a big difference.

00:58:26.730 --> 00:58:29.220
Because as we heard in the
private board, again on frailty,

00:58:29.220 --> 00:58:30.870
we have people being
transported to hospital

00:58:30.870 --> 00:58:32.700
where it is definitely not
the right thing for them.

00:58:32.700 --> 00:58:35.430
And that makes no sense.

00:58:35.430 --> 00:58:38.460
So we are trying to
mitigate, Amanda as you say,

00:58:38.460 --> 00:58:41.987
the risk once you get to A&amp;E
by avoiding you being in A&amp;E

00:58:41.987 --> 00:58:43.323
if you don't need to be.

00:58:44.730 --> 00:58:49.730
In terms of how we engage with systems

00:58:49.800 --> 00:58:52.260
to make sure we understand

00:58:52.260 --> 00:58:53.910
or we collectively understand the risks

00:58:53.910 --> 00:58:55.740
and we can do our best on implementation,

00:58:55.740 --> 00:58:57.393
we're doing a few things,

00:58:59.280 --> 00:59:00.900
not all of which are public at the moment,

00:59:00.900 --> 00:59:03.630
so I'm just wondering if
I should talk about them.

00:59:03.630 --> 00:59:05.703
But one of the things
is to build on the work

00:59:05.703 --> 00:59:08.200
that NHS Impact is doing

00:59:09.120 --> 00:59:14.120
to pick up the two or three
critical interventions

00:59:14.310 --> 00:59:17.040
in flow in UEC that we know
make the biggest difference

00:59:17.040 --> 00:59:18.270
when they're done well.

00:59:18.270 --> 00:59:20.820
And to effectively write
the playbook for everyone

00:59:20.820 --> 00:59:22.440
so that if you're not currently doing it,

00:59:22.440 --> 00:59:23.940
you have something to react to.

00:59:23.940 --> 00:59:27.420
Now I'm sure people who
were involved in, you know,

00:59:27.420 --> 00:59:30.240
Model Hospital or the
monetization agency previously,

00:59:30.240 --> 00:59:32.280
which obviously I wasn't,
will have experience of this,

00:59:32.280 --> 00:59:34.980
but the key thing here is to say, look,

00:59:34.980 --> 00:59:37.920
here is what we think good looks like.

00:59:37.920 --> 00:59:39.450
What that precisely looks like,

00:59:39.450 --> 00:59:41.487
given the flow and nature of your patients

00:59:41.487 --> 00:59:44.400
and the physical layout,
may look slightly different,

00:59:44.400 --> 00:59:45.780
but these are the critical components

00:59:45.780 --> 00:59:46.770
that we think you need to have in place.

00:59:46.770 --> 00:59:49.650
And then back that with
training and support for staff

00:59:49.650 --> 00:59:51.900
so that they feel able to both
implement the core of that

00:59:51.900 --> 00:59:54.870
but also to adjust it
accordingly for their systems.

00:59:54.870 --> 00:59:57.270
And we want system leaders to lead that

00:59:57.270 --> 00:59:59.870
as opposed to that coming
directly from NHS England.

01:00:01.080 --> 01:00:01.980
In terms of other support,

01:00:01.980 --> 01:00:04.710
we're obviously continuing with tiering,

01:00:04.710 --> 01:00:07.950
which if I look at the data
has been really effective.

01:00:07.950 --> 01:00:09.090
The systems that are tiered,

01:00:09.090 --> 01:00:12.450
not just in UEC, but also
for elective and cancer,

01:00:12.450 --> 01:00:16.200
improve more than the
sort of nextdoor systems

01:00:16.200 --> 01:00:18.210
in terms of performance.

01:00:18.210 --> 01:00:20.790
But we've adjusted the
tiering approach in UEC

01:00:20.790 --> 01:00:23.850
for this winter based on
what we learned last winter

01:00:23.850 --> 01:00:25.770
about what was helpful and what wasn't,

01:00:25.770 --> 01:00:27.990
which includes being
more directly involved

01:00:27.990 --> 01:00:29.146
sometimes with trusts,

01:00:29.146 --> 01:00:30.720
and particularly with ambulance services,

01:00:30.720 --> 01:00:33.180
rather than staying at
the level of the system.

01:00:33.180 --> 01:00:36.240
So we're trying to adjust
and be agile in that way

01:00:36.240 --> 01:00:38.690
and doing different things
for different systems.

01:00:39.960 --> 01:00:41.280
I do think,

01:00:41.280 --> 01:00:42.480
this is a personal perspective

01:00:42.480 --> 01:00:44.010
rather than the plan perspective,

01:00:44.010 --> 01:00:46.530
is the more we can create

01:00:46.530 --> 01:00:51.180
the sort of capacity sense of ownership

01:00:51.180 --> 01:00:55.710
and confidence in the
toolkit for frontline staff

01:00:55.710 --> 01:00:58.170
to take ownership of improving
things where they are,

01:00:58.170 --> 01:01:01.380
within clear guardrails of
clinical safety, et cetera,

01:01:01.380 --> 01:01:02.730
the more we will see the benefit.

01:01:02.730 --> 01:01:04.410
And I feel like things like virtual wards,

01:01:04.410 --> 01:01:05.460
we already see that.

01:01:05.460 --> 01:01:07.470
There are places where you can see

01:01:07.470 --> 01:01:10.454
when you go into the control centre

01:01:10.454 --> 01:01:12.120
that they've really got it,

01:01:12.120 --> 01:01:13.980
and there is somebody
really championing it,

01:01:13.980 --> 01:01:15.000
making sure it's safe,

01:01:15.000 --> 01:01:16.860
making sure it's maximally
used for patients,

01:01:16.860 --> 01:01:18.900
and places where they've
theoretically done it

01:01:18.900 --> 01:01:20.040
but it's not quite owned.

01:01:20.040 --> 01:01:22.470
Particularly perhaps not clinically owned,

01:01:22.470 --> 01:01:23.670
where everybody's doing it

01:01:23.670 --> 01:01:25.200
but we're not seeing the same benefit.

01:01:25.200 --> 01:01:27.810
So I think there is a lot of
work to do, back to your point,

01:01:27.810 --> 01:01:29.613
about how do we help staff

01:01:29.613 --> 01:01:33.060
sort of handle a difficult winter,

01:01:33.060 --> 01:01:36.000
agency is the single biggest
thing you can give people

01:01:36.000 --> 01:01:41.000
to help them feel engaged
and respected at work.

01:01:42.030 --> 01:01:43.860
Not everybody is going
to have absolute agency

01:01:43.860 --> 01:01:45.630
in every situation. That's
not the world we work in.

01:01:45.630 --> 01:01:47.550
But I think we can do a
bit more to give people

01:01:47.550 --> 01:01:49.320
the tools and the
training and the framework

01:01:49.320 --> 01:01:51.150
within which they can then
improve things locally

01:01:51.150 --> 01:01:52.410
for patients which is what in the end

01:01:52.410 --> 01:01:54.240
everybody does want to do.

01:01:54.240 --> 01:01:56.040
- Very good, thank you. Duncan?

01:01:56.040 --> 01:01:58.053
- Duncan Burton, Chief Nursing Officer.

01:01:58.946 --> 01:01:59.779
I just wanted-

01:02:03.007 --> 01:02:04.860
I guess I just wanted to add to that,

01:02:04.860 --> 01:02:06.463
actually one of I think
the most important things

01:02:06.463 --> 01:02:08.340
as well is in support of staff

01:02:08.340 --> 01:02:11.250
is around when we do
have those circumstances

01:02:11.250 --> 01:02:15.510
of patients being looked
after in corridors, et cetera,

01:02:15.510 --> 01:02:18.510
for minimising the amount
of time that happens,

01:02:18.510 --> 01:02:20.550
but really what more can we do

01:02:20.550 --> 01:02:23.070
to support around things
like privacy and dignity,

01:02:23.070 --> 01:02:26.283
making sure patients have
got access to food and drink,

01:02:27.517 --> 01:02:30.840
and all those really
important critical factors

01:02:30.840 --> 01:02:34.560
that impact their experience
but also their safety as well.

01:02:34.560 --> 01:02:35.393
So I think, you know,

01:02:35.393 --> 01:02:38.280
I will certainly be
working with you, Emily,

01:02:38.280 --> 01:02:40.290
and the team in terms
of what more we can do

01:02:40.290 --> 01:02:43.500
to support in that space and
support our colleagues in that.

01:02:43.500 --> 01:02:44.948
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

01:02:44.948 --> 01:02:47.050
- Slightly conscious
of time at this point,

01:02:47.050 --> 01:02:51.397
can I move us on to the
financial performance update,

01:02:51.397 --> 01:02:52.263
Julian?

01:02:53.100 --> 01:02:54.060
- Sure.

01:02:54.060 --> 01:02:57.753
I mean, building really
on what Emily has said,

01:02:58.934 --> 01:03:03.090
you know, this is clearly a tough year

01:03:03.090 --> 01:03:05.820
and probably tougher than most.

01:03:05.820 --> 01:03:09.360
We are dealing with rising
demand, rising activity,

01:03:09.360 --> 01:03:11.640
seeking to improve performance

01:03:11.640 --> 01:03:13.383
in exactly the way Emily has said.

01:03:14.490 --> 01:03:16.230
But the kind of total amount of funding

01:03:16.230 --> 01:03:20.130
that we have put in is
broadly flat in real terms

01:03:20.130 --> 01:03:22.380
based on the measure the
sort of government used

01:03:22.380 --> 01:03:25.143
for public sector inflation.

01:03:26.520 --> 01:03:31.140
And underlying that you have
costs which are increasing,

01:03:31.140 --> 01:03:33.450
you know, significantly faster than

01:03:33.450 --> 01:03:34.590
that measure of inflation.

01:03:34.590 --> 01:03:38.100
I mean the national living
wage is up 10% this year.

01:03:38.100 --> 01:03:41.970
That impacts on our
continuing healthcare costs.

01:03:41.970 --> 01:03:45.300
We are continuing to prescribe,

01:03:45.300 --> 01:03:48.240
and by the way, this is a
good thing for patients,

01:03:48.240 --> 01:03:51.030
higher volumes of hormone
replacement therapy,

01:03:51.030 --> 01:03:54.480
higher volumes of direct
oral anticoagulants,

01:03:54.480 --> 01:03:57.630
which, you know, mean
sort of a far lower risk

01:03:57.630 --> 01:04:01.500
of heart attacks and such like,

01:04:01.500 --> 01:04:03.510
so those are good things,

01:04:03.510 --> 01:04:07.410
but those things create
real pressures on spend.

01:04:07.410 --> 01:04:10.140
And as a result, if you're a provider,

01:04:10.140 --> 01:04:12.577
you know, sort of
baseline allocations are,

01:04:12.577 --> 01:04:17.577
you know, probably less
than flat in real terms.

01:04:17.850 --> 01:04:22.170
That is after we have injected
about 2 billion pounds

01:04:22.170 --> 01:04:24.240
in two system allocations,

01:04:24.240 --> 01:04:29.160
we've agreed plans with all systems,

01:04:29.160 --> 01:04:31.560
but relative to those
baseline allocations,

01:04:31.560 --> 01:04:34.440
we've had to inject another
2 billion pounds into,

01:04:34.440 --> 01:04:39.440
in particular, 31 systems
to get reasonable plans.

01:04:40.440 --> 01:04:42.450
So, it's tough.

01:04:42.450 --> 01:04:47.450
The level of efficiency that
ICBs and trusts are seeking

01:04:48.390 --> 01:04:50.820
to deliver this year is, you know,

01:04:50.820 --> 01:04:55.260
close on 9 billion pounds, about 5 to 6%,

01:04:55.260 --> 01:05:00.260
that is both sort of cost
out and higher activity.

01:05:01.680 --> 01:05:04.530
So it is really, it is tough,

01:05:04.530 --> 01:05:09.530
it is really tight and, you know,

01:05:09.750 --> 01:05:11.970
providers in particular
are really focused on how

01:05:11.970 --> 01:05:16.473
they continue to improve sort
of baseline productivity.

01:05:17.580 --> 01:05:19.740
But you know, this is
against the backdrop.

01:05:19.740 --> 01:05:22.080
There's, Richard, you said at the start,

01:05:22.080 --> 01:05:26.400
that the NAO have set out of
sort of unprecedented pressures

01:05:26.400 --> 01:05:29.820
due to a combination of poor investment,

01:05:29.820 --> 01:05:32.430
historic investment in the estate,

01:05:32.430 --> 01:05:34.050
inflationary pressures,

01:05:34.050 --> 01:05:36.270
and still recognising there is a cost

01:05:36.270 --> 01:05:38.580
to post-pandemic recovery.

01:05:38.580 --> 01:05:40.380
We still have, you know,

01:05:40.380 --> 01:05:42.900
a couple of thousands of
patients at any given time

01:05:42.900 --> 01:05:45.450
in hospital with COVID.

01:05:45.450 --> 01:05:46.620
In fact, I think Steve's just said

01:05:46.620 --> 01:05:50.828
I've slightly understated
that number, but you know,

01:05:50.828 --> 01:05:52.380
and that is just an ongoing factor

01:05:52.380 --> 01:05:55.140
in what the NHS has to deal with.

01:05:55.140 --> 01:05:57.780
And look, our job remains,
as Emily has said,

01:05:57.780 --> 01:06:00.150
how do we provide the best possible care

01:06:00.150 --> 01:06:02.853
within the resources provided.

01:06:03.720 --> 01:06:08.133
So, that's where we are.

01:06:09.012 --> 01:06:10.473
At the end of month two,

01:06:12.030 --> 01:06:17.030
total system spend is about
300 million pounds off-plan,

01:06:19.290 --> 01:06:20.123
at the level of the NHS

01:06:20.123 --> 01:06:22.167
is about 200 million pounds off-plan.

01:06:22.167 --> 01:06:26.160
So, that clearly flags like a real risk

01:06:26.160 --> 01:06:29.100
as we sit here today.

01:06:29.100 --> 01:06:33.810
And our clear focus with
ICBs and with providers

01:06:33.810 --> 01:06:36.270
is how we get back onto plan

01:06:36.270 --> 01:06:37.733
and how boards in particular,

01:06:37.733 --> 01:06:40.170
this is not just the chief
finance officer's job,

01:06:40.170 --> 01:06:43.590
this is a board's job and
the executives team job,

01:06:43.590 --> 01:06:46.980
is how they deliver the
financial and operational plans

01:06:46.980 --> 01:06:50.100
to which they have committed.

01:06:50.100 --> 01:06:52.560
And clearly in some places
that is not on track

01:06:52.560 --> 01:06:54.963
and we need them to get back on track.

01:06:56.280 --> 01:06:57.600
Given the position,

01:06:57.600 --> 01:06:59.427
we've assessed the risk across

01:06:59.427 --> 01:07:02.760
our 42 systems and their providers,

01:07:02.760 --> 01:07:05.070
we've identified the 10 systems

01:07:05.070 --> 01:07:07.320
where we are most concerned

01:07:07.320 --> 01:07:09.210
about the level of risk they're running.

01:07:09.210 --> 01:07:12.700
We are doing a short, sharp intervention

01:07:15.374 --> 01:07:17.520
where they are bringing in,

01:07:17.520 --> 01:07:19.740
we're asking them to bring
in some additional support

01:07:19.740 --> 01:07:23.040
so we can properly check that the plans

01:07:23.040 --> 01:07:26.400
that they have said
they have are, you know,

01:07:26.400 --> 01:07:27.870
they've got the detail worked out,

01:07:27.870 --> 01:07:30.000
they're on track with delivering them,

01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:32.070
and supporting people
to identify if they need

01:07:32.070 --> 01:07:34.590
to take action to recover
those or do other things.

01:07:34.590 --> 01:07:36.960
We identify that quickly

01:07:36.960 --> 01:07:41.940
so that we can make sure
we get back on track.

01:07:41.940 --> 01:07:44.850
I will say though that you can see

01:07:44.850 --> 01:07:47.433
that people are making progress.

01:07:48.750 --> 01:07:52.890
You know, 70% of provider
cost goes on the workforce.

01:07:52.890 --> 01:07:55.980
In aggregate, that total
workforce cost in real terms

01:07:55.980 --> 01:07:59.610
needs to come down by about
1.5% across the country.

01:07:59.610 --> 01:08:01.920
That is largely being done

01:08:01.920 --> 01:08:04.920
through reductions in
temporary staffing costs.

01:08:04.920 --> 01:08:06.690
So after four years

01:08:06.690 --> 01:08:09.900
of quite rapid growth in
substantive workforce,

01:08:09.900 --> 01:08:11.160
there's a real focus now

01:08:11.160 --> 01:08:14.820
on then reducing the
temporary staffing costs.

01:08:14.820 --> 01:08:15.653
We think, by the way,

01:08:15.653 --> 01:08:16.486
we think that actually genuinely

01:08:16.486 --> 01:08:18.063
has a benefit for productivity.

01:08:18.960 --> 01:08:21.750
You know, it's better for staff

01:08:21.750 --> 01:08:24.960
that there is less temporary staffing.

01:08:24.960 --> 01:08:28.110
Last year that cost went down

01:08:28.110 --> 01:08:30.570
from 3.5 to 3 billion pounds

01:08:30.570 --> 01:08:33.030
and in the first three
months of this year,

01:08:33.030 --> 01:08:35.130
the run rate would say we are on track

01:08:35.130 --> 01:08:39.510
to reduce that to well below
2.5 billion pounds agency

01:08:39.510 --> 01:08:40.680
this year.

01:08:40.680 --> 01:08:44.670
And we are seeing now the bank
costs coming down as well.

01:08:44.670 --> 01:08:48.720
So look, people are serious,
they're taking action,

01:08:48.720 --> 01:08:51.220
but we are going to work with those

01:08:52.530 --> 01:08:55.260
who we think is the greatest
risk of them not hitting plans

01:08:55.260 --> 01:08:59.100
to make sure that we are on track.

01:08:59.100 --> 01:09:00.360
Now clearly one of the things

01:09:00.360 --> 01:09:02.860
that threw us off last
year was industrial action.

01:09:04.320 --> 01:09:06.185
We have had a strike,

01:09:06.185 --> 01:09:10.530
a five day strike at the end
of June, beginning of July.

01:09:10.530 --> 01:09:13.110
That will impact costs

01:09:13.110 --> 01:09:15.900
and we will have to
discuss with the government

01:09:15.900 --> 01:09:19.083
and with the rest of the
NHS how we deal with those.

01:09:20.220 --> 01:09:22.710
And clearly other further strikes

01:09:22.710 --> 01:09:24.390
will not only hit operational performance,

01:09:24.390 --> 01:09:26.550
it will hit the cost base.

01:09:26.550 --> 01:09:31.440
Look, we are hopeful that
between the government

01:09:31.440 --> 01:09:34.030
and the unions we can see a resolution

01:09:36.660 --> 01:09:39.120
to any future risk of industrial action.

01:09:39.120 --> 01:09:42.000
But that is just one we
will need to keep an eye on.

01:09:42.000 --> 01:09:45.090
So, things are really tight.

01:09:45.090 --> 01:09:48.600
We are not entirely on
track as we sit here today.

01:09:48.600 --> 01:09:51.330
Our focus is working with
those systems and providers

01:09:51.330 --> 01:09:54.750
where we think we have the
biggest risk to turn it around.

01:09:54.750 --> 01:09:58.290
But you can see people
are really focused on it.

01:09:58.290 --> 01:09:59.269
- Thank you.

01:09:59.269 --> 01:10:02.183
Helen, I know you've got
a comment or question,

01:10:02.183 --> 01:10:03.016
and Robert.

01:10:03.016 --> 01:10:03.849
So, Helen?

01:10:03.849 --> 01:10:05.670
- Sorry, forgive me, I didn't
introduce myself last time.

01:10:05.670 --> 01:10:07.890
So Helen Stokes-Lampard,
Non-Executive Director,

01:10:07.890 --> 01:10:10.260
and pertinent to this next point,

01:10:10.260 --> 01:10:13.713
I happen to be an NHS
GP as well, a partner.

01:10:15.060 --> 01:10:17.940
Look Julian, super clear
as ever and really helpful.

01:10:17.940 --> 01:10:20.490
Not positive, not exciting and positive,

01:10:20.490 --> 01:10:22.530
but very important and clear.

01:10:22.530 --> 01:10:26.610
You picked it right at the
end about industrial action.

01:10:26.610 --> 01:10:29.250
Just to be clear in my
mind, perhaps for others,

01:10:29.250 --> 01:10:32.580
that what we are looking
at here does not include

01:10:32.580 --> 01:10:35.100
any implication of any
additional pay award,

01:10:35.100 --> 01:10:37.860
any settlement to any industrial action.

01:10:37.860 --> 01:10:39.210
Those things are not included

01:10:39.210 --> 01:10:40.380
in what we're discussing there?

01:10:40.380 --> 01:10:43.950
- No, those things are not included.

01:10:43.950 --> 01:10:46.110
I mean we have, you know,

01:10:46.110 --> 01:10:51.110
we basically have funding to
provide a 2% increase in pay.

01:10:52.470 --> 01:10:54.330
Clearly we are waiting to see both

01:10:54.330 --> 01:10:56.700
what the recommendations are
of the pay review bodies,

01:10:56.700 --> 01:10:58.920
the government's response
to those recommendations

01:10:58.920 --> 01:11:01.710
and whatever other, you know,

01:11:01.710 --> 01:11:06.420
the discussions will
go on with the unions.

01:11:06.420 --> 01:11:09.210
But that's not included in here.

01:11:09.210 --> 01:11:10.590
- So thank you, because you did reference

01:11:10.590 --> 01:11:12.210
the very significant and very important

01:11:12.210 --> 01:11:14.430
and welcoming increase in
the national living wage,

01:11:14.430 --> 01:11:16.260
which of course for those affected

01:11:16.260 --> 01:11:18.450
is the best part of 10% compared

01:11:18.450 --> 01:11:19.931
to a baseline increase of 2%.

01:11:19.931 --> 01:11:22.350
It's a difficult sort of circle to square.

01:11:22.350 --> 01:11:23.750
So thanks very much, Julian.

01:11:25.410 --> 01:11:26.243
- Robert?

01:11:28.440 --> 01:11:30.490
- Robert Lechler, Non-Executive Director.

01:11:31.650 --> 01:11:33.570
Thank you for that
Julian, it is very tight,

01:11:33.570 --> 01:11:37.023
and you do an amazing job
walking this tightrope.

01:11:38.670 --> 01:11:41.160
A win towards the end
of the last government

01:11:41.160 --> 01:11:46.160
was the commitment to invest
over 3 billion in technology,

01:11:46.620 --> 01:11:50.640
which I think most of us
would say it's not a luxury,

01:11:50.640 --> 01:11:52.500
it's key to delivering the productivity

01:11:52.500 --> 01:11:53.883
that you are so focused on.

01:11:55.140 --> 01:12:00.140
How confident are you
that that won't get raided

01:12:00.330 --> 01:12:03.393
in trying to balance the books?

01:12:05.730 --> 01:12:10.730
- I don't want to prejudge
any decisions this government

01:12:11.010 --> 01:12:13.050
will need to make as it looks at,

01:12:13.050 --> 01:12:15.930
you know, sort of total financial picture.

01:12:15.930 --> 01:12:18.270
I think it's clear from the
conversations we have had

01:12:18.270 --> 01:12:20.940
with the new Secretary of State

01:12:20.940 --> 01:12:22.710
and the conversations we at least have

01:12:22.710 --> 01:12:24.543
with treasury officials,

01:12:26.040 --> 01:12:31.040
that they both want us
to materially improve

01:12:31.320 --> 01:12:33.960
sort of rate of productivity
improvement in the NHS.

01:12:33.960 --> 01:12:35.820
Indeed, they all understand
it's pretty important

01:12:35.820 --> 01:12:39.000
when you think about total
economic productivity

01:12:39.000 --> 01:12:41.160
given the size of the NHS.

01:12:41.160 --> 01:12:44.610
And you know, certainly as we
talk with treasury officials,

01:12:44.610 --> 01:12:46.020
I think they absolutely recognise

01:12:46.020 --> 01:12:47.760
that if we're going to do that,

01:12:47.760 --> 01:12:50.373
you have got to invest,

01:12:51.406 --> 01:12:55.110
and basically technology-enabled
change and innovation

01:12:55.110 --> 01:12:57.420
is going to be at the
heart of what we have to do

01:12:57.420 --> 01:12:59.880
to make the NHS both economically,

01:12:59.880 --> 01:13:03.840
financially sustainable and continue well,

01:13:03.840 --> 01:13:08.490
you know, to deliver and
improve great care for patients.

01:13:08.490 --> 01:13:12.723
So, we will continue to work with them.

01:13:15.180 --> 01:13:16.865
- Mary?
- Thanks very much.

01:13:16.865 --> 01:13:19.547
Mary Watkins, Non-Executive Director.

01:13:19.547 --> 01:13:23.520
One of the issues around
further investment in pay

01:13:23.520 --> 01:13:28.470
would be that we would
probably retain more staff

01:13:28.470 --> 01:13:32.880
and also improve continuity of care,

01:13:32.880 --> 01:13:37.170
which in turn would get
people back to work.

01:13:37.170 --> 01:13:39.780
And so therefore, I just
wanted to kind of emphasise

01:13:39.780 --> 01:13:42.870
that what you're talking about is an issue

01:13:42.870 --> 01:13:46.230
that if the investment
were properly focused,

01:13:46.230 --> 01:13:50.073
we could really make a change
to the health of the nation.

01:13:53.850 --> 01:13:58.440
- So look, we will be,

01:13:58.440 --> 01:14:03.150
you know, again, we are
really focused on how we work

01:14:03.150 --> 01:14:06.090
with the Secretary of State on their plans

01:14:06.090 --> 01:14:09.030
for the three shifts that he had,

01:14:09.030 --> 01:14:10.880
that the government that he has set out

01:14:10.880 --> 01:14:13.830
in their health mission.

01:14:13.830 --> 01:14:18.830
And you know, clearly we
think the evidence says,

01:14:20.883 --> 01:14:21.900
and indeed we've been talking about it

01:14:21.900 --> 01:14:23.070
for quite a long time,

01:14:23.070 --> 01:14:25.800
that if we can get the right
investment into primary care

01:14:25.800 --> 01:14:29.490
broadly defined and into
our community services,

01:14:29.490 --> 01:14:34.320
we can do a lot to care for
people closer to their homes,

01:14:34.320 --> 01:14:36.150
which is better for them,

01:14:36.150 --> 01:14:38.250
in the end, better for the taxpayer,

01:14:38.250 --> 01:14:41.820
and means our acute trust
can focus on the stuff

01:14:41.820 --> 01:14:44.030
that is genuinely an emergency
that can only be dealt with

01:14:44.030 --> 01:14:45.570
in an acute setting.

01:14:45.570 --> 01:14:47.490
I think that is absolutely the goal

01:14:47.490 --> 01:14:50.849
and ambition we all share, and indeed,

01:14:50.849 --> 01:14:53.250
you know, we're pretty focused on working

01:14:53.250 --> 01:14:56.610
with the Secretary of State and his team

01:14:56.610 --> 01:15:00.000
and the department to sort
of make a reality of that.

01:15:00.000 --> 01:15:03.147
- [Watkins] Yes, I suppose
what I was trying to do,

01:15:03.147 --> 01:15:05.337
and perhaps I didn't
present it in the best way,

01:15:05.337 --> 01:15:07.680
was to congratulate you on trying pull

01:15:07.680 --> 01:15:09.633
that triangulation together.

01:15:12.240 --> 01:15:14.553
- I'll thank you, Mary,
on Julian's behalf.

01:15:16.950 --> 01:15:19.710
Okay. Any other comments
or questions for Julian?

01:15:19.710 --> 01:15:21.660
Clearly we've gone into this year knowing

01:15:21.660 --> 01:15:23.340
it's a really tight year,

01:15:23.340 --> 01:15:25.440
and Julian thank you for the explanation

01:15:25.440 --> 01:15:26.940
about how it's trending,

01:15:26.940 --> 01:15:28.320
certainly after the first two months

01:15:28.320 --> 01:15:31.353
and looking into expectation
for the third month.

01:15:32.310 --> 01:15:35.310
Can we move to the next
item, which is Steve,

01:15:35.310 --> 01:15:36.143
I think you are up,

01:15:36.143 --> 01:15:38.850
it's autumn/winter 2024
seasonal vaccination.

01:15:38.850 --> 01:15:40.080
- Yeah, thank you Chair.

01:15:40.080 --> 01:15:43.590
So, I'm just going to talk
briefly about three things.

01:15:43.590 --> 01:15:47.370
So, a brief introduction
to the vaccine strategy,

01:15:47.370 --> 01:15:51.360
a focus on autumn/winter campaign,

01:15:51.360 --> 01:15:55.170
and a word briefly on
outbreaks for completeness.

01:15:55.170 --> 01:15:56.370
So just picking up the points

01:15:56.370 --> 01:15:58.800
that colleagues have already said.

01:15:58.800 --> 01:15:59.940
So everyone knows this,

01:15:59.940 --> 01:16:01.560
but again, worth repeating,

01:16:01.560 --> 01:16:05.340
vaccines are the most effective
public health intervention

01:16:05.340 --> 01:16:07.260
second only to clean water.

01:16:07.260 --> 01:16:11.940
So they are both good at
improving the health of the nation

01:16:11.940 --> 01:16:15.180
and fit nicely into one
of the health missions.

01:16:15.180 --> 01:16:18.570
And that's why in December, 2023,

01:16:18.570 --> 01:16:22.050
we published the NHS Vaccination Strategy,

01:16:22.050 --> 01:16:26.430
which is designed to try and
improve uptake of vaccines

01:16:26.430 --> 01:16:30.330
across a range of vaccine-preventable
diseases over time,

01:16:30.330 --> 01:16:33.120
recognising that there has
been a historic decline

01:16:33.120 --> 01:16:34.950
in vaccination rates.

01:16:34.950 --> 01:16:38.850
The strategy basically sets
out to do three key things.

01:16:38.850 --> 01:16:41.876
So one is a single front door

01:16:41.876 --> 01:16:45.660
with people understanding
what they're eligible for

01:16:45.660 --> 01:16:48.210
and being able to access a range

01:16:48.210 --> 01:16:50.730
of different providers in a convenient way

01:16:50.730 --> 01:16:52.560
that suits their lifestyle,

01:16:52.560 --> 01:16:54.540
using things like the NHS app

01:16:54.540 --> 01:16:56.490
and the National Booking Service

01:16:56.490 --> 01:16:58.680
to really drive up convenience.

01:16:58.680 --> 01:17:01.110
The second is recognising

01:17:01.110 --> 01:17:03.420
that one of the key things in the strategy

01:17:03.420 --> 01:17:06.120
is not just to improve uptake overall,

01:17:06.120 --> 01:17:09.990
but is to try and encourage
and support people

01:17:09.990 --> 01:17:13.800
who have historically not come
forward for a vaccination.

01:17:13.800 --> 01:17:16.380
These are not people
who are hard to reach,

01:17:16.380 --> 01:17:18.840
it's just that the NHS has
not organised the services

01:17:18.840 --> 01:17:22.924
in a way that they have confidence in

01:17:22.924 --> 01:17:24.000
and that is convenient for them.

01:17:24.000 --> 01:17:28.020
So the second thing the
strategy makes a big bet on

01:17:28.020 --> 01:17:30.810
is supplementing that convenient offer,

01:17:30.810 --> 01:17:32.850
that core offer across general practise

01:17:32.850 --> 01:17:34.470
and community pharmacy

01:17:34.470 --> 01:17:36.690
with what we've described as outreach.

01:17:36.690 --> 01:17:40.830
So, vaccines being given in
places that people go to,

01:17:40.830 --> 01:17:42.870
in places they trust,

01:17:42.870 --> 01:17:44.727
and given by people who have

01:17:44.727 --> 01:17:47.550
the understanding and experience
of their life circumstances

01:17:47.550 --> 01:17:48.873
to build confidence.

01:17:50.370 --> 01:17:54.000
And the third thing the strategy says is,

01:17:54.000 --> 01:17:56.010
we've talked about a number
of vaccine programmes

01:17:56.010 --> 01:17:58.380
already in this meeting.

01:17:58.380 --> 01:17:59.970
We sometimes have
historically looked at them

01:17:59.970 --> 01:18:00.860
as vertical things.

01:18:00.860 --> 01:18:04.073
So we talk about HPV, we talk about MMR,

01:18:04.073 --> 01:18:06.120
we talk about flu or COVID,

01:18:06.120 --> 01:18:09.810
actually, the third thing
is saying two things.

01:18:09.810 --> 01:18:11.880
One, this is everybody's business.

01:18:11.880 --> 01:18:15.300
So every point at which
people touch the NHS,

01:18:15.300 --> 01:18:18.600
wherever that is, we
want clinical colleagues

01:18:18.600 --> 01:18:21.690
to understand someone's vaccination status

01:18:21.690 --> 01:18:22.920
and to encourage them to have one

01:18:22.920 --> 01:18:23.880
and be able to give it to them

01:18:23.880 --> 01:18:26.490
if they haven't already had it.

01:18:26.490 --> 01:18:31.200
But also to recognise that vaccines

01:18:31.200 --> 01:18:32.850
are one part of prevention

01:18:32.850 --> 01:18:35.190
and we shouldn't be talking
about individual programmes.

01:18:35.190 --> 01:18:37.200
We should be thinking about a person

01:18:37.200 --> 01:18:39.030
and what they're eligible for in totality

01:18:39.030 --> 01:18:41.160
and prevention across the whole,

01:18:41.160 --> 01:18:43.740
whether it's blood pressure checks,

01:18:43.740 --> 01:18:47.370
the health check, vaccines,
screening, et cetera.

01:18:47.370 --> 01:18:49.110
And setting that in the context

01:18:49.110 --> 01:18:50.310
of what's important to people,

01:18:50.310 --> 01:18:53.400
which might not be about offering vaccines

01:18:53.400 --> 01:18:54.233
first and foremost,

01:18:54.233 --> 01:18:56.820
it might be about a broader
health promotion piece.

01:18:56.820 --> 01:18:57.690
So that's what the strategy says.

01:18:57.690 --> 01:18:59.220
The reason I'm highlighting that

01:18:59.220 --> 01:19:01.950
is because what we're trying
to do in autumn/winter

01:19:01.950 --> 01:19:05.610
this year reflects some of
those things in the strategy.

01:19:05.610 --> 01:19:07.650
So uptake, which is
what this is all about,

01:19:07.650 --> 01:19:09.210
is driven by three things,

01:19:09.210 --> 01:19:11.010
convenience of the offer,

01:19:11.010 --> 01:19:13.530
confidence of people in the vaccine,

01:19:13.530 --> 01:19:15.990
so, do I think it's safe?

01:19:15.990 --> 01:19:18.540
Do I have the information
that I want about it?

01:19:18.540 --> 01:19:19.800
And complacency.

01:19:19.800 --> 01:19:23.190
So do I think that the thing
is going to protect me against

01:19:23.190 --> 01:19:24.573
is serious for me?

01:19:26.592 --> 01:19:30.060
And I want to just
concentrate on three vaccines

01:19:30.060 --> 01:19:33.570
that we will have a big
focus on this autumn/winter,

01:19:33.570 --> 01:19:35.520
two of which you will be very well known

01:19:35.520 --> 01:19:36.690
to colleagues around this table,

01:19:36.690 --> 01:19:39.423
one of which is a very
important new programme.

01:19:40.380 --> 01:19:44.400
So the first one is just to
remind colleagues on flu.

01:19:44.400 --> 01:19:46.920
So the flu programme
will start in September

01:19:46.920 --> 01:19:49.930
for children and pregnant women

01:19:51.090 --> 01:19:55.260
and it will then move in October

01:19:55.260 --> 01:19:56.580
to all of the other cohorts,

01:19:56.580 --> 01:20:00.060
so over 65's and people
who are clinically at risk.

01:20:00.060 --> 01:20:02.040
That is important just
to remind colleagues that

01:20:02.040 --> 01:20:03.930
as it's a change from last year,

01:20:03.930 --> 01:20:07.710
it's a later start because
JCVI has recommended

01:20:07.710 --> 01:20:09.780
that that will give
better clinical protection

01:20:09.780 --> 01:20:12.090
and clinical benefit
to people at the height

01:20:12.090 --> 01:20:13.293
of the flu season.

01:20:14.910 --> 01:20:18.870
We are awaiting JCVI's advice

01:20:18.870 --> 01:20:21.720
and the government's
consideration of that advice

01:20:21.720 --> 01:20:23.433
in respect to the COVID programme.

01:20:24.900 --> 01:20:26.905
But what we have put in place

01:20:26.905 --> 01:20:29.070
and we have worked with providers on

01:20:29.070 --> 01:20:33.870
is saying once that advice is considered,

01:20:33.870 --> 01:20:35.520
we'll make it available obviously,

01:20:35.520 --> 01:20:37.110
but plan on the basis of something

01:20:37.110 --> 01:20:40.440
that is broadly similar to last year.

01:20:40.440 --> 01:20:42.450
And again, that would include alignment

01:20:42.450 --> 01:20:44.340
with the flu programme.

01:20:44.340 --> 01:20:45.750
We're at the moment, as
you'll see from the paper,

01:20:45.750 --> 01:20:47.970
putting in place the network

01:20:47.970 --> 01:20:51.210
and we're partway through the
procurement process for that

01:20:51.210 --> 01:20:52.950
and early indications are

01:20:52.950 --> 01:20:56.310
that there is even stronger
interest from providers,

01:20:56.310 --> 01:20:59.340
whether that's general
practise or community pharmacy,

01:20:59.340 --> 01:21:00.450
than there has been in the past.

01:21:00.450 --> 01:21:02.760
So that should really help us increase

01:21:02.760 --> 01:21:04.083
the convenience even more.

01:21:05.520 --> 01:21:07.200
This year, we've talked about,

01:21:07.200 --> 01:21:08.033
so we talked about it last year,

01:21:08.033 --> 01:21:09.270
about co-administration a lot.

01:21:09.270 --> 01:21:11.010
So the ability to get
flu and COVID together

01:21:11.010 --> 01:21:13.710
if you wanted to do so this year,

01:21:13.710 --> 01:21:15.480
you'll be able to book that

01:21:15.480 --> 01:21:16.920
through the National Booking Service.

01:21:16.920 --> 01:21:19.110
You can book either jab individually

01:21:19.110 --> 01:21:21.660
or you'll be able to book one together

01:21:21.660 --> 01:21:23.610
and you'll also be able
to book for yourself

01:21:23.610 --> 01:21:25.050
and a family member or a friend

01:21:25.050 --> 01:21:27.837
to go at the same time
if you want to do so.

01:21:27.837 --> 01:21:30.690
And that reflects some
feedback from people

01:21:30.690 --> 01:21:33.153
about something that they
felt would be helpful.

01:21:35.310 --> 01:21:39.453
If I just concentrate
then for a second on RSV,

01:21:40.560 --> 01:21:41.880
which is respiratory virus,

01:21:41.880 --> 01:21:44.040
So respiratory syncytial virus,

01:21:44.040 --> 01:21:44.970
I can't even say that,

01:21:44.970 --> 01:21:46.650
Helen will tell me that
I've pronounced that

01:21:46.650 --> 01:21:47.483
all terribly wrong.

01:21:47.483 --> 01:21:48.870
Respiratory virus.

01:21:48.870 --> 01:21:53.870
So this is a virus and a
disease that is not new

01:21:55.267 --> 01:21:59.520
but it can have quite a significant impact

01:21:59.520 --> 01:22:02.100
on children and older adults.

01:22:02.100 --> 01:22:07.050
So RSV has driven about
30,000 hospitalizations

01:22:07.050 --> 01:22:09.510
each year in under 5's,

01:22:09.510 --> 01:22:13.260
and it drives about 9,000 hospitalizations

01:22:13.260 --> 01:22:16.020
for over 75-year-olds.

01:22:16.020 --> 01:22:20.610
Around 20 to 30 children die each year

01:22:20.610 --> 01:22:23.010
from contracting the virus.

01:22:23.010 --> 01:22:25.230
And Helen will know better than many,

01:22:25.230 --> 01:22:28.050
but in addition to that more
serious end of the spectrum,

01:22:28.050 --> 01:22:30.930
it also drives lots of
contacts in primary care

01:22:30.930 --> 01:22:32.910
and general practise in particular.

01:22:32.910 --> 01:22:37.350
So the great news is that in late 2023,

01:22:37.350 --> 01:22:40.440
a vaccine which has gone through
a number of clinical trials

01:22:40.440 --> 01:22:43.140
was authorised for use by the MRHA,

01:22:43.140 --> 01:22:47.010
which is the body that tests
the safety of vaccines.

01:22:47.010 --> 01:22:49.710
And the vaccine that's been authorised

01:22:49.710 --> 01:22:52.800
and chosen for use has the potential

01:22:52.800 --> 01:22:57.800
to reduce those hospitalizations
and deaths by up to 80%.

01:22:58.190 --> 01:23:00.420
So that again, is really
quite significant,

01:23:00.420 --> 01:23:03.900
both in terms of protection
but also the wider NHS picture.

01:23:03.900 --> 01:23:06.240
So this is a really important kind of,

01:23:06.240 --> 01:23:10.233
Amanda used the phrase,
all-in piece for the NHS.

01:23:11.520 --> 01:23:13.503
We are mobilising the,

01:23:15.053 --> 01:23:18.840
we will start to deliver the
campaign at quite a big pace.

01:23:18.840 --> 01:23:22.080
We've had quite a short
amount of time to plan it,

01:23:22.080 --> 01:23:25.530
but it will start in September

01:23:25.530 --> 01:23:27.720
and there are two parts to it.

01:23:27.720 --> 01:23:32.720
The first part is a
service to protect infants,

01:23:33.210 --> 01:23:35.220
so newborns and infants,

01:23:35.220 --> 01:23:39.810
that is going to be delivered
by offering pregnant women

01:23:39.810 --> 01:23:43.563
a vaccine around 28 weeks.

01:23:44.490 --> 01:23:48.023
And so those women who are
pregnant on the 1st of September

01:23:48.023 --> 01:23:52.590
and are 28 weeks plus will
be offered the vaccine

01:23:52.590 --> 01:23:55.260
that will be delivered largely
through maternity providers.

01:23:55.260 --> 01:23:57.390
And then of course as more
people become eligible

01:23:57.390 --> 01:23:58.920
at their 28th week, through the season,

01:23:58.920 --> 01:24:00.813
they will be offered a vaccination.

01:24:02.820 --> 01:24:07.820
The second part is for
people who are over 75

01:24:08.097 --> 01:24:10.710
and this will be what's
called an all-year programme.

01:24:10.710 --> 01:24:12.870
So as people turn 75,

01:24:12.870 --> 01:24:15.540
they will be invited by
their general practitioner

01:24:15.540 --> 01:24:16.890
to come and have a vaccine.

01:24:17.820 --> 01:24:20.670
Now we want to vaccinate
as many people as possible

01:24:20.670 --> 01:24:24.270
in advance of the winter season.

01:24:24.270 --> 01:24:28.050
And so we're going to run a
catch-up campaign in effect

01:24:28.050 --> 01:24:30.207
for those who are already over 75,

01:24:30.207 --> 01:24:31.650
and we're going to try and vaccinate

01:24:31.650 --> 01:24:33.450
as many of those people as we can

01:24:33.450 --> 01:24:36.330
who want to be vaccinated
in September and October

01:24:36.330 --> 01:24:38.733
because that will maximise protection.

01:24:40.380 --> 01:24:43.650
So those are the sort of
three big seasonal programmes

01:24:43.650 --> 01:24:46.230
for autumn and winter.

01:24:46.230 --> 01:24:48.660
And then I won't cover it in huge detail,

01:24:48.660 --> 01:24:51.030
but to make colleagues
aware, it's in the paper,

01:24:51.030 --> 01:24:53.970
there have been a couple
of areas of outbreaks.

01:24:53.970 --> 01:24:56.370
So, in measles and in whooping cough,

01:24:56.370 --> 01:24:59.163
both of which are preventable
through vaccination.

01:25:00.150 --> 01:25:02.040
And we've just put a little
bit of information in the paper

01:25:02.040 --> 01:25:04.020
to highlight the work
that colleagues have done

01:25:04.020 --> 01:25:07.440
up and down the country to
try and improve the rates

01:25:07.440 --> 01:25:10.953
of MMR vaccination and
to reduce the outbreaks,

01:25:11.790 --> 01:25:15.420
that those efforts have been
successful in vaccinating

01:25:15.420 --> 01:25:17.880
a large number of people
after 2 million people

01:25:17.880 --> 01:25:19.953
were invited by the NHS to come forward.

01:25:21.330 --> 01:25:23.640
This is one of those painting
the Forth Bridge things,

01:25:23.640 --> 01:25:25.680
you just have to keep going and keep going

01:25:25.680 --> 01:25:27.750
because MMR vaccination rates

01:25:27.750 --> 01:25:29.070
are still lower than we would like

01:25:29.070 --> 01:25:33.570
and particularly so in London
where there continues to be

01:25:33.570 --> 01:25:36.450
a measles control group in place.

01:25:36.450 --> 01:25:37.773
And on whooping cough,

01:25:39.420 --> 01:25:41.040
the vaccination for that is pertussis,

01:25:41.040 --> 01:25:45.540
that is also given and
offered to pregnant mums.

01:25:45.540 --> 01:25:48.480
And that offer is a
little bit more fragmented

01:25:48.480 --> 01:25:49.313
than we would like.

01:25:49.313 --> 01:25:52.530
So we're working with
maternity service providers,

01:25:52.530 --> 01:25:55.410
with Duncan and his
team across the country,

01:25:55.410 --> 01:25:58.680
to try and put a slightly
more consistent offer

01:25:58.680 --> 01:26:00.843
to women in place.

01:26:02.100 --> 01:26:04.500
And I'll end by just sort of highlighting

01:26:04.500 --> 01:26:06.630
the point we made earlier
about thinking about people

01:26:06.630 --> 01:26:08.550
rather than programmes.

01:26:08.550 --> 01:26:11.160
So those of you who have
been paying close attention

01:26:11.160 --> 01:26:15.540
will spot that for people,
for pregnant women,

01:26:15.540 --> 01:26:17.310
there are four vaccines,

01:26:17.310 --> 01:26:20.403
flu, COVID, pertussis, and RSV.

01:26:21.810 --> 01:26:24.240
And actually the conversation
Duncan and I and the team

01:26:24.240 --> 01:26:25.290
have been having is we've got to pivot

01:26:25.290 --> 01:26:28.050
how we think about these
rather than thinking

01:26:28.050 --> 01:26:30.660
about RSV, flu, COVID,

01:26:30.660 --> 01:26:32.700
we think about the maternity offer

01:26:32.700 --> 01:26:34.890
that exists in secondary care,

01:26:34.890 --> 01:26:38.460
but also importantly
supplementing that offer

01:26:38.460 --> 01:26:41.640
in other convenient ways
that people might wish

01:26:41.640 --> 01:26:43.530
to access a vaccination.

01:26:43.530 --> 01:26:47.130
And of course there are
people who become pregnant

01:26:47.130 --> 01:26:49.140
and don't have contact with the NHS.

01:26:49.140 --> 01:26:50.490
So something that, again,

01:26:50.490 --> 01:26:53.370
we need to really carefully lean into.

01:26:53.370 --> 01:26:55.590
And I said I was going
to finish on that point,

01:26:55.590 --> 01:26:57.270
one more, and apology, which is just,

01:26:57.270 --> 01:26:58.950
we've talked about the NHS a lot.

01:26:58.950 --> 01:27:01.500
However, one of the things
that is really important

01:27:01.500 --> 01:27:03.930
that again, is part of the strategy is,

01:27:03.930 --> 01:27:06.030
the NHS is an important part of this,

01:27:06.030 --> 01:27:08.250
but it is not the only part.

01:27:08.250 --> 01:27:09.120
And the contribution

01:27:09.120 --> 01:27:10.740
that the voluntary community sector make

01:27:10.740 --> 01:27:15.570
and local government make
is completely critical,

01:27:15.570 --> 01:27:17.550
particularly in the
context of our ambition

01:27:17.550 --> 01:27:20.340
to serve people who we have
underserved in the past.

01:27:20.340 --> 01:27:22.440
So that's, I hope that's helpful.

01:27:22.440 --> 01:27:23.880
- Very good.

01:27:23.880 --> 01:27:28.110
I mean, very good and
extremely clear walkthrough

01:27:28.110 --> 01:27:31.140
of this autumn/winter seasonal
vaccination programme,

01:27:31.140 --> 01:27:32.970
particularly with the new elements to it.

01:27:32.970 --> 01:27:33.990
Can I open for questions?

01:27:33.990 --> 01:27:35.460
So, Mike and then Helen.

01:27:35.460 --> 01:27:36.293
Mike?

01:27:38.090 --> 01:27:40.023
- Mike Coupe, Non-Executive Director.

01:27:41.850 --> 01:27:45.270
It's great that you are
working on accessibility

01:27:45.270 --> 01:27:47.850
and convenience and particularly
for underserved groups.

01:27:47.850 --> 01:27:51.570
So, that sounds like a
massive step on from the past.

01:27:51.570 --> 01:27:53.340
You talked about one of
the critical criteria

01:27:53.340 --> 01:27:57.390
being confidence and that's I suspect down

01:27:57.390 --> 01:28:01.863
to how we communicate
with potential vaccinee's.

01:28:02.970 --> 01:28:05.280
Perhaps it would be helpful
just to unpack that a little bit

01:28:05.280 --> 01:28:07.380
and talk to us about
what you will be doing

01:28:07.380 --> 01:28:10.233
around communication
and building confidence.

01:28:11.610 --> 01:28:13.660
- [Meddings] Should we gather, so, Helen?

01:28:14.887 --> 01:28:17.160
- Perfectly on what
Mike said. Thanks Mike.

01:28:17.160 --> 01:28:20.798
Asking for a friend, co-administration,

01:28:20.798 --> 01:28:22.800
we talked last year there was a big push

01:28:22.800 --> 01:28:25.350
on co-administering
flu and COVID vaccines.

01:28:25.350 --> 01:28:26.340
It worked extremely well,

01:28:26.340 --> 01:28:29.580
but there wasn't a huge
push publicly for that.

01:28:29.580 --> 01:28:30.870
So confidence in public

01:28:30.870 --> 01:28:32.400
for having co-administration of vaccines,

01:28:32.400 --> 01:28:34.200
I'm thinking of those pregnant women

01:28:34.200 --> 01:28:35.400
being offered four vaccines

01:28:35.400 --> 01:28:38.250
and the over 75's will be
offered three this winter.

01:28:38.250 --> 01:28:41.100
I think the comms in this
is massively important

01:28:41.100 --> 01:28:44.520
because to be candid, the
efficiency to the NHS is massive.

01:28:44.520 --> 01:28:46.830
Giving three or four
vaccines simultaneously costs

01:28:46.830 --> 01:28:49.350
a fraction of giving
three or four separately.

01:28:49.350 --> 01:28:51.600
And it is in my experience generally,

01:28:51.600 --> 01:28:53.400
and I'd like to hear it from you,

01:28:53.400 --> 01:28:54.510
completely safe to do so.

01:28:54.510 --> 01:28:56.250
The immune system is
very, very good at it.

01:28:56.250 --> 01:28:58.110
But how do we build confidence in people

01:28:58.110 --> 01:29:00.750
which will allow us to
vaccinate far more people

01:29:00.750 --> 01:29:02.730
swiftly and effectively,
it'd be wonderful.

01:29:02.730 --> 01:29:04.590
And PS, this is fantastic work.

01:29:04.590 --> 01:29:07.350
Thank you so, so much.
You've made me very happy.

01:29:07.350 --> 01:29:08.400
- [Meddings] Keep holding these.

01:29:08.400 --> 01:29:09.723
I know Mark wanted to come in.

01:29:09.723 --> 01:29:12.030
- Yes, I mean it's an
enormously important programme

01:29:12.030 --> 01:29:13.140
and it's very good news

01:29:13.140 --> 01:29:15.750
that there's a respiratory
syncytial virus vaccine,

01:29:15.750 --> 01:29:18.870
which is very scary for a
parent of a young child.

01:29:18.870 --> 01:29:21.240
Really scary. And it does
put them in hospital.

01:29:21.240 --> 01:29:24.480
And equally it's a nasty
infection in old people as well.

01:29:24.480 --> 01:29:26.760
And this programme has to be critical

01:29:26.760 --> 01:29:28.800
to managing the winter pressures.

01:29:28.800 --> 01:29:33.060
My question is looking at the data on MMR,

01:29:33.060 --> 01:29:35.610
the question is how effective
the emails, letters,

01:29:35.610 --> 01:29:38.130
and texts to the million parents were?

01:29:38.130 --> 01:29:42.090
Because when I look at
the vaccine delivery,

01:29:42.090 --> 01:29:45.930
you sent a million letters
to quite a wide population,

01:29:45.930 --> 01:29:47.280
but there were in fact only a total

01:29:47.280 --> 01:29:51.390
of 361,000 MMR vaccines delivered,

01:29:51.390 --> 01:29:54.040
which suggests that it may not be a very

01:29:54.040 --> 01:29:56.181
successful programme.

01:29:56.181 --> 01:29:59.996
And that's not necessarily
the fault of the programme,

01:29:59.996 --> 01:30:01.380
it's just that actually,

01:30:01.380 --> 01:30:05.009
it may be that people are
quite resistant, sadly,

01:30:05.009 --> 01:30:07.620
to getting vaccination against, again,

01:30:07.620 --> 01:30:10.770
another nasty set of
infections which have,

01:30:10.770 --> 01:30:13.620
where the vaccine has transformed
a lot of lives of people

01:30:13.620 --> 01:30:14.580
who would otherwise,

01:30:14.580 --> 01:30:17.850
congenital rubella for
example, virtually gone.

01:30:17.850 --> 01:30:22.440
Measles, a killer, mumps orchitis.

01:30:22.440 --> 01:30:23.970
- So this, I'm going,

01:30:23.970 --> 01:30:26.220
there are going to be
two more coming at you.

01:30:26.220 --> 01:30:27.540
Is that all right to batch in?

01:30:27.540 --> 01:30:28.920
That would be a batch of five, Steve.

01:30:28.920 --> 01:30:31.560
So, Tanuj and then Robert?

01:30:31.560 --> 01:30:33.690
- Very quick. I thought
it was a great paper.

01:30:33.690 --> 01:30:36.480
Tanuj Kapilashrami, Associate NED.

01:30:36.480 --> 01:30:37.313
Great paper.

01:30:37.313 --> 01:30:40.470
I actually was going to
speak about lessons learned

01:30:40.470 --> 01:30:42.870
from communications in the
past and that's been covered.

01:30:42.870 --> 01:30:46.560
I want to shift the same kind of thread

01:30:46.560 --> 01:30:49.470
to education in the community, right?

01:30:49.470 --> 01:30:51.570
I mean, even with all of
the stuff that's done,

01:30:51.570 --> 01:30:55.710
we often meet people with serious
concerns about vaccination

01:30:55.710 --> 01:30:57.420
and comms is one end of it,

01:30:57.420 --> 01:30:58.950
but it's sort of education
with the community,

01:30:58.950 --> 01:31:00.480
and how are you thinking about it,

01:31:00.480 --> 01:31:02.490
partnerships in that space,
et cetera, et cetera?

01:31:02.490 --> 01:31:03.720
Thank you.

01:31:03.720 --> 01:31:05.460
- [Meddings] And then lastly, Robert.

01:31:05.460 --> 01:31:07.530
- Yeah, so I think it's
terrific programme,

01:31:07.530 --> 01:31:09.990
and obviously we've learned a huge amount

01:31:09.990 --> 01:31:11.550
through the COVID experience

01:31:11.550 --> 01:31:13.500
and vaccine hesitancy and all that.

01:31:13.500 --> 01:31:14.463
Two questions.

01:31:15.510 --> 01:31:19.530
One is, so others have asked
about sort of confidence

01:31:19.530 --> 01:31:20.970
and nervousness.

01:31:20.970 --> 01:31:22.770
I'm more interested in the complacency,

01:31:22.770 --> 01:31:24.597
which I think there probably is a lot of,

01:31:24.597 --> 01:31:26.097
and particularly around COVID.

01:31:27.120 --> 01:31:32.040
So the messaging to address complacency?

01:31:32.040 --> 01:31:33.270
I mean, you don't want to scare people,

01:31:33.270 --> 01:31:35.190
on the other hand you want to
make people aware of the risk.

01:31:35.190 --> 01:31:37.710
Is that media, or how are you doing that?

01:31:37.710 --> 01:31:40.560
The second question is
just what's your ambition

01:31:40.560 --> 01:31:44.520
in terms of vaccination rates
in the over 65's for example?

01:31:44.520 --> 01:31:46.370
I mean, do you have a target in mind?

01:31:47.460 --> 01:31:50.130
- So there you've got five
comments or questions,

01:31:50.130 --> 01:31:51.943
and you can probably answer some of them

01:31:51.943 --> 01:31:53.640
instead of wrap them up thematically.

01:31:53.640 --> 01:31:55.410
But I'll throw one specific,

01:31:55.410 --> 01:31:57.883
which Steve might know,
either of the Steve's,

01:31:57.883 --> 01:32:02.400
do we know what currently the
flu is going through Asia?

01:32:02.400 --> 01:32:03.270
Australia, Asia,

01:32:03.270 --> 01:32:04.530
have we got any information

01:32:04.530 --> 01:32:06.280
about what might be coming our way?

01:32:07.320 --> 01:32:08.340
- [Steve] I don't actually.

01:32:08.340 --> 01:32:10.440
- [Russel] I haven't
looked at that recently.

01:32:10.440 --> 01:32:12.483
Helen, you were going to New Zealand?

01:32:13.950 --> 01:32:16.050
- [Helen] It was all about
the COVID in New Zealand.

01:32:16.050 --> 01:32:17.575
No, I haven't heard this year yet.

01:32:17.575 --> 01:32:20.884
- It was too early to talk.
- Too early. Okay fine.

01:32:20.884 --> 01:32:24.107
All right, sorry. Thank you, so, Steve?

01:32:24.107 --> 01:32:24.940
- I'm glad it wasn't just me

01:32:24.940 --> 01:32:26.550
that couldn't answer that question.

01:32:26.550 --> 01:32:29.580
So, those questions fit exactly

01:32:29.580 --> 01:32:31.770
in sort of confidence and complacency.

01:32:31.770 --> 01:32:34.560
And Helen, I'll deal with your
co-admin question as well.

01:32:34.560 --> 01:32:39.540
So these are the two risks
that we see in terms of uptake.

01:32:39.540 --> 01:32:42.690
The one that is most
material is complacency from

01:32:42.690 --> 01:32:45.780
the kind of engagement that we've done.

01:32:45.780 --> 01:32:49.860
And that is partly because the perception

01:32:49.860 --> 01:32:53.190
about the severity of the disease

01:32:53.190 --> 01:32:55.260
has gone down in people's minds.

01:32:55.260 --> 01:32:57.210
Not everybody's minds,
but in general terms,

01:32:57.210 --> 01:32:58.833
particularly in younger people,

01:33:00.240 --> 01:33:03.330
that the perception that some
of these diseases are serious

01:33:03.330 --> 01:33:06.381
has gone down and therefore the appetite

01:33:06.381 --> 01:33:08.880
to take up the offer has dropped.

01:33:08.880 --> 01:33:11.823
And there's also honestly
a bit of vaccine fatigue,

01:33:12.900 --> 01:33:14.550
which is why we have to think about it

01:33:14.550 --> 01:33:15.663
slightly more broadly.

01:33:18.360 --> 01:33:21.510
On the point around education,

01:33:21.510 --> 01:33:25.152
so we've learned this in COVID,

01:33:25.152 --> 01:33:26.070
we've learned it in MMR,

01:33:26.070 --> 01:33:27.543
we've learned it in polio,

01:33:28.770 --> 01:33:31.470
and it's, you have to do
lots of different things.

01:33:31.470 --> 01:33:32.303
You're absolutely right,

01:33:32.303 --> 01:33:34.890
sort of media based comms is one thing.

01:33:34.890 --> 01:33:37.080
It will serve a certain demographic

01:33:37.080 --> 01:33:41.190
but actually some of the
most effective things,

01:33:41.190 --> 01:33:43.290
some of which is still in
place, but not totally,

01:33:43.290 --> 01:33:45.810
are things like community ambassadors.

01:33:45.810 --> 01:33:48.930
So, people want information.

01:33:48.930 --> 01:33:49.763
I think that, again,

01:33:49.763 --> 01:33:51.900
the research that we've done shows that.

01:33:51.900 --> 01:33:54.780
Most people are interested
and prepared to get a vaccine

01:33:54.780 --> 01:33:57.240
but they want to understand it

01:33:57.240 --> 01:33:58.140
and they want to understand it

01:33:58.140 --> 01:33:59.610
from somebody that they trust.

01:33:59.610 --> 01:34:00.960
And that is not always,

01:34:00.960 --> 01:34:02.970
I'm about to say something
very career-limiting,

01:34:02.970 --> 01:34:05.853
that is not always an NHS clinician.

01:34:07.020 --> 01:34:08.700
So use of community ambassadors

01:34:08.700 --> 01:34:10.440
and the voluntary and community sector

01:34:10.440 --> 01:34:12.570
has been phenomenally important.

01:34:12.570 --> 01:34:16.080
And again, we've learned
that in MMR in London.

01:34:16.080 --> 01:34:18.420
So big, big comms campaign.

01:34:18.420 --> 01:34:19.850
But also what we're just about to do

01:34:19.850 --> 01:34:23.253
is a very targeted
campaign in North London

01:34:23.253 --> 01:34:25.413
based in the community.

01:34:26.790 --> 01:34:29.550
That also sort of speaks to
Mark's very reasonable challenge

01:34:29.550 --> 01:34:32.520
on, well, that was a lot
of invites, did it work?

01:34:32.520 --> 01:34:33.900
And I think the answer is,

01:34:33.900 --> 01:34:35.880
we'll never know cause and effect,

01:34:35.880 --> 01:34:38.580
just to reassure people in
terms of our green footprint,

01:34:38.580 --> 01:34:39.810
it's not letters.

01:34:39.810 --> 01:34:42.657
There's a combination of
texts, app, and so on.

01:34:42.657 --> 01:34:45.030
And I think with MMR our view was,

01:34:45.030 --> 01:34:46.260
given the measles outbreak,

01:34:46.260 --> 01:34:49.530
you have to do everything and
then you have to do it again.

01:34:49.530 --> 01:34:51.420
Because even if a million invites

01:34:51.420 --> 01:34:53.580
gets us 370,000 vaccinations,

01:34:53.580 --> 01:34:56.670
that's 370,000 more than we had before.

01:34:56.670 --> 01:35:00.720
But we should continue to
try and hone how we do it.

01:35:00.720 --> 01:35:02.900
- [Walport] Yes. But is
that 370,000 work for all?

01:35:02.900 --> 01:35:04.500
- They were for all that's
right. That's right.

01:35:04.500 --> 01:35:06.351
- This was only, yeah.
- And we don't know

01:35:06.351 --> 01:35:09.600
within that group who
would've come forward anyway

01:35:09.600 --> 01:35:12.000
because it was, it's not,

01:35:12.000 --> 01:35:14.460
you don't drive people to
a National Booking Service.

01:35:14.460 --> 01:35:18.510
It has had some evaluation
with UKHSA, which is in draft,

01:35:18.510 --> 01:35:21.600
and we're going to look at
that with the team, again,

01:35:21.600 --> 01:35:22.650
when that's finished.

01:35:23.580 --> 01:35:24.413
And then,

01:35:24.413 --> 01:35:28.440
so I think that sort of picks
up Mike's confidence point.

01:35:28.440 --> 01:35:30.453
So I'll give you a different example.

01:35:31.560 --> 01:35:34.350
Polio, when we were trying
to encourage polio uptake,

01:35:34.350 --> 01:35:37.320
again, we did lots of inviting.

01:35:37.320 --> 01:35:41.850
We then went for a model
where school nursing services,

01:35:41.850 --> 01:35:44.220
school age immunisation
services phoned people

01:35:44.220 --> 01:35:46.860
who were not vaccinated
to have a conversation

01:35:46.860 --> 01:35:49.980
about what information
might you want and so on.

01:35:49.980 --> 01:35:52.500
And there was a very large
number of phone calls made

01:35:52.500 --> 01:35:55.440
in the nearly 10,000,

01:35:55.440 --> 01:35:59.550
and around a thousand people after that

01:35:59.550 --> 01:36:00.750
then went and had a vaccination.

01:36:00.750 --> 01:36:03.003
So again, it's hard yards on this.

01:36:06.330 --> 01:36:07.500
You just have to continue to learn

01:36:07.500 --> 01:36:09.210
because it's different in every community.

01:36:09.210 --> 01:36:11.700
And then finally, Richard,
apologies for the timing.

01:36:11.700 --> 01:36:14.970
On co-admin, so again,

01:36:14.970 --> 01:36:18.060
what the research says
is that lots of people

01:36:18.060 --> 01:36:20.490
would like to have co-administration.

01:36:20.490 --> 01:36:22.200
Our offer last year was
a little bit fragmented.

01:36:22.200 --> 01:36:23.370
We've made it easier this year.

01:36:23.370 --> 01:36:25.320
You're right, it's much more efficient.

01:36:26.310 --> 01:36:29.980
We've organised it to largely
have a couple at one time

01:36:31.170 --> 01:36:33.810
and there are some that
you can't co-administer

01:36:33.810 --> 01:36:34.643
with each other.

01:36:34.643 --> 01:36:38.490
So not everything can be
co-administered with COVID,

01:36:38.490 --> 01:36:40.920
but RSV and pertussis or
whooping cough can be.

01:36:40.920 --> 01:36:45.780
So we're trying to work out
the best way to approach that

01:36:45.780 --> 01:36:48.720
and also to give people
choice so that they don't feel

01:36:48.720 --> 01:36:50.700
like they have to have too many at once

01:36:50.700 --> 01:36:52.140
if they don't want to.

01:36:52.140 --> 01:36:53.242
- Steve, thank you.

01:36:53.242 --> 01:36:54.960
I mean, very good answers
to those questions.

01:36:54.960 --> 01:36:56.613
I'm going to move us on, I think.

01:36:57.510 --> 01:36:59.340
Outside, I mean wholly separate,

01:36:59.340 --> 01:37:02.280
I wouldn't mind knowing,
something just separately,

01:37:02.280 --> 01:37:05.400
how powerful is the NHS app

01:37:05.400 --> 01:37:08.700
given it's got 34 million subscribers

01:37:08.700 --> 01:37:10.560
as a source of information

01:37:10.560 --> 01:37:14.130
and nudging or prompting to vaccination?

01:37:14.130 --> 01:37:16.290
I'd quite like to know how that's going.

01:37:16.290 --> 01:37:17.850
- We can share the numbers with you.

01:37:17.850 --> 01:37:19.770
The app was very effective last year,

01:37:19.770 --> 01:37:21.570
so yeah, we can share that with you.

01:37:21.570 --> 01:37:23.520
- Very good. Thank you very much, okay.

01:37:24.390 --> 01:37:27.450
If the board's happy, should
we move on to item six,

01:37:27.450 --> 01:37:30.090
which is an update on cancer
diagnosis and treatment?

01:37:30.090 --> 01:37:33.270
And I think Peter, you
and David are going to,

01:37:33.270 --> 01:37:34.410
I think lead on this.

01:37:34.410 --> 01:37:36.076
Is that right?
- Thank you very much. Yes.

01:37:36.076 --> 01:37:38.913
Peter Johnson, Clinical
Director for Cancer in NHSE.

01:37:39.930 --> 01:37:44.640
You've had a paper setting
out the headline messages.

01:37:44.640 --> 01:37:49.170
I will recap briefly. Essentially,
we have a lot of cancer.

01:37:49.170 --> 01:37:51.570
We have approximately
one in two lifetime risk

01:37:51.570 --> 01:37:54.450
in my birth cohort of developing cancer

01:37:54.450 --> 01:37:58.710
as the alternative causes of
mortality have fallen back.

01:37:58.710 --> 01:38:00.900
So cancer has become the
leading cause of death

01:38:00.900 --> 01:38:02.100
under the age of 75,

01:38:02.100 --> 01:38:03.930
greater than cardiovascular stroke,

01:38:03.930 --> 01:38:06.273
liver, and respiratory disease combined.

01:38:07.950 --> 01:38:09.750
Despite the increasing incidence,

01:38:09.750 --> 01:38:13.380
we see a continued linear
drop in mortality from cancer

01:38:13.380 --> 01:38:16.590
and an improval in survival
figures in this country.

01:38:16.590 --> 01:38:19.470
But we started at a lower baseline level

01:38:19.470 --> 01:38:21.840
than comparator countries.

01:38:21.840 --> 01:38:23.880
And this is largely driven, not entirely,

01:38:23.880 --> 01:38:26.010
but largely driven by the fact

01:38:26.010 --> 01:38:28.350
that most cancers presented a later stage,

01:38:28.350 --> 01:38:29.880
generally speaking in the UK,

01:38:29.880 --> 01:38:31.560
than they do in other countries.

01:38:31.560 --> 01:38:33.960
We've therefore focused
our efforts in terms of

01:38:33.960 --> 01:38:37.050
in trying to improve survival
on improving our rates

01:38:37.050 --> 01:38:41.460
of early cancer diagnosis
which clearly has a variety

01:38:41.460 --> 01:38:44.460
of incremental requirements all the way

01:38:44.460 --> 01:38:48.060
from public awareness through
to presentation to GP's,

01:38:48.060 --> 01:38:51.540
through to referral, through
to diagnostic services.

01:38:51.540 --> 01:38:54.780
And in focusing on
earlier cancer diagnosis,

01:38:54.780 --> 01:38:58.110
we have encouraged the referral of people

01:38:58.110 --> 01:38:59.640
in greater and greater numbers

01:38:59.640 --> 01:39:01.260
for investigation of symptoms.

01:39:01.260 --> 01:39:03.930
The diagnosis of cancer is dominated

01:39:03.930 --> 01:39:05.160
by people with symptoms.

01:39:05.160 --> 01:39:08.160
More than 90% of cancers are
diagnosed in people who present

01:39:08.160 --> 01:39:10.170
with symptoms of some sort or another.

01:39:10.170 --> 01:39:12.300
And a great problem for
colleagues in primary care

01:39:12.300 --> 01:39:14.400
of course, is that in many cases,

01:39:14.400 --> 01:39:16.590
the symptoms are not at
all specific to cancer

01:39:16.590 --> 01:39:19.050
and could be due to a
variety of other causes.

01:39:19.050 --> 01:39:21.990
But 90% of cancers
present symptomatically,

01:39:21.990 --> 01:39:24.810
I'll come back to the ones that
we pick up asymptomatically

01:39:24.810 --> 01:39:27.060
through screening a bit later on.

01:39:27.060 --> 01:39:29.310
In order to drive up
rates of early diagnosis,

01:39:29.310 --> 01:39:30.570
we've encouraged more referrals.

01:39:30.570 --> 01:39:33.210
We've seen a 10% annual
increase in referrals

01:39:33.210 --> 01:39:34.833
over the last decade or more.

01:39:35.700 --> 01:39:39.420
Back in 2010, we were seeing
around a million referrals

01:39:39.420 --> 01:39:42.120
for possible cancer into the system.

01:39:42.120 --> 01:39:44.640
In the last year we saw 3 million.

01:39:44.640 --> 01:39:47.370
And so the compound effect of all this

01:39:47.370 --> 01:39:48.870
has produced enormous pressure,

01:39:48.870 --> 01:39:51.570
particularly on diagnostic services.

01:39:51.570 --> 01:39:52.740
Important to recognise

01:39:52.740 --> 01:39:55.320
that of the people referred
with possible cancer,

01:39:55.320 --> 01:39:57.690
more than 90% don't have it.

01:39:57.690 --> 01:39:59.730
So our so-called conversion rate,

01:39:59.730 --> 01:40:01.455
the number of people referred

01:40:01.455 --> 01:40:03.360
and the number of people
who actually have cancer,

01:40:03.360 --> 01:40:05.790
it's about a 6 to 7%.

01:40:05.790 --> 01:40:07.800
So we see an awful lot of people in order

01:40:07.800 --> 01:40:09.933
to make sure they don't have cancer.

01:40:10.800 --> 01:40:13.470
But the pressures this has
imposed on diagnostic services

01:40:13.470 --> 01:40:14.640
have been very significant.

01:40:14.640 --> 01:40:16.500
And this is what's led in large part

01:40:16.500 --> 01:40:17.880
to the performance challenges

01:40:17.880 --> 01:40:21.840
that we've seen over the last
few years whereby we have seen

01:40:21.840 --> 01:40:26.280
our speed of diagnosis
gradually dwindling.

01:40:26.280 --> 01:40:29.250
This has been reversed to some
degree in the last two years

01:40:29.250 --> 01:40:31.320
by the investment that we
were hearing about earlier,

01:40:31.320 --> 01:40:34.110
particularly in community
diagnostic centres.

01:40:34.110 --> 01:40:35.940
The majority of cancer diagnostics

01:40:35.940 --> 01:40:37.560
come down to some form of imaging,

01:40:37.560 --> 01:40:40.500
whether it's ultrasound,
CT scanning, or MRI,

01:40:40.500 --> 01:40:43.950
or endoscopy in large part
followed then by a biopsy

01:40:43.950 --> 01:40:46.830
and cellular pathology services.

01:40:46.830 --> 01:40:49.170
And that investment, I'm glad to say,

01:40:49.170 --> 01:40:51.210
has started to turn the situation around.

01:40:51.210 --> 01:40:53.160
As Emily said earlier,

01:40:53.160 --> 01:40:55.230
we met the faster diagnosis standard

01:40:55.230 --> 01:40:58.710
to rule in or rule out
cancer within 28 days

01:40:58.710 --> 01:41:01.470
in over 75% of people earlier this year

01:41:01.470 --> 01:41:03.240
and continue to do so.

01:41:03.240 --> 01:41:06.090
We have stabilised the situation

01:41:06.090 --> 01:41:08.820
whereby we measure from time of diagnosis

01:41:08.820 --> 01:41:12.690
to initiation of treatment
with a target of 62 days.

01:41:12.690 --> 01:41:14.730
We are not where we want to be on that.

01:41:14.730 --> 01:41:17.940
The target is 85%, we are in
the mid 60's at the moment.

01:41:17.940 --> 01:41:20.280
So we clearly have an
enormous amount to do

01:41:20.280 --> 01:41:24.000
in order to recoup that
deficit that has built up.

01:41:24.000 --> 01:41:24.840
We have, however,

01:41:24.840 --> 01:41:27.480
brought down the number of
people waiting a long time

01:41:27.480 --> 01:41:28.950
past 62 days,

01:41:28.950 --> 01:41:31.320
which had built up during
COVID back to the levels,

01:41:31.320 --> 01:41:32.520
in fact lower than the levels,

01:41:32.520 --> 01:41:34.620
that we were seeing prior to the pandemic.

01:41:35.580 --> 01:41:38.760
So other than simply seeing
more and more people referred

01:41:38.760 --> 01:41:40.650
on urgent suspected cancer pathways,

01:41:40.650 --> 01:41:43.440
what we need to do is
to be smarter at seeing

01:41:43.440 --> 01:41:45.960
how we can pick up cancer
at an earlier stage.

01:41:45.960 --> 01:41:48.750
So we have a number of case
finding initiatives to try

01:41:48.750 --> 01:41:52.050
and target the groups at
greatest risk of cancer,

01:41:52.050 --> 01:41:53.730
the most productive of which at the moment

01:41:53.730 --> 01:41:55.620
is our targeted lung health checks,

01:41:55.620 --> 01:41:59.280
whereby we are checking
people's smoking history

01:41:59.280 --> 01:42:01.230
from their primary care records,

01:42:01.230 --> 01:42:03.330
making a risk assessment
over the telephone

01:42:03.330 --> 01:42:05.220
as to their likelihood
of developing cancer

01:42:05.220 --> 01:42:06.450
in the next two years,

01:42:06.450 --> 01:42:10.380
and for those that are
more of a 1 or 2% risk,

01:42:10.380 --> 01:42:13.230
inviting them for a low dose CT scan

01:42:13.230 --> 01:42:16.410
in the main part provided in
a community setting located

01:42:16.410 --> 01:42:18.720
near to where they live and work,

01:42:18.720 --> 01:42:21.603
in many cases on mobile CT scanners.

01:42:22.440 --> 01:42:23.940
This has been very successful.

01:42:23.940 --> 01:42:26.580
We've diagnosed several thousand cancers

01:42:26.580 --> 01:42:27.840
in the last couple of years

01:42:27.840 --> 01:42:30.000
since we started running this programme.

01:42:30.000 --> 01:42:32.430
And most strikingly
these are being diagnosed

01:42:32.430 --> 01:42:34.740
at an earlier stage in
much higher proportion.

01:42:34.740 --> 01:42:38.820
Historically, 25 or 30%
of most of lung cancers

01:42:38.820 --> 01:42:40.740
would be diagnosed at an early stage

01:42:40.740 --> 01:42:42.030
in the lung health checks programme.

01:42:42.030 --> 01:42:44.010
That's over 75%.

01:42:44.010 --> 01:42:45.570
And also very significantly

01:42:45.570 --> 01:42:48.270
because we've been
targeting the communities

01:42:48.270 --> 01:42:49.830
with the highest level of deprivation

01:42:49.830 --> 01:42:53.670
and the highest rates of late
diagnosis of lung cancer,

01:42:53.670 --> 01:42:57.000
because we saw that there
was an inequality gradient

01:42:57.000 --> 01:42:58.950
from the most well off
to the least well off,

01:42:58.950 --> 01:43:01.590
with the least well off
most likely to be diagnosed

01:43:01.590 --> 01:43:03.120
with late stage lung cancer,

01:43:03.120 --> 01:43:04.920
we've actually, through targeting that,

01:43:04.920 --> 01:43:06.990
we've managed to invert that relationship

01:43:06.990 --> 01:43:09.507
so that the people living
in the worse off communities

01:43:09.507 --> 01:43:11.950
are now more likely to have
their lung cancer diagnosed

01:43:11.950 --> 01:43:15.420
at an early stage as a direct
consequence of this programme.

01:43:15.420 --> 01:43:18.330
And I think that's a
very important message,

01:43:18.330 --> 01:43:21.720
that taking healthcare to
people in the communities

01:43:21.720 --> 01:43:23.280
where you most want to target them

01:43:23.280 --> 01:43:25.260
is something where we can move the dial.

01:43:25.260 --> 01:43:27.210
Very important finding,

01:43:27.210 --> 01:43:29.160
and one which I think
we will be replicating

01:43:29.160 --> 01:43:31.260
in a number of other areas.

01:43:31.260 --> 01:43:35.550
At the same time, we want to
make sure that we are managing

01:43:35.550 --> 01:43:38.040
and helping the people referred in

01:43:38.040 --> 01:43:40.050
as efficiently and
effectively as possible.

01:43:40.050 --> 01:43:41.700
So bringing innovations

01:43:41.700 --> 01:43:43.710
into the area of triage of our referrals.

01:43:43.710 --> 01:43:47.790
The notable case is lower GI
cancer, colorectal cancer,

01:43:47.790 --> 01:43:50.850
where the implementation of
faecal immunochemical testing

01:43:50.850 --> 01:43:52.530
for people presenting with symptoms.

01:43:52.530 --> 01:43:54.210
We already use it in
the screening programme,

01:43:54.210 --> 01:43:56.460
but using it for people with symptoms

01:43:56.460 --> 01:43:59.370
to triage out the people at highest risk

01:43:59.370 --> 01:44:01.020
of actually having bowel cancer

01:44:01.020 --> 01:44:03.330
and offer them a colonoscopy rapidly

01:44:03.330 --> 01:44:06.000
versus the people with a very
low risk of colorectal cancer

01:44:06.000 --> 01:44:07.710
who can be managed in other ways,

01:44:07.710 --> 01:44:09.990
has again been something we've been using

01:44:09.990 --> 01:44:12.210
to try and improve our productivity

01:44:12.210 --> 01:44:15.240
and the effectiveness of
our diagnostic services.

01:44:15.240 --> 01:44:18.390
And a variety of other areas
of innovation also coming in

01:44:18.390 --> 01:44:19.230
to try, and as I say,

01:44:19.230 --> 01:44:22.650
drive up the productivity
using new technologies

01:44:22.650 --> 01:44:24.510
such as circulating DNA,

01:44:24.510 --> 01:44:26.580
which we can pick up in the circulation

01:44:26.580 --> 01:44:28.320
of people with lung cancers

01:44:28.320 --> 01:44:29.490
and potentially use this

01:44:29.490 --> 01:44:31.320
as an alternative means of diagnosis

01:44:31.320 --> 01:44:33.810
looking at the molecular
abnormalities in the DNA,

01:44:33.810 --> 01:44:36.870
in the circulation in those people

01:44:36.870 --> 01:44:38.880
where it's difficult to get a biopsy

01:44:38.880 --> 01:44:41.490
and it's time-consuming to
get the molecular results back

01:44:41.490 --> 01:44:43.830
by using circulating DNA in a pilot,

01:44:43.830 --> 01:44:46.920
which we're expanding to
10,000 people this year.

01:44:46.920 --> 01:44:49.080
We have been able to
significantly telescope

01:44:49.080 --> 01:44:52.560
the time to diagnosis and
thereby streamline the process

01:44:52.560 --> 01:44:54.300
and make much better use of the resources

01:44:54.300 --> 01:44:56.010
that we have at our disposal.

01:44:56.010 --> 01:44:59.940
So, a number of things,
and the paper lists others,

01:44:59.940 --> 01:45:03.300
where we are trying to more
efficiently help people

01:45:03.300 --> 01:45:06.000
who present with symptoms, who
are referred into the system,

01:45:06.000 --> 01:45:08.040
to alleviate some of the pressures.

01:45:08.040 --> 01:45:10.440
For people without symptoms,
the screening programmes,

01:45:10.440 --> 01:45:13.590
we need to maximise the
effectiveness of our programmes

01:45:13.590 --> 01:45:16.306
in breast, cervical,
and colorectal cancer,

01:45:16.306 --> 01:45:18.810
as has already been alluded to.

01:45:18.810 --> 01:45:21.840
In breast cancer, that's
primarily about driving up uptake,

01:45:21.840 --> 01:45:23.250
which has diminished progressively

01:45:23.250 --> 01:45:25.710
and diminished significantly
during the pandemic.

01:45:25.710 --> 01:45:28.710
So to try and make sure
that we can bring people in

01:45:28.710 --> 01:45:31.500
and restore their confidence
in the breast screening system.

01:45:31.500 --> 01:45:33.480
Cervical cancer screening is hovering

01:45:33.480 --> 01:45:35.490
and is close to where we want it to be,

01:45:35.490 --> 01:45:37.050
but with some extra effort

01:45:37.050 --> 01:45:40.440
and I think the self-sampling
opportunities that we can see

01:45:40.440 --> 01:45:43.380
for people who find it
difficult to have a smear

01:45:43.380 --> 01:45:45.960
for whatever reason is a big opportunity.

01:45:45.960 --> 01:45:47.130
And in colon cancer,

01:45:47.130 --> 01:45:49.860
faecal immunochemical testing
has been very successful

01:45:49.860 --> 01:45:52.260
and people will have seen the
public information campaigns

01:45:52.260 --> 01:45:54.990
that we've run to try and encourage people

01:45:54.990 --> 01:45:58.050
to return their faecal
immunochemical testing,

01:45:58.050 --> 01:46:00.300
blood in poo for short tests,

01:46:00.300 --> 01:46:03.030
which has driven uptake
progressively upwards

01:46:03.030 --> 01:46:05.760
in a linear way over the
last couple of years.

01:46:05.760 --> 01:46:07.530
There are some innovations on the horizon

01:46:07.530 --> 01:46:08.610
in this area as well

01:46:08.610 --> 01:46:11.340
and people will have seen
the work we've been doing

01:46:11.340 --> 01:46:14.850
to test the possibility of
multi-cancer or detection tests,

01:46:14.850 --> 01:46:18.360
again, using the technology
of circulating DNA,

01:46:18.360 --> 01:46:19.890
to see whether it may be possible

01:46:19.890 --> 01:46:22.500
in an asymptomatic
population at risk of cancer

01:46:22.500 --> 01:46:25.170
in their fifties, sixties
and early seventies

01:46:25.170 --> 01:46:26.520
to increase uptake.

01:46:26.520 --> 01:46:29.700
And we've literally
just concluded last week

01:46:29.700 --> 01:46:32.460
the three years of annual blood testing

01:46:32.460 --> 01:46:34.200
in a very large randomised trial,

01:46:34.200 --> 01:46:37.062
which included 140,000 people

01:46:37.062 --> 01:46:40.350
who we recruited three years
ago in the middle of COVID

01:46:40.350 --> 01:46:41.970
over a nine month period,

01:46:41.970 --> 01:46:43.920
whereby they have an annual blood test

01:46:43.920 --> 01:46:45.510
and we are analysing that to see

01:46:45.510 --> 01:46:48.413
whether we can pick up early
cancer signals in half of them

01:46:48.413 --> 01:46:50.460
and the other half is the control group.

01:46:50.460 --> 01:46:52.410
And we will see the results of that

01:46:52.410 --> 01:46:55.020
in late 2025, early 2026,

01:46:55.020 --> 01:46:56.340
and then be able to make a judgement

01:46:56.340 --> 01:46:57.900
as to whether this will help us

01:46:57.900 --> 01:47:00.933
to find more asymptomatic cancer.

01:47:02.160 --> 01:47:04.140
The net result of this activity,

01:47:04.140 --> 01:47:06.090
not withstanding the
pressures on the system

01:47:06.090 --> 01:47:08.250
and the challenges and the difficulties

01:47:08.250 --> 01:47:10.740
of increased waiting
times for diagnostics,

01:47:10.740 --> 01:47:12.510
is that we have in the last two years

01:47:12.510 --> 01:47:14.490
seen a progressive raise

01:47:14.490 --> 01:47:17.250
in the rate of early diagnosis of cancer.

01:47:17.250 --> 01:47:18.990
Since we've been recording this,

01:47:18.990 --> 01:47:22.500
it's been hovering at about 55 or 56%

01:47:22.500 --> 01:47:23.520
and in the last two years,

01:47:23.520 --> 01:47:25.590
for the first time ever
we've recorded an uptick

01:47:25.590 --> 01:47:28.170
of 2% in the early diagnosis rate.

01:47:28.170 --> 01:47:30.270
The proportion of people whose cancers

01:47:30.270 --> 01:47:32.730
are diagnosed at stage one or two.

01:47:32.730 --> 01:47:35.700
That is clearly a long way
from where our ambition is,

01:47:35.700 --> 01:47:38.020
which is that 75% of
people should be diagnosed

01:47:38.020 --> 01:47:39.360
at an early stage.

01:47:39.360 --> 01:47:42.750
But nonetheless it's an
encouraging sign initially

01:47:42.750 --> 01:47:46.470
that all this, running the system very hot

01:47:46.470 --> 01:47:50.130
in order to diagnose cancers,
is starting to pay off.

01:47:50.130 --> 01:47:52.560
And really importantly,

01:47:52.560 --> 01:47:55.770
the experience of people
diagnosed with cancer

01:47:55.770 --> 01:47:59.550
continues to be reported
relatively positively.

01:47:59.550 --> 01:48:01.470
Our cancer patient experience survey,

01:48:01.470 --> 01:48:04.977
which we send out to around
120,000 people each year

01:48:04.977 --> 01:48:07.890
and we get a rate of return of about 50%,

01:48:07.890 --> 01:48:10.140
we've just seen this year's figures.

01:48:10.140 --> 01:48:11.820
And in general terms,

01:48:11.820 --> 01:48:14.940
the level of satisfaction for
people diagnosed with cancer

01:48:14.940 --> 01:48:18.720
with the service they receive
is holding it at a high level,

01:48:18.720 --> 01:48:20.940
I think, it's 8.89 out of 10,

01:48:20.940 --> 01:48:24.810
which in terms of the
sort of things you see

01:48:24.810 --> 01:48:27.360
in the commercial sector
is really very high.

01:48:27.360 --> 01:48:29.130
And the majority of our indicators

01:48:29.130 --> 01:48:30.990
have improved over the last year.

01:48:30.990 --> 01:48:33.420
The notable one which got worse

01:48:33.420 --> 01:48:36.450
was that fewer people felt
that the length of time

01:48:36.450 --> 01:48:37.900
they were waiting for tests

01:48:39.840 --> 01:48:41.340
felt that that was reasonable down

01:48:41.340 --> 01:48:45.674
from 78.4% of people who thought
it was reasonable last year

01:48:45.674 --> 01:48:49.800
to 77.6% of people who felt
it was reasonable this year.

01:48:49.800 --> 01:48:52.980
So even where we are not
doing as well as we should

01:48:52.980 --> 01:48:55.230
and where we have big
pressures in the system,

01:48:55.230 --> 01:48:57.660
in general, the levels of
the people's experience

01:48:57.660 --> 01:48:58.800
of their care is very good.

01:48:58.800 --> 01:49:02.010
But we do recognise particularly
in the time it takes us

01:49:02.010 --> 01:49:04.650
to go from a diagnosis
to initiating treatment,

01:49:04.650 --> 01:49:08.130
that we have a significant
amount of work to do on that.

01:49:08.130 --> 01:49:11.700
And the implication of that I fear

01:49:11.700 --> 01:49:15.000
is that the proportion of
diagnostic activity occupied

01:49:15.000 --> 01:49:17.970
by diagnosing or ruling out cancer

01:49:17.970 --> 01:49:21.600
is likely to need to rise over
the next two or three years.

01:49:21.600 --> 01:49:24.630
- Thank you, so I'm going to pause,

01:49:24.630 --> 01:49:29.630
and see if people have
comments or questions.

01:49:29.970 --> 01:49:32.310
So Andrew, I know you want to come.

01:49:32.310 --> 01:49:35.730
- So, really fantastic
progress on the diagnostic part

01:49:35.730 --> 01:49:38.280
of the whole pathway, but are there,

01:49:38.280 --> 01:49:40.470
Peter, are there particular constraints

01:49:40.470 --> 01:49:43.380
on hitting the 62-day standard?

01:49:43.380 --> 01:49:46.920
Is it theatre time,
consultant time, diagnostics,

01:49:46.920 --> 01:49:49.252
or is it a combination of things?

01:49:49.252 --> 01:49:50.613
Are there just one or two things

01:49:50.613 --> 01:49:54.360
that in most organisations
are the limiting factors?

01:49:54.360 --> 01:49:57.420
- And hold see if there are
other comments or questions.

01:49:57.420 --> 01:49:59.103
Peter, I mean I would ask,

01:50:00.960 --> 01:50:03.300
I don't know, it's a
real layman's question

01:50:03.300 --> 01:50:05.160
when you talk about innovation,

01:50:05.160 --> 01:50:06.860
but I would say as I look at that,

01:50:07.770 --> 01:50:09.990
I agree with the thrust of
Andrew's question actually,

01:50:09.990 --> 01:50:11.790
which is this wonderful progress,

01:50:11.790 --> 01:50:14.040
but actually there's still
very high proportions,

01:50:14.040 --> 01:50:15.480
high percentage of the people

01:50:15.480 --> 01:50:18.972
who are not getting
treatment started in the,

01:50:18.972 --> 01:50:22.590
you know, anywhere near our
constitutional targets actually.

01:50:22.590 --> 01:50:26.130
So, you know, we have to acknowledge that,

01:50:26.130 --> 01:50:28.290
and we'll do our best to try and hit it.

01:50:28.290 --> 01:50:30.120
But just on the innovation, I,

01:50:30.120 --> 01:50:32.370
it's a combination of the
cancer vaccine launchpad

01:50:32.370 --> 01:50:33.203
and that,

01:50:34.110 --> 01:50:36.900
I think it was called a
personalised cancer vaccine

01:50:36.900 --> 01:50:40.260
given to that gentleman in Birmingham

01:50:40.260 --> 01:50:41.400
who I think had had bowel,

01:50:41.400 --> 01:50:42.233
you'll have to correct me,

01:50:42.233 --> 01:50:45.927
had had bowel cancer and
it was post the surgery

01:50:45.927 --> 01:50:50.610
and other interventions,
I think, vaccination.

01:50:50.610 --> 01:50:54.960
And it's the prospect of
potentially a fourth armament

01:50:54.960 --> 01:50:58.590
with which to fight cancer
surgery, chemo, radiotherapy.

01:50:58.590 --> 01:51:03.590
Is it silly of me to think
there might be a fourth armament

01:51:05.130 --> 01:51:08.040
that can operate at real
scale, which would be,

01:51:08.040 --> 01:51:11.340
I'll call it bespoke vaccination,
personalised vaccinations,

01:51:11.340 --> 01:51:12.510
and particularly for,

01:51:12.510 --> 01:51:14.520
I'll call them the higher volume cancers.

01:51:14.520 --> 01:51:15.353
And the other innovation,

01:51:15.353 --> 01:51:18.510
which I do think is really
potentially quite exciting.

01:51:18.510 --> 01:51:19.620
The briefing I've had,

01:51:19.620 --> 01:51:23.220
is the liquid biopsy's
ability actually to identify,

01:51:23.220 --> 01:51:24.480
and you did allude to it actually,

01:51:24.480 --> 01:51:25.560
in terms of a different way

01:51:25.560 --> 01:51:29.103
to identify cancer within populations.

01:51:30.990 --> 01:51:31.823
So, those were mine.

01:51:31.823 --> 01:51:33.570
Do you want any other
comments or questions

01:51:33.570 --> 01:51:36.210
for Peter or for David?

01:51:36.210 --> 01:51:39.990
So, do you want to pick up
Andrew's question and my comment?

01:51:39.990 --> 01:51:44.610
- So the interval between
referral and initiating treatment,

01:51:44.610 --> 01:51:46.983
if we look at where the challenges lie,

01:51:47.982 --> 01:51:50.250
in rough proportion about
three quarters of the challenge

01:51:50.250 --> 01:51:53.520
is in diagnostic times
and when we saw the times,

01:51:53.520 --> 01:51:56.460
as we've seen the times going
out over the last decade,

01:51:56.460 --> 01:51:58.020
as I say, happily stabilised now,

01:51:58.020 --> 01:51:59.550
and starting to turn around,

01:51:59.550 --> 01:52:01.800
but the majority of the deficit

01:52:01.800 --> 01:52:03.300
has been in time to diagnosis.

01:52:03.300 --> 01:52:05.310
So that's people getting a scan,

01:52:05.310 --> 01:52:08.280
people having a biopsy waiting
for the result to come back.

01:52:08.280 --> 01:52:10.830
That's about three quarters
of what we needed to solve.

01:52:10.830 --> 01:52:14.040
And that's one reason
why I think the ability

01:52:14.040 --> 01:52:15.840
to hit the faster diagnosis standard

01:52:15.840 --> 01:52:17.610
is going to be so important

01:52:17.610 --> 01:52:19.110
because what that speaks to

01:52:19.110 --> 01:52:21.360
is the timeliness of investigations

01:52:21.360 --> 01:52:24.837
and making use of the investment
in diagnostic capacity.

01:52:24.837 --> 01:52:27.030
There are still some
significant bottlenecks

01:52:27.030 --> 01:52:28.650
I'm quite anxious about.

01:52:28.650 --> 01:52:30.540
Cellular pathology and our ability

01:52:30.540 --> 01:52:34.080
to recruit sufficient
numbers of histopathologists

01:52:34.080 --> 01:52:36.960
who are the people who
actually make the diagnosis.

01:52:36.960 --> 01:52:41.430
But that's where most of the deficit lies.

01:52:41.430 --> 01:52:43.200
There is about a quarter of it,

01:52:43.200 --> 01:52:46.290
which is timely access to treatment,

01:52:46.290 --> 01:52:49.560
and of that the biggest
determinant is cancer surgery

01:52:49.560 --> 01:52:53.730
because most initial treatment
for cancer is surgical,

01:52:53.730 --> 01:52:57.300
and there, that's about
access to theatre capacity.

01:52:57.300 --> 01:52:59.700
And what we see is that as we address

01:52:59.700 --> 01:53:02.430
the elective waiting times pressures

01:53:02.430 --> 01:53:03.990
and we increase the number

01:53:03.990 --> 01:53:05.760
of elective surgical operations,

01:53:05.760 --> 01:53:08.310
so it's much more difficult
to flex the ability

01:53:08.310 --> 01:53:10.800
to increase cancer surgery in response.

01:53:10.800 --> 01:53:13.530
So every time we focus
on elective recovery,

01:53:13.530 --> 01:53:15.390
we see the cancer surgical waiting times

01:53:15.390 --> 01:53:16.680
getting a bit worse.

01:53:16.680 --> 01:53:19.440
So, there are pressures in cancer surgery.

01:53:19.440 --> 01:53:22.650
We are investing heavily in
things like robotic technology,

01:53:22.650 --> 01:53:26.010
which are very good for
driving down length of stay,

01:53:26.010 --> 01:53:28.050
improving the patient experience,

01:53:28.050 --> 01:53:31.410
reducing morbidity postoperatively,

01:53:31.410 --> 01:53:34.467
and something like 95% of
prostate cancer operations

01:53:34.467 --> 01:53:36.300
are now carried out robotically.

01:53:36.300 --> 01:53:39.630
And we will see this
spread into other areas,

01:53:39.630 --> 01:53:43.920
but access to theatres and the
ability to get our surgeons

01:53:43.920 --> 01:53:45.780
operating as much as they would like to,

01:53:45.780 --> 01:53:48.150
you can almost never
get a surgeon operating

01:53:48.150 --> 01:53:50.820
as much as they would like to
because that's what they do.

01:53:50.820 --> 01:53:54.720
But we do have a workforce
in cancer surgery

01:53:54.720 --> 01:53:56.880
who are probably not
getting as much theatre time

01:53:56.880 --> 01:53:57.713
as they would wish

01:53:57.713 --> 01:54:00.393
and that is undoubtedly
constraining our productivity.

01:54:01.530 --> 01:54:03.870
- And I know Robert, I think,

01:54:03.870 --> 01:54:05.910
wanted to ask a question, and then Mark,

01:54:05.910 --> 01:54:07.860
and then we'll probably need to move on

01:54:07.860 --> 01:54:09.180
to the next item.

01:54:09.180 --> 01:54:10.200
- Quick one, Peter,

01:54:10.200 --> 01:54:13.740
it's a really helpful update
and some great progress.

01:54:13.740 --> 01:54:18.060
You mentioned histopathology
as something of a bottleneck.

01:54:18.060 --> 01:54:20.580
What's the state of play in
terms of digital pathology

01:54:20.580 --> 01:54:24.330
linked to AI in interpreting histology?

01:54:24.330 --> 01:54:25.980
- Again, digital pathology is an area

01:54:25.980 --> 01:54:27.630
in which we've invested very substantially

01:54:27.630 --> 01:54:29.370
over the last few years.

01:54:29.370 --> 01:54:32.730
There is a very large amount
of digital pathology kit

01:54:32.730 --> 01:54:34.380
now in our hospitals.

01:54:34.380 --> 01:54:36.810
And what we are working hard on doing

01:54:36.810 --> 01:54:38.160
is the implementation plan.

01:54:38.160 --> 01:54:39.930
And this sits mostly with my colleagues

01:54:39.930 --> 01:54:43.080
in the diagnostic part of the operation,

01:54:43.080 --> 01:54:44.880
but there is a real push to implement this

01:54:44.880 --> 01:54:48.270
and we are seeing more and more
trusts going fully digital,

01:54:48.270 --> 01:54:49.650
which has enormous advantages

01:54:49.650 --> 01:54:51.240
because it means that
you can move the work

01:54:51.240 --> 01:54:53.160
around the pathology networks

01:54:53.160 --> 01:54:55.410
and you're not reliant on
the site-specific expertise

01:54:55.410 --> 01:54:56.490
and one specific hospital.

01:54:56.490 --> 01:54:57.780
You can rivet.

01:54:57.780 --> 01:55:01.260
There is a challenge as with
all things digital and IT

01:55:01.260 --> 01:55:04.260
to make sure that we've got
genuine interoperability

01:55:04.260 --> 01:55:07.230
and scalability for these systems.

01:55:07.230 --> 01:55:09.210
But actually I'm optimistic about that,

01:55:09.210 --> 01:55:12.240
and it's an area where
we are pushing very hard.

01:55:12.240 --> 01:55:16.590
To return to your question
about the immune system,

01:55:16.590 --> 01:55:19.830
the cancer vaccine area is primarily aimed

01:55:19.830 --> 01:55:21.090
at the adjuvant treatment,

01:55:21.090 --> 01:55:22.410
the postoperative treatment of people

01:55:22.410 --> 01:55:24.030
who've had a resection of a tumour

01:55:24.030 --> 01:55:26.370
where there's a risk of
recurrence from residual cells

01:55:26.370 --> 01:55:28.020
and the ability to generate a vaccine

01:55:28.020 --> 01:55:31.170
which will drive the immune
system to recognise those,

01:55:31.170 --> 01:55:33.930
which is an area where
what we are anxious to do

01:55:33.930 --> 01:55:35.730
is make sure we accelerate the research

01:55:35.730 --> 01:55:36.930
as briskly as possible.

01:55:36.930 --> 01:55:40.140
And I'm glad to say that the
trial that we've started with,

01:55:40.140 --> 01:55:41.730
which is a trial in the adjuvant treatment

01:55:41.730 --> 01:55:43.290
of colorectal cancer,

01:55:43.290 --> 01:55:46.590
the UK is now leading in
international recruitment.

01:55:46.590 --> 01:55:49.890
This trial's running in the
United States, Germany, Spain.

01:55:49.890 --> 01:55:51.540
And having put this system in place,

01:55:51.540 --> 01:55:53.370
we are now leading the recruitment in that

01:55:53.370 --> 01:55:55.260
and we've got more trials coming through

01:55:55.260 --> 01:55:56.700
as a consequence of that,

01:55:56.700 --> 01:55:58.170
which I think is going
to be very important

01:55:58.170 --> 01:55:59.003
for our patients.

01:55:59.003 --> 01:56:01.950
The whole area of immune
treatment for cancer

01:56:01.950 --> 01:56:04.770
is one where there's been
a progressive expansion

01:56:04.770 --> 01:56:07.260
over the last few years
and that will continue.

01:56:07.260 --> 01:56:08.093
For example,

01:56:08.093 --> 01:56:11.310
people with mismatch repair
deficient colorectal cancers now

01:56:11.310 --> 01:56:14.640
if you treat them with an
immune stimulating antibody

01:56:14.640 --> 01:56:16.800
preoperatively, in a proportion of cases,

01:56:16.800 --> 01:56:19.350
there is no tumour left by the
time they come to operation.

01:56:19.350 --> 01:56:22.380
So, the landscape of how we
manage many of these tumours

01:56:22.380 --> 01:56:24.150
is likely to change as
a consequence of this.

01:56:24.150 --> 01:56:26.640
- Thank you, I'm going to pick up,

01:56:26.640 --> 01:56:27.857
so Mark I think was going to ask-

01:56:27.857 --> 01:56:29.370
- [Walport] Robert
essentially asked my question.

01:56:29.370 --> 01:56:31.345
- And Julian, you want to ask?

01:56:31.345 --> 01:56:32.310
- Just a follow-up,

01:56:32.310 --> 01:56:33.660
you answered Robert's question,

01:56:33.660 --> 01:56:37.380
and I've got one other, in
answer to Robert's question,

01:56:37.380 --> 01:56:39.480
you said there's a lot of kit,

01:56:39.480 --> 01:56:41.100
people are beginning to use it,

01:56:41.100 --> 01:56:44.340
but like, Peter, you wander around a lot.

01:56:44.340 --> 01:56:46.620
Is the investment at this point paying off

01:56:46.620 --> 01:56:47.970
or have we got to go back around

01:56:47.970 --> 01:56:52.970
and really make sure
that it is paying off?

01:56:52.980 --> 01:56:54.900
And my second question is,

01:56:54.900 --> 01:56:56.580
when you look ahead two or three years

01:56:56.580 --> 01:57:00.753
for the innovation
rounds that we have run,

01:57:01.590 --> 01:57:03.756
what do you honestly think
is the most promising thing

01:57:03.756 --> 01:57:07.584
that is going to sort of come into

01:57:07.584 --> 01:57:10.950
sort of real use delivery,

01:57:10.950 --> 01:57:13.850
whatever the phrase is, over
the next three or four years?

01:57:14.760 --> 01:57:17.783
- So in terms of digital
pathologies specifically,

01:57:17.783 --> 01:57:20.280
I think the capital
investment has been effective.

01:57:20.280 --> 01:57:25.280
I think the ongoing cost of implementation

01:57:25.440 --> 01:57:28.470
and running it is something
which provider organisations

01:57:28.470 --> 01:57:30.180
are still working out.

01:57:30.180 --> 01:57:32.340
For example, the cost of
storage of digital images

01:57:32.340 --> 01:57:34.756
is something that people
are wrestling with.

01:57:34.756 --> 01:57:38.160
And I think the kind of project
management implementation

01:57:38.160 --> 01:57:40.687
capabilities within the providers

01:57:40.687 --> 01:57:42.840
is something which is quite challenging.

01:57:42.840 --> 01:57:44.340
They're trying to put in

01:57:44.340 --> 01:57:46.710
new laboratory information
management systems

01:57:46.710 --> 01:57:48.090
at the same time as going digital,

01:57:48.090 --> 01:57:50.340
at the same time as delivering

01:57:50.340 --> 01:57:53.760
the workforce expansions they need.

01:57:53.760 --> 01:57:57.120
But I do see in the diagnostics team

01:57:57.120 --> 01:57:59.190
a real push to get this done

01:57:59.190 --> 01:58:01.530
and every week I hear about
another large provider

01:58:01.530 --> 01:58:04.710
that's gone fully digital
and that is moving forward,

01:58:04.710 --> 01:58:06.630
I think quite well.

01:58:06.630 --> 01:58:09.993
In terms of the innovations
that we are funding,

01:58:11.850 --> 01:58:14.100
we've got a slew that are in development.

01:58:14.100 --> 01:58:17.441
I think for example the, the cytosponge,

01:58:17.441 --> 01:58:20.610
that swallowable device where
you have a pill on a string

01:58:20.610 --> 01:58:22.560
which has a sponge within a capsule,

01:58:22.560 --> 01:58:24.630
you swallow it and the capsule
pops inside the stomach

01:58:24.630 --> 01:58:26.550
and you can pull the sponge back out

01:58:26.550 --> 01:58:30.840
and examine the cells lining
the oesophagus in doing so,

01:58:30.840 --> 01:58:34.410
has where we've implemented
it for taking the pressure

01:58:34.410 --> 01:58:36.750
off the upper GI endoscopy waiting lists

01:58:36.750 --> 01:58:38.430
proved highly effective.

01:58:38.430 --> 01:58:40.440
Not for the primary diagnosis of cancer

01:58:40.440 --> 01:58:43.170
because I think you still
need an endoscopy for that.

01:58:43.170 --> 01:58:45.540
But for example, people
who've got heartburn

01:58:45.540 --> 01:58:48.150
and changes and pre-malignant
changes in the oesophagus

01:58:48.150 --> 01:58:50.790
which need monitoring, they
have a lot of endoscopies.

01:58:50.790 --> 01:58:52.410
But we can probably substitute those

01:58:52.410 --> 01:58:54.870
in large part with this
kind of investigation.

01:58:54.870 --> 01:58:57.600
So I think there are diagnostic techniques

01:58:57.600 --> 01:58:59.610
and devices coming
through which will help us

01:58:59.610 --> 01:59:01.230
to become more productive and to help

01:59:01.230 --> 01:59:04.200
to manage this progressively
increasing burden.

01:59:04.200 --> 01:59:06.867
- Peter, I think we could go on

01:59:06.867 --> 01:59:11.749
for quite a long time on hearing you

01:59:11.749 --> 01:59:14.217
on the topic of cancer and innovation

01:59:14.217 --> 01:59:16.560
and also actually our performance.

01:59:16.560 --> 01:59:19.740
But I'm conscious of time
for the board meeting.

01:59:19.740 --> 01:59:23.820
So, just to say thank
you very much for that.

01:59:23.820 --> 01:59:26.760
I mean, David, did you
want to comment at all?

01:59:26.760 --> 01:59:28.470
I mean it's slightly
unfair to throw you a ball

01:59:28.470 --> 01:59:31.180
without a particular question
but you are a key part of it.

01:59:31.180 --> 01:59:34.268
- Well, the one thing I
sometimes get accused of in,

01:59:34.268 --> 01:59:36.090
you know, as we talk about
these national numbers

01:59:36.090 --> 01:59:37.380
and national percentages,

01:59:37.380 --> 01:59:39.540
we lose sight of the
patient in this sometimes.

01:59:39.540 --> 01:59:41.610
And actually what I
think is really important

01:59:41.610 --> 01:59:43.110
to remember is that that, you know,

01:59:43.110 --> 01:59:45.960
that 2.4 percentage
point increase we've seen

01:59:45.960 --> 01:59:48.210
in early diagnosis may seem modest

01:59:48.210 --> 01:59:51.180
but actually that's
7,000 or so more people

01:59:51.180 --> 01:59:53.850
each year being diagnosed
at stage one and two

01:59:53.850 --> 01:59:56.370
as opposed to stage three and four,

01:59:56.370 --> 01:59:57.960
with all the, you know,

01:59:57.960 --> 02:00:00.570
opportunity for curative
treatment that goes with that.

02:00:00.570 --> 02:00:02.487
So it really matters to
the people in our care.

02:00:02.487 --> 02:00:04.626
And that's nothing we've done.

02:00:04.626 --> 02:00:08.250
- Small percentages can
hide large numbers actually.

02:00:08.250 --> 02:00:11.040
So David, thank you.

02:00:11.040 --> 02:00:13.080
So let's move on.

02:00:13.080 --> 02:00:15.510
Steve, I'm going to ask
you to try and help here

02:00:15.510 --> 02:00:17.850
and just when the board
looks at the agenda,

02:00:17.850 --> 02:00:19.560
we're going to slightly shift it round.

02:00:19.560 --> 02:00:20.672
So what we're going to do,

02:00:20.672 --> 02:00:23.250
we're going to take item
seven and item nine.

02:00:23.250 --> 02:00:26.100
So, welcome Jayne.

02:00:26.100 --> 02:00:27.720
So we have Dr. Jayne Chidgey-Clark here,

02:00:27.720 --> 02:00:28.820
the National Guardian,

02:00:29.910 --> 02:00:31.170
and actually what we're going to do,

02:00:31.170 --> 02:00:33.210
Jayne, is start if
that's alright with you.

02:00:33.210 --> 02:00:37.380
But actually then in
parallel or alongside it

02:00:37.380 --> 02:00:38.850
actually bring up item nine

02:00:38.850 --> 02:00:41.760
which is our own Freedom
to Speak Up Annual Report

02:00:41.760 --> 02:00:46.760
and to have our Freedom to
Speak Up Guardian's reflections

02:00:46.890 --> 02:00:47.723
on that.

02:00:47.723 --> 02:00:48.930
Steve, if you can help coordinate,

02:00:48.930 --> 02:00:51.300
and then actually I'm going
to move, if that's okay,

02:00:51.300 --> 02:00:53.130
for Habib and Bola.

02:00:53.130 --> 02:00:55.020
I'm going to move the health inequalities

02:00:55.020 --> 02:00:56.760
to be the item after those,

02:00:56.760 --> 02:00:58.650
but I'm putting the Freedom to Speak Up

02:00:58.650 --> 02:01:01.980
and National Guardian's
piece items together.

02:01:01.980 --> 02:01:03.568
- Thank you, Richard.

02:01:03.568 --> 02:01:04.800
Sorry, as the board knows,

02:01:04.800 --> 02:01:08.910
at the heart of everything
the NHS does, NHS England,

02:01:08.910 --> 02:01:10.320
is that ambition,

02:01:10.320 --> 02:01:12.833
that constant striving for
the highest quality care

02:01:12.833 --> 02:01:14.610
and the highest quality services

02:01:14.610 --> 02:01:16.590
for our patients and citizens.

02:01:16.590 --> 02:01:18.420
A really important component of that

02:01:18.420 --> 02:01:20.310
is keeping patients safe

02:01:20.310 --> 02:01:24.180
and ensuring that we do not
inadvertently cause harm

02:01:24.180 --> 02:01:26.220
as we are caring for patients.

02:01:26.220 --> 02:01:29.070
And in turn a key component
of that is ensuring

02:01:29.070 --> 02:01:31.980
that we learn when things do go wrong.

02:01:31.980 --> 02:01:34.530
And of course in the vast
majority of times, they go right,

02:01:34.530 --> 02:01:36.240
but occasionally they go wrong,

02:01:36.240 --> 02:01:39.780
and occasionally we can see
where there are services

02:01:39.780 --> 02:01:43.680
that are at risk of issues
and things going wrong.

02:01:43.680 --> 02:01:45.720
There are many ways in which we do that.

02:01:45.720 --> 02:01:48.510
Incident reporting, which
is now well-established,

02:01:48.510 --> 02:01:51.180
use of data through audit
and registry programmes,

02:01:51.180 --> 02:01:52.560
for example.

02:01:52.560 --> 02:01:56.250
But the Freedom to Speak
Up processes and culture

02:01:56.250 --> 02:02:00.420
is a really critical part
of that learning, too.

02:02:00.420 --> 02:02:04.590
And so today we have
two papers, as you say,

02:02:04.590 --> 02:02:05.640
on Freedom to Speak Up

02:02:05.640 --> 02:02:08.140
and I'm grateful that we
are taking them together.

02:02:09.330 --> 02:02:10.380
We've got the first report

02:02:10.380 --> 02:02:12.120
from Jayne, the National Guardian,

02:02:12.120 --> 02:02:14.400
a welcome from me as well, Jayne.

02:02:14.400 --> 02:02:17.370
NHS England and the CQC jointly fund

02:02:17.370 --> 02:02:20.160
and sponsor the National
Guardian's Office.

02:02:20.160 --> 02:02:22.197
And as part of that,

02:02:22.197 --> 02:02:25.080
the National Guardian
comes every year to report.

02:02:25.080 --> 02:02:27.060
It's not the annual report
of the National Guardian

02:02:27.060 --> 02:02:28.050
that's laid before Parliament,

02:02:28.050 --> 02:02:30.630
but it's a report to
the board of NHS England

02:02:30.630 --> 02:02:33.297
on the work of the National Guardian.

02:02:33.297 --> 02:02:36.300
And then we also have a second paper,

02:02:36.300 --> 02:02:37.920
which is our own report

02:02:37.920 --> 02:02:40.950
on our own Freedom to Speak Up processes

02:02:40.950 --> 02:02:44.010
within NHS England as an organisation.

02:02:44.010 --> 02:02:46.350
I'll say a few more words
of introduction specifically

02:02:46.350 --> 02:02:50.370
to that paper when we come to it.

02:02:50.370 --> 02:02:51.210
And I'm happy, Richard,

02:02:51.210 --> 02:02:53.550
to take discussion of
both papers at the end

02:02:53.550 --> 02:02:55.890
or each paper after each other.

02:02:55.890 --> 02:02:57.450
I'll leave that to you.

02:02:57.450 --> 02:02:59.700
But before I hand over to
Jayne for the first paper,

02:02:59.700 --> 02:03:01.110
of course it's really important to note

02:03:01.110 --> 02:03:04.290
that Freedom to Speak Up is
not just about the process,

02:03:04.290 --> 02:03:05.670
it's not just about the guardians,

02:03:05.670 --> 02:03:07.860
it's actually about the culture

02:03:07.860 --> 02:03:09.311
and it's about the role

02:03:09.311 --> 02:03:12.240
that particularly
leadership within the NHS

02:03:12.240 --> 02:03:14.370
but not just leadership has

02:03:14.370 --> 02:03:18.490
in creating a culture where
people feel enabled to

02:03:19.440 --> 02:03:21.742
and empowered to speak up,

02:03:21.742 --> 02:03:25.140
and in which the organisation
is in listening mode

02:03:25.140 --> 02:03:26.610
and responds to that speaking up.

02:03:26.610 --> 02:03:31.320
And as the National Guardian's
website says so plainly,

02:03:31.320 --> 02:03:33.870
it's about speaking up about
anything that gets in the way

02:03:33.870 --> 02:03:35.250
of doing a great job,

02:03:35.250 --> 02:03:37.680
and Freedom to Speak Up
Guardians support workers

02:03:37.680 --> 02:03:41.430
to speak up when they feel
that they're able to do so

02:03:41.430 --> 02:03:42.450
in other ways.

02:03:42.450 --> 02:03:43.740
And with that, I'll hand over to Jayne

02:03:43.740 --> 02:03:45.540
for her annual report to us.

02:03:45.540 --> 02:03:46.710
Jayne.

02:03:46.710 --> 02:03:47.640
- Thank you very much, Steve,

02:03:47.640 --> 02:03:49.080
and hank you very much,
Chair, for the invite.

02:03:49.080 --> 02:03:51.270
I can't believe it's a
year since I was last here.

02:03:51.270 --> 02:03:52.770
Where's it gone?

02:03:52.770 --> 02:03:55.290
And it is really important
to have this opportunity

02:03:55.290 --> 02:03:57.690
both to speak to you both as our funder

02:03:57.690 --> 02:03:59.760
but also as a key partner
in the work that we do.

02:03:59.760 --> 02:04:01.140
Because as the National Guardian's Office

02:04:01.140 --> 02:04:02.430
and as your National Guardian,

02:04:02.430 --> 02:04:03.960
I can't do this work alone.

02:04:03.960 --> 02:04:05.127
So I really appreciate
the opportunities to come

02:04:05.127 --> 02:04:07.710
and talk with the board
about what we've been doing,

02:04:07.710 --> 02:04:09.120
what we're planning on doing,

02:04:09.120 --> 02:04:10.890
hearing your thoughts and reflections,

02:04:10.890 --> 02:04:13.020
and also an opportunity
to thank your teams,

02:04:13.020 --> 02:04:16.350
particularly Navina's team
who we work closely with

02:04:16.350 --> 02:04:17.970
with this work that we're doing.

02:04:17.970 --> 02:04:20.850
So I don't intend to go
through the paper in detail.

02:04:20.850 --> 02:04:23.370
I'm happy to take questions or
comments on any parts of it.

02:04:23.370 --> 02:04:25.980
There's a few key highlights
I'd like to pull out.

02:04:25.980 --> 02:04:27.960
First off, just on governance,

02:04:27.960 --> 02:04:29.640
just this is actually a formal requirement

02:04:29.640 --> 02:04:31.920
that I come here underneath our

02:04:31.920 --> 02:04:33.087
Department of Health and Social Care

02:04:33.087 --> 02:04:35.280
and CQC governance framework.

02:04:35.280 --> 02:04:38.160
I come to you once a year,
go to CQC once a year,

02:04:38.160 --> 02:04:42.030
and also to CQC's Governance
and Audit Committee,

02:04:42.030 --> 02:04:45.420
as well as our own independently chaired

02:04:45.420 --> 02:04:48.720
Accountability and Liaison
board in which Sir Andrew sits,

02:04:48.720 --> 02:04:50.280
one of Navina's directors sits,

02:04:50.280 --> 02:04:52.470
along with CQC directors in the DH.

02:04:52.470 --> 02:04:54.180
So that's our governance.

02:04:54.180 --> 02:04:55.920
But what's really important
to talk to you about

02:04:55.920 --> 02:04:57.690
is the work that we've
been doing this year

02:04:57.690 --> 02:04:59.580
and really give you a reflection

02:04:59.580 --> 02:05:01.410
on some of the challenges that we face.

02:05:01.410 --> 02:05:02.313
Because I think we're at a time,

02:05:02.313 --> 02:05:05.100
and I said it last year,
I was really worried,

02:05:05.100 --> 02:05:06.450
I remain really worried,

02:05:06.450 --> 02:05:08.460
and I think we should
all remain really worried

02:05:08.460 --> 02:05:12.114
about the extent that there is still to do

02:05:12.114 --> 02:05:16.770
for changing the culture for
better in NHS organisations,

02:05:16.770 --> 02:05:17.760
and from my perspective,

02:05:17.760 --> 02:05:19.770
wider for those
organisations that, you know,

02:05:19.770 --> 02:05:22.593
look after people with NHS funding.

02:05:23.550 --> 02:05:24.450
So you'll see in the report,

02:05:24.450 --> 02:05:27.200
I start off by just
talking about our strategy.

02:05:27.200 --> 02:05:29.490
We published a refreshed
strategy this month.

02:05:29.490 --> 02:05:31.080
It was due to be published
just before the election,

02:05:31.080 --> 02:05:33.690
but because of pre-election
period, we couldn't.

02:05:33.690 --> 02:05:35.880
There'll be nothing brand
new in there for you.

02:05:35.880 --> 02:05:38.250
It just focuses for us as an organisation

02:05:38.250 --> 02:05:40.320
our work plan for the
next three to five years,

02:05:40.320 --> 02:05:41.160
our priorities.

02:05:41.160 --> 02:05:44.310
We've done it in conjunction
with our key stakeholders

02:05:44.310 --> 02:05:47.310
and that will help frame our
work and underpinning it,

02:05:47.310 --> 02:05:51.240
since it's a really important
year on year business plan

02:05:51.240 --> 02:05:54.240
and initiatives that we
will be responding to.

02:05:54.240 --> 02:05:56.520
What's really important in that strategy

02:05:56.520 --> 02:05:58.890
is a change of focus in our terminology

02:05:58.890 --> 02:06:00.780
from people feeling safe to speak up

02:06:00.780 --> 02:06:03.090
to people feeling confident to speak up.

02:06:03.090 --> 02:06:05.460
And that felt really
important to our stakeholders

02:06:05.460 --> 02:06:06.900
as well as ourselves

02:06:06.900 --> 02:06:08.850
because in order to be confident

02:06:08.850 --> 02:06:11.430
you have to know that
something's going to be done.

02:06:11.430 --> 02:06:12.840
And I think that's part of, you know,

02:06:12.840 --> 02:06:16.410
we talk about the barriers
of a good, open culture

02:06:16.410 --> 02:06:18.990
as being, the two biggest being fear,

02:06:18.990 --> 02:06:20.460
something's bad's going to happen

02:06:20.460 --> 02:06:21.780
if I put my head above the parapet

02:06:21.780 --> 02:06:23.310
and make a suggestion for improvement,

02:06:23.310 --> 02:06:26.820
or point out something that may be a risk

02:06:26.820 --> 02:06:29.100
to a patient or to a colleague.

02:06:29.100 --> 02:06:32.010
And then alongside
that, it's the futility.

02:06:32.010 --> 02:06:33.990
Why bother? I've spoken
up, nothing's been done.

02:06:33.990 --> 02:06:36.060
And that's what Sir Robert was envisaging

02:06:36.060 --> 02:06:38.790
when he introduced, or
suggested the introduction,

02:06:38.790 --> 02:06:39.960
of a new role,

02:06:39.960 --> 02:06:43.020
the role not to replace the existing roots

02:06:43.020 --> 02:06:44.820
for speaking up because
ideally we should all be able

02:06:44.820 --> 02:06:46.260
to talk to our line manager.

02:06:46.260 --> 02:06:47.910
We should all be able to talk, you know,

02:06:47.910 --> 02:06:49.290
within our existing structures.

02:06:49.290 --> 02:06:52.530
But we know for different
reasons, people can't.

02:06:52.530 --> 02:06:54.540
So that's what the additional safety route

02:06:54.540 --> 02:06:56.130
of having a guardian in an organisation.

02:06:56.130 --> 02:06:58.350
But when I talk about speak up culture,

02:06:58.350 --> 02:07:00.600
yes, we need to get the
guardian routes right.

02:07:00.600 --> 02:07:02.160
And you know, I'm really interested

02:07:02.160 --> 02:07:03.420
and I'm glad you've put your paper

02:07:03.420 --> 02:07:06.150
on the same day as I'm here
to hear that and see that.

02:07:06.150 --> 02:07:09.360
But actually what we
need from organisations

02:07:09.360 --> 02:07:11.790
is to be thinking about the wider culture.

02:07:11.790 --> 02:07:14.760
How are we supporting our
managers in speaking up?

02:07:14.760 --> 02:07:16.680
How are we supporting them

02:07:16.680 --> 02:07:18.990
to do the right thing when
people do speak up to them,

02:07:18.990 --> 02:07:21.180
not just when it comes
to the guardian route?

02:07:21.180 --> 02:07:22.500
Because you know, in effect,

02:07:22.500 --> 02:07:24.000
stuff coming through the guardian route

02:07:24.000 --> 02:07:26.280
is a bit of a failure of
those other processes.

02:07:26.280 --> 02:07:27.990
So, we need that overarching,

02:07:27.990 --> 02:07:29.040
and that's why I'm speaking to you

02:07:29.040 --> 02:07:30.990
as a board for your employees

02:07:30.990 --> 02:07:35.990
as well as you leading the
NHS organisations out there.

02:07:36.120 --> 02:07:40.380
So that said, I've included
highlights of progress

02:07:40.380 --> 02:07:42.450
of what we've been up to in the last year.

02:07:42.450 --> 02:07:47.040
We do have now over 1,200
trained and registered guardians

02:07:47.040 --> 02:07:49.590
which is a year on year increase,

02:07:49.590 --> 02:07:51.510
both within obviously all NHS trusts

02:07:51.510 --> 02:07:53.580
are mandated to through the contract,

02:07:53.580 --> 02:07:55.350
but in addition, we have ICBs,

02:07:55.350 --> 02:07:57.210
we have private providers,

02:07:57.210 --> 02:07:59.970
we have national bodies like yourselves,

02:07:59.970 --> 02:08:04.080
and we have primary care
increasingly up-taking

02:08:04.080 --> 02:08:05.370
using guardian services.

02:08:05.370 --> 02:08:06.960
More on that in a minute.

02:08:06.960 --> 02:08:08.940
What's really interesting
is that other sectors

02:08:08.940 --> 02:08:12.330
outside of health are coming to us to say,

02:08:12.330 --> 02:08:14.550
let's learn about this NHS model.

02:08:14.550 --> 02:08:16.830
And I think that's really
encouraging that people are seeing

02:08:16.830 --> 02:08:18.150
that there are some benefits here.

02:08:18.150 --> 02:08:19.620
Obviously, we believe, you believe,

02:08:19.620 --> 02:08:22.470
we believe there are benefits
in what we're seeing,

02:08:22.470 --> 02:08:23.303
and wanting to look at that.

02:08:23.303 --> 02:08:25.410
And we are sharing that practise with them

02:08:25.410 --> 02:08:28.020
through a very vibrant concept network.

02:08:28.020 --> 02:08:30.270
But you know, I've had
conversations with cabinet office,

02:08:30.270 --> 02:08:35.270
with some of the blue light
services, with education.

02:08:35.460 --> 02:08:37.140
This work is for all organisations.

02:08:37.140 --> 02:08:38.910
We all need to get our culture right.

02:08:38.910 --> 02:08:40.210
So, it's really important.

02:08:41.850 --> 02:08:44.670
Last week, and I'm sorry
the links to the report

02:08:44.670 --> 02:08:45.510
aren't in your report

02:08:45.510 --> 02:08:47.340
because of your timing
of getting papers in.

02:08:47.340 --> 02:08:48.270
But if you're interested in,

02:08:48.270 --> 02:08:50.370
and I hope you will all
individually be interested in,

02:08:50.370 --> 02:08:52.110
looking at the two
really important reports

02:08:52.110 --> 02:08:54.330
we've produced last week and this week.

02:08:54.330 --> 02:08:55.830
The first is on annual data.

02:08:55.830 --> 02:08:59.640
So we collect anonymized
data from all the guardians

02:08:59.640 --> 02:09:01.290
and when we look at that data,

02:09:01.290 --> 02:09:05.910
there's a 27% increase in cases reported

02:09:05.910 --> 02:09:07.350
through the guardian route.

02:09:07.350 --> 02:09:09.450
Now on the one hand that's a good thing

02:09:09.450 --> 02:09:11.010
because at least they're
bringing them to there

02:09:11.010 --> 02:09:12.030
because they might not be going

02:09:12.030 --> 02:09:13.230
anywhere else in organisations.

02:09:13.230 --> 02:09:14.910
And when you think about
the significant number

02:09:14.910 --> 02:09:16.620
that will be patient safety ones,

02:09:16.620 --> 02:09:20.730
and I don't just mean those
coded as patient safety issues

02:09:20.730 --> 02:09:22.140
because actually the worker wellbeing

02:09:22.140 --> 02:09:23.190
and worker safety issues,

02:09:23.190 --> 02:09:25.170
the bullying, harassment, victimisation,

02:09:25.170 --> 02:09:28.620
we know they have a direct
impact on patient safety.

02:09:28.620 --> 02:09:31.598
We've seen the evidence
of that both in the UK

02:09:31.598 --> 02:09:33.480
and internationally.

02:09:33.480 --> 02:09:36.207
So in effect, the majority of those cases

02:09:36.207 --> 02:09:39.000
are going to have patient
safety implications.

02:09:39.000 --> 02:09:41.160
So, am I concerned that there's a rise?

02:09:41.160 --> 02:09:42.090
I am and I'm not.

02:09:42.090 --> 02:09:43.830
I am in the sense of,

02:09:43.830 --> 02:09:45.240
what's wrong with those other routes

02:09:45.240 --> 02:09:46.920
that they're not working well enough

02:09:46.920 --> 02:09:48.746
that we're seeing an increase?

02:09:48.746 --> 02:09:49.579
But on the other hand,

02:09:49.579 --> 02:09:51.030
I'm glad that they're
going to the guardian

02:09:51.030 --> 02:09:51.980
because that's, you know,

02:09:51.980 --> 02:09:56.610
37,000 opportunities to
listen, learn and improve,

02:09:56.610 --> 02:09:59.220
and potentially prevent
harm in the future.

02:09:59.220 --> 02:10:02.040
So, that's I think key headlines for that.

02:10:02.040 --> 02:10:04.440
The other headline to share with you

02:10:04.440 --> 02:10:07.470
is the 4% that's the same as last year.

02:10:07.470 --> 02:10:10.680
The 4% of cases that are still
being reported to guardians

02:10:10.680 --> 02:10:12.480
is feeling the person speaking up feels

02:10:12.480 --> 02:10:14.280
they've suffered detriment for doing so.

02:10:14.280 --> 02:10:16.080
We've seen that in some high profile cases

02:10:16.080 --> 02:10:18.660
going through employment
tribunals and in the media.

02:10:18.660 --> 02:10:20.460
And clearly that's something that's,

02:10:20.460 --> 02:10:21.870
through the work that you are leading,

02:10:21.870 --> 02:10:24.390
through the leadership
capability framework,

02:10:24.390 --> 02:10:26.430
we have to tackle as a system

02:10:26.430 --> 02:10:28.860
to ensure that leaders and managers

02:10:28.860 --> 02:10:31.230
are not allowing detriment to happen

02:10:31.230 --> 02:10:33.750
for doing the right thing,
which is what speaking up is.

02:10:33.750 --> 02:10:36.390
So that figure, and talking
to your point, David,

02:10:36.390 --> 02:10:39.630
about small percentages,
what's that in real numbers?

02:10:39.630 --> 02:10:42.510
That's over 1,500 individuals,

02:10:42.510 --> 02:10:44.580
and that's the ones
using the guardian route.

02:10:44.580 --> 02:10:46.020
How about those that
are suffering detriment

02:10:46.020 --> 02:10:48.450
for speaking up to their
line manager, or going to HR,

02:10:48.450 --> 02:10:50.940
or going to, you know,
the patient safety team?

02:10:50.940 --> 02:10:53.763
So it is a significant issue,
this issue of detriment.

02:10:55.260 --> 02:10:59.070
If I just come to the NHS Staff Survey,

02:10:59.070 --> 02:11:00.990
so you published your staff
survey earlier in the year,

02:11:00.990 --> 02:11:02.850
we published yesterday,

02:11:02.850 --> 02:11:05.910
our analysis of the
speaking up component of it.

02:11:05.910 --> 02:11:10.377
Key headlines for that is
another warning call to us all.

02:11:10.377 --> 02:11:13.230
And that's the clinical practise questions

02:11:13.230 --> 02:11:15.780
are showing a decline.

02:11:15.780 --> 02:11:19.500
So, do I feel safe speaking
up about clinical concerns

02:11:19.500 --> 02:11:22.353
and feeling that it's a
five-year all-time low.

02:11:23.400 --> 02:11:24.840
3 out of 10 people don't feel,

02:11:24.840 --> 02:11:26.070
who answered the staff survey,

02:11:26.070 --> 02:11:28.950
and we don't know those who
didn't answer the staff survey.

02:11:28.950 --> 02:11:31.440
So we have absolutely
no room for complacency.

02:11:31.440 --> 02:11:33.957
And so the work that you are
doing, that we are doing,

02:11:33.957 --> 02:11:36.930
the important work of CQC and
their regulation of well-led,

02:11:36.930 --> 02:11:40.380
and really importantly the work
of individual organisations

02:11:40.380 --> 02:11:42.090
to really engage.

02:11:42.090 --> 02:11:43.620
And I think you used the word earlier

02:11:43.620 --> 02:11:47.520
about engaging our workforce,
Steve, is absolutely key.

02:11:47.520 --> 02:11:50.550
Because if we don't engage
really meaningfully,

02:11:50.550 --> 02:11:52.380
and that's not just having a policy,

02:11:52.380 --> 02:11:54.570
adopting the national policy
and having a guardian,

02:11:54.570 --> 02:11:56.640
but if we don't engage
through staff forums,

02:11:56.640 --> 02:11:58.170
if we don't engage through

02:11:58.170 --> 02:12:01.560
our line management
structures and meetings,

02:12:01.560 --> 02:12:03.390
then people aren't going to come to us

02:12:03.390 --> 02:12:05.220
and tell us what we need to know.

02:12:05.220 --> 02:12:06.783
So, really important.

02:12:08.340 --> 02:12:10.260
Overall the score is stable,

02:12:10.260 --> 02:12:14.010
but that's hiding the fact
that the clinical concerns

02:12:14.010 --> 02:12:17.220
are decreasing and we need to see that,

02:12:17.220 --> 02:12:20.643
shifting the dial on that in
a more positive perspective.

02:12:21.986 --> 02:12:22.971
I then talk about some of the other work,

02:12:22.971 --> 02:12:25.410
the work of the Ambulance
Trust Review that we did

02:12:25.410 --> 02:12:27.480
resulted in NHS England being commissioned

02:12:27.480 --> 02:12:29.820
to carry out their more wider
reaching cultural review

02:12:29.820 --> 02:12:30.690
into ambulance trust,

02:12:30.690 --> 02:12:32.790
which I'm sure you've
regularly heard about.

02:12:32.790 --> 02:12:35.013
And that ongoing work is really important,

02:12:35.850 --> 02:12:39.060
never more so from what was
on Sky News yesterday, Steve,

02:12:39.060 --> 02:12:42.540
that you commented on in
terms of the ongoing concerns

02:12:42.540 --> 02:12:43.680
around sexual safety.

02:12:43.680 --> 02:12:45.180
But it's much wider than that.

02:12:46.680 --> 02:12:51.090
In terms of, that Speak Up
Review obviously concluded,

02:12:51.090 --> 02:12:52.290
but we're continuing the work

02:12:52.290 --> 02:12:55.080
with making sure the
recommendations become embedded.

02:12:55.080 --> 02:12:56.850
We've commenced our second one

02:12:56.850 --> 02:12:59.580
and that's on the theme of
overseas trained workers.

02:12:59.580 --> 02:13:02.940
We know from local, as well
as international research,

02:13:02.940 --> 02:13:04.740
that those of our colleagues,

02:13:04.740 --> 02:13:08.040
of which there are so
many valuable in the NHS,

02:13:08.040 --> 02:13:11.070
we know they have a more
difficult time speaking up

02:13:11.070 --> 02:13:13.710
and also on the response to speaking up.

02:13:13.710 --> 02:13:15.690
So, we're wanting to do
a deep dive into that.

02:13:15.690 --> 02:13:17.550
We're working closely with
NHS England colleagues

02:13:17.550 --> 02:13:19.380
because I know you are doing lots of work

02:13:19.380 --> 02:13:20.580
in that area as well.

02:13:20.580 --> 02:13:22.167
But we hope it will give value added,

02:13:22.167 --> 02:13:23.790
and we can come up with
some recommendations

02:13:23.790 --> 02:13:27.000
that will help organisations
in considering particular needs

02:13:27.000 --> 02:13:29.040
of overseas trade workers when it comes

02:13:29.040 --> 02:13:31.590
to thinking about the
cultural differences.

02:13:31.590 --> 02:13:32.520
And we might say,

02:13:32.520 --> 02:13:33.990
we've got a great Freedom
to Speak Up policy,

02:13:33.990 --> 02:13:35.970
come and talk to us. But if
I don't come from a culture

02:13:35.970 --> 02:13:38.520
where that is acceptable
or deemed respectful,

02:13:38.520 --> 02:13:41.430
then we are on a hiding
to nothing to start.

02:13:41.430 --> 02:13:44.880
So, some really important
work to consider there

02:13:44.880 --> 02:13:46.020
and we'll be publishing that

02:13:46.020 --> 02:13:47.370
hopefully by the end of this year.

02:13:47.370 --> 02:13:48.783
Work is well underway now.

02:13:49.800 --> 02:13:51.390
I've highlighted a
section that talks about

02:13:51.390 --> 02:13:52.770
our partnership working.

02:13:52.770 --> 02:13:54.420
I've alluded to some
of it with yourselves,

02:13:54.420 --> 02:13:56.250
but it's with others as well,

02:13:56.250 --> 02:13:58.800
and particularly another one

02:13:58.800 --> 02:14:00.570
that's been of import with yourselves,

02:14:00.570 --> 02:14:02.730
and that's the work that
Sir Andrew co-chaired

02:14:02.730 --> 02:14:06.900
with ourselves following
the Lucy Letby trial

02:14:06.900 --> 02:14:10.020
and announcements whereby
we were commissioned

02:14:10.020 --> 02:14:13.890
to look at escalation routes
for serious matters of concern.

02:14:13.890 --> 02:14:16.050
And that work is ongoing,

02:14:16.050 --> 02:14:19.770
and it's a really important
piece of work to conclude

02:14:19.770 --> 02:14:22.890
because we do need everybody
out there in the system

02:14:22.890 --> 02:14:24.390
to know how they can escalate

02:14:24.390 --> 02:14:28.230
if they don't feel internally
that their concerns

02:14:28.230 --> 02:14:30.438
are being taken seriously.

02:14:30.438 --> 02:14:31.950
And I think it's really
important to say that I know

02:14:31.950 --> 02:14:33.210
from the board visits that I've done

02:14:33.210 --> 02:14:34.560
in multiple organisations,

02:14:34.560 --> 02:14:36.660
guardians I've spoken to is,

02:14:36.660 --> 02:14:38.700
and following the letters
that you've sent out,

02:14:38.700 --> 02:14:40.800
I know that organisations
are asking themselves

02:14:40.800 --> 02:14:42.060
those uncomfortable questions.

02:14:42.060 --> 02:14:43.470
Could this happen here?

02:14:43.470 --> 02:14:46.020
And it's really important that we continue

02:14:46.020 --> 02:14:48.090
to ask ourselves those questions

02:14:48.090 --> 02:14:50.667
because that's why it's so
important that people can tell us

02:14:50.667 --> 02:14:52.713
and will act on it if they do.

02:14:54.567 --> 02:14:57.150
I noted that I went to the
Health and Social Care Committee

02:14:57.150 --> 02:14:58.500
and took part in the inquiry into

02:14:58.500 --> 02:15:01.260
the NHS Leadership Performance
and Patient Safety inquiry.

02:15:01.260 --> 02:15:04.260
And I wanted to highlight
a particular comment

02:15:04.260 --> 02:15:06.000
that I made there at that committee.

02:15:06.000 --> 02:15:09.060
And that's, I feel the
importance that needs

02:15:09.060 --> 02:15:10.800
to come from the top, and from the top,

02:15:10.800 --> 02:15:12.660
I mean from DH and from government

02:15:12.660 --> 02:15:14.580
as well as from yourselves,

02:15:14.580 --> 02:15:18.090
around the importance of
finance and performance.

02:15:18.090 --> 02:15:20.730
Yes, there's a lot of
discussion about that today,

02:15:20.730 --> 02:15:23.580
but actually culture needs
to be that third arm.

02:15:23.580 --> 02:15:25.590
And I know at local level

02:15:25.590 --> 02:15:28.500
that quality discussion is very apparent,

02:15:28.500 --> 02:15:29.910
but it's a quality discussion

02:15:29.910 --> 02:15:32.100
I don't think always goes wider

02:15:32.100 --> 02:15:33.660
to be that wider cultural discussion.

02:15:33.660 --> 02:15:36.240
And I'd like to actually see
that as a bit of a change

02:15:36.240 --> 02:15:38.037
because we know what we measure

02:15:38.037 --> 02:15:40.740
and what we focus on gets the attention.

02:15:40.740 --> 02:15:42.960
And I think we need to set
some example in saying,

02:15:42.960 --> 02:15:44.807
this is really important
that we look at this

02:15:44.807 --> 02:15:46.770
in a greater depth,

02:15:46.770 --> 02:15:51.000
with equal weighting to
finance and performance.

02:15:51.000 --> 02:15:53.520
Because we know we're not
going to have best performance

02:15:53.520 --> 02:15:55.470
and we're not going to
use our finances best

02:15:55.470 --> 02:15:57.033
if we don't have great culture.

02:15:58.680 --> 02:16:00.300
The work with primary medical services

02:16:00.300 --> 02:16:03.030
and integrated care systems
is really important.

02:16:03.030 --> 02:16:05.910
The Guardian model has
been well-established now

02:16:05.910 --> 02:16:08.160
in NHS trusts,

02:16:08.160 --> 02:16:12.270
but it has other challenges
within primary medical services

02:16:12.270 --> 02:16:15.450
and we're working closely
with your teams on that.

02:16:15.450 --> 02:16:17.490
We don't have all the
answers for that yet,

02:16:17.490 --> 02:16:18.510
but it is very important,

02:16:18.510 --> 02:16:21.000
and particularly
understanding the role of ICBs

02:16:21.000 --> 02:16:23.040
in that ICBs are looking forward

02:16:23.040 --> 02:16:25.410
to greater clarity on their role

02:16:25.410 --> 02:16:27.660
and their jurisdiction in that space.

02:16:27.660 --> 02:16:28.830
So, it's an important piece of work

02:16:28.830 --> 02:16:31.383
that we are looking forward
to continuing with you.

02:16:32.640 --> 02:16:34.006
I've included in my report

02:16:34.006 --> 02:16:36.450
some discussion around what we're doing

02:16:36.450 --> 02:16:38.400
to improve our support for guardians,

02:16:38.400 --> 02:16:41.880
their training and compliance issues,

02:16:41.880 --> 02:16:45.030
and also further
development of their roles.

02:16:45.030 --> 02:16:47.070
Happy to take any questions on that.

02:16:47.070 --> 02:16:48.566
On the compliance note,

02:16:48.566 --> 02:16:51.480
we've realised that
what's really important

02:16:51.480 --> 02:16:54.120
is that we give information
back to the regulators

02:16:54.120 --> 02:16:55.770
if organisations are non-compliant,

02:16:55.770 --> 02:16:57.210
either by having a guardian,

02:16:57.210 --> 02:17:00.690
guardian's not trained
and registered with us,

02:17:00.690 --> 02:17:02.700
or if they're not submitting data.

02:17:02.700 --> 02:17:04.050
Because these are key requirements

02:17:04.050 --> 02:17:05.970
that you've put in the NHS contract.

02:17:05.970 --> 02:17:10.011
And we worry that if those organisations

02:17:10.011 --> 02:17:12.840
aren't investing the time
in their guardian service

02:17:12.840 --> 02:17:14.700
to be able to do those things,

02:17:14.700 --> 02:17:18.356
so if we hear or we see that a guardian

02:17:18.356 --> 02:17:20.400
is not submitting data,

02:17:20.400 --> 02:17:22.140
or an organisation doesn't have one,

02:17:22.140 --> 02:17:23.430
I would write to them and ask,

02:17:23.430 --> 02:17:24.330
you know, what's happening?

02:17:24.330 --> 02:17:25.650
Can we help you with this?

02:17:25.650 --> 02:17:27.420
If I'm not going to
get anywhere with that,

02:17:27.420 --> 02:17:29.700
then I will be escalating
to yourselves and CQC

02:17:29.700 --> 02:17:32.010
and looking for some
support in that compliance,

02:17:32.010 --> 02:17:34.230
because it's either important or it isn't.

02:17:34.230 --> 02:17:35.910
And I think that's really important.

02:17:35.910 --> 02:17:37.320
And quite often it's not

02:17:37.320 --> 02:17:39.420
because a guardian isn't submitting data

02:17:39.420 --> 02:17:40.890
because they're not performing,

02:17:40.890 --> 02:17:42.540
it quite often is because the guardian

02:17:42.540 --> 02:17:45.090
hasn't been given adequate
time to carry out the role.

02:17:45.090 --> 02:17:49.020
We still see a big disparity
in how the Guardian role

02:17:49.020 --> 02:17:52.980
is implemented and some
boards and some organisations

02:17:52.980 --> 02:17:57.150
are not putting enough
resource into that role

02:17:57.150 --> 02:17:58.320
to enable it to actually do

02:17:58.320 --> 02:18:00.750
its both proactive and
reactive components.

02:18:00.750 --> 02:18:02.880
So we'll be looking for further guidance

02:18:02.880 --> 02:18:04.130
for that later this year.

02:18:05.040 --> 02:18:06.030
Section on some of the comms

02:18:06.030 --> 02:18:08.100
and engagement that we've been doing.

02:18:08.100 --> 02:18:10.590
And really, just out of my conclusions,

02:18:10.590 --> 02:18:11.880
what I just want to highlight to you

02:18:11.880 --> 02:18:13.410
is that there's a lot of work to do,

02:18:13.410 --> 02:18:15.030
as absolutely no complacency from me

02:18:15.030 --> 02:18:17.760
and my very small office.

02:18:17.760 --> 02:18:19.500
But what's really important
is that we continue

02:18:19.500 --> 02:18:21.420
to work with yourself as the regulator

02:18:21.420 --> 02:18:24.540
with CQC and with our system partners

02:18:24.540 --> 02:18:26.520
because all of us can't afford not to.

02:18:26.520 --> 02:18:28.920
This is too important an issue.

02:18:28.920 --> 02:18:31.320
We saw what happens when
it goes horribly wrong.

02:18:32.340 --> 02:18:34.290
And I know we're all
waiting for the results

02:18:34.290 --> 02:18:35.220
of the Thirlwall Inquiry,

02:18:35.220 --> 02:18:39.660
but in the meantime we know
we need to increase the focus

02:18:39.660 --> 02:18:41.100
on improving culture.

02:18:41.100 --> 02:18:44.520
Because without it, I think Megan Wrights

02:18:44.520 --> 02:18:47.250
is a researcher in this
field of psychological safety

02:18:47.250 --> 02:18:48.780
and speaking up and she talks

02:18:48.780 --> 02:18:50.160
that the missing voices,

02:18:50.160 --> 02:18:54.000
the risk of missing voices
is careers, relationships,

02:18:54.000 --> 02:18:55.680
and ultimately lives.

02:18:55.680 --> 02:18:57.150
That's the stakes we're talking about.

02:18:57.150 --> 02:18:58.830
So I hope my report's been helpful.

02:18:58.830 --> 02:19:01.860
Happy to take any questions or comments,

02:19:01.860 --> 02:19:03.022
and thank you very much.

02:19:03.022 --> 02:19:03.947
- So, Jayne, thank you.

02:19:03.947 --> 02:19:05.797
I mean, that was really a very cogent

02:19:06.840 --> 02:19:08.550
and thoughtful presentation

02:19:08.550 --> 02:19:09.870
of the key sheets from your report,

02:19:09.870 --> 02:19:11.820
both historically what you are seeing

02:19:11.820 --> 02:19:14.280
and also looking forward
to some of the aspects

02:19:14.280 --> 02:19:16.500
that you are clearly further focused on.

02:19:16.500 --> 02:19:17.333
What I'd like to do,

02:19:17.333 --> 02:19:20.070
if I can hold the board back for a moment,

02:19:20.070 --> 02:19:23.010
I think I would actually
like, if possible,

02:19:23.010 --> 02:19:26.310
Steve, Dorota and Annelise if possible.

02:19:26.310 --> 02:19:27.143
But Steve, do you want to-

02:19:27.143 --> 02:19:29.564
- Yeah, a few introductions.
- Bring in the NHS England,

02:19:29.564 --> 02:19:32.490
FTSU Guardian's Annual Report.

02:19:32.490 --> 02:19:33.797
- Yeah. So a few
introductory words for me.

02:19:33.797 --> 02:19:34.893
So the first thing is

02:19:34.893 --> 02:19:38.610
that this is our annual
report covering 2023 to '24.

02:19:38.610 --> 02:19:41.333
It is a period where Richard Barker,

02:19:41.333 --> 02:19:43.650
you heard at the start of the meeting,

02:19:43.650 --> 02:19:45.390
has recently stepped down and retired

02:19:45.390 --> 02:19:47.550
from being the Regional Director

02:19:47.550 --> 02:19:48.990
for the Northeast and Yorkshire,

02:19:48.990 --> 02:19:51.000
was the Executive Lead and sponsor

02:19:51.000 --> 02:19:52.380
for the Freedom to Speak Up programme.

02:19:52.380 --> 02:19:54.480
So, just to acknowledge all the leadership

02:19:54.480 --> 02:19:58.350
that Richard has given to the
programme over the last year.

02:19:58.350 --> 02:20:01.170
He has now handed it over
to me as of this month.

02:20:01.170 --> 02:20:03.240
It is a role that I have performed before.

02:20:03.240 --> 02:20:06.090
I last did this five years ago.

02:20:06.090 --> 02:20:08.550
And actually coming into
it in the early handover,

02:20:08.550 --> 02:20:11.313
I'm just struck by some
of the similarities,

02:20:12.342 --> 02:20:14.280
as in five years ago,

02:20:14.280 --> 02:20:15.480
we have just gone through a period

02:20:15.480 --> 02:20:17.520
where another organisation,

02:20:17.520 --> 02:20:19.407
the two organisations have come in,

02:20:19.407 --> 02:20:21.120
and so a lot of work has been done.

02:20:21.120 --> 02:20:24.630
I'm glad to say that the unified service

02:20:24.630 --> 02:20:26.900
as a result of the recent bringing in

02:20:26.900 --> 02:20:30.600
of HEE and NHS Digital
has gone live in June.

02:20:30.600 --> 02:20:31.860
So similar challenges

02:20:31.860 --> 02:20:34.380
as the last time when we
brought NHS Improvement in,

02:20:34.380 --> 02:20:36.840
but I can also see how
progress has been made,

02:20:36.840 --> 02:20:38.400
and it's clear to me,

02:20:38.400 --> 02:20:40.980
and you said, Jayne, at the end,

02:20:40.980 --> 02:20:44.490
much has been done and I can
see the work that has been done

02:20:44.490 --> 02:20:48.480
within the team since
I was last responsible.

02:20:48.480 --> 02:20:50.880
But there is clearly still much to do

02:20:50.880 --> 02:20:53.190
and that's highlighted in the report.

02:20:53.190 --> 02:20:55.380
So, just a few things to highlight.

02:20:55.380 --> 02:20:57.390
So in the last year
there's been a real focus

02:20:57.390 --> 02:20:58.290
on a number of things,

02:20:58.290 --> 02:21:00.900
on working closely with HR,

02:21:00.900 --> 02:21:02.820
and ensuring there's an alignment between

02:21:02.820 --> 02:21:04.920
the Freedom to Speak Up
processes and HR processes.

02:21:04.920 --> 02:21:06.000
There is an interface.

02:21:06.000 --> 02:21:09.330
It's important to ensure
that they're aligned.

02:21:09.330 --> 02:21:10.920
There's been work that I
know that's been going on

02:21:10.920 --> 02:21:12.300
with the board and executive team

02:21:12.300 --> 02:21:14.460
about how we can support the guardians

02:21:14.460 --> 02:21:17.400
as a senior leadership better.

02:21:17.400 --> 02:21:18.450
That is always challenging.

02:21:18.450 --> 02:21:20.160
Time is always precious,

02:21:20.160 --> 02:21:23.160
but it is important that the leadership

02:21:23.160 --> 02:21:26.243
of the organisation engages
regularly with our guardians.

02:21:26.243 --> 02:21:28.410
There's a number of ways in
which we've been doing that,

02:21:28.410 --> 02:21:30.150
but we can always do better.

02:21:30.150 --> 02:21:32.290
And I'm glad to say that
the guardians are working

02:21:32.290 --> 02:21:34.630
with the Freedom to Speak Up team closely

02:21:35.602 --> 02:21:37.560
on our new people plan

02:21:37.560 --> 02:21:38.790
because we are still hearing,

02:21:38.790 --> 02:21:40.140
again, through our staff survey,

02:21:40.140 --> 02:21:41.943
that there are barriers to speaking up

02:21:41.943 --> 02:21:44.490
that some of the themes around
behaviours and leadership

02:21:44.490 --> 02:21:45.780
still come up.

02:21:45.780 --> 02:21:47.970
And it's important that
we take the learning

02:21:47.970 --> 02:21:52.970
that the guardians have into
our work on the organisation

02:21:54.180 --> 02:21:57.660
and the plan for the new organisation.

02:21:57.660 --> 02:21:59.460
But it's really important that we,

02:21:59.460 --> 02:22:01.110
in the spirit of listening,

02:22:01.110 --> 02:22:02.940
that we bring our guardians to this

02:22:02.940 --> 02:22:04.470
so that the board can hear directly.

02:22:04.470 --> 02:22:09.060
So we have Annelise and Dorota
who are two of our guardians.

02:22:09.060 --> 02:22:13.530
And so their opportunity to
speak directly to us as a board.

02:22:13.530 --> 02:22:15.420
I think Annelise you're going to go first,

02:22:15.420 --> 02:22:18.240
but let me hand over to you
at the end of the table.

02:22:18.240 --> 02:22:19.340
- Thank you very much.

02:22:20.907 --> 02:22:23.640
So, just some reflections
on cases that I've managed.

02:22:23.640 --> 02:22:28.440
I think the complexity
of them is increasing.

02:22:28.440 --> 02:22:33.210
I think that the handover
between Freedom to Speak Up

02:22:33.210 --> 02:22:36.060
and HR can be often quite complex.

02:22:36.060 --> 02:22:39.750
And indeed navigating that in supporting

02:22:39.750 --> 02:22:42.423
the individual to
understand that complexity,

02:22:43.320 --> 02:22:46.470
especially where we might
get in some circumstances,

02:22:46.470 --> 02:22:50.343
counter-allegations made,
which makes that very complex.

02:22:51.480 --> 02:22:53.700
And keeping sight of Freedom to Speak Up

02:22:53.700 --> 02:22:56.430
in that process is tricky.

02:22:56.430 --> 02:23:00.960
Keeping people physically
and mentally well

02:23:00.960 --> 02:23:05.910
and capable still to
function is a real challenge.

02:23:05.910 --> 02:23:08.580
And I think that's something that we

02:23:08.580 --> 02:23:12.510
really strongly focus on
in Freedom to Speak Up.

02:23:12.510 --> 02:23:16.410
Obviously linking in with
the various relevant elements

02:23:16.410 --> 02:23:19.560
of support networks we have in place.

02:23:19.560 --> 02:23:23.544
Complex confidentiality I think is

02:23:23.544 --> 02:23:24.627
a little bit of a challenge.

02:23:24.627 --> 02:23:27.990
And what I mean by that
is when an individual

02:23:27.990 --> 02:23:32.990
may have spoken up about a
senior person in their team,

02:23:34.560 --> 02:23:38.310
or their directorate, and
indeed the challenge then,

02:23:38.310 --> 02:23:41.220
when the rumour mill
starts within the team,

02:23:41.220 --> 02:23:44.070
actually managing that and
keeping the person safe

02:23:44.070 --> 02:23:46.083
in that situation could be a challenge.

02:23:47.760 --> 02:23:52.290
Also enabling, I think
our very busy directors

02:23:52.290 --> 02:23:55.710
to be able to have the
space and time to reflect

02:23:55.710 --> 02:23:57.916
on some of this learning,

02:23:57.916 --> 02:24:02.916
some of the space to grow,

02:24:03.570 --> 02:24:07.410
and actually heal in a
respect from the team,

02:24:07.410 --> 02:24:08.373
as a team,

02:24:09.240 --> 02:24:11.840
when they come across some
really tricky challenges.

02:24:12.990 --> 02:24:15.060
Lastly, Chair, what I would like to say

02:24:15.060 --> 02:24:18.116
is we've spent an awful long time

02:24:18.116 --> 02:24:19.980
in the beginning part of the meeting

02:24:19.980 --> 02:24:22.293
hearing all of the challenges that go on,

02:24:23.250 --> 02:24:27.797
and indeed the complexity that
we have to manage as an NHS.

02:24:28.984 --> 02:24:31.500
And indeed I'd like to
say thank you to Amanda

02:24:31.500 --> 02:24:35.790
and indeed the NEDs because I
appreciate how busy you are.

02:24:35.790 --> 02:24:40.230
But what you do do is take the
time to actually listen to us

02:24:40.230 --> 02:24:42.615
and come and hear our voices

02:24:42.615 --> 02:24:45.060
and you truly listen in that space.

02:24:45.060 --> 02:24:48.120
And so thank you to the
NEDs, and indeed Amanda,

02:24:48.120 --> 02:24:50.880
and the NGO for all the
support you provide us.

02:24:50.880 --> 02:24:51.969
Thank you.

02:24:51.969 --> 02:24:52.935
- Annelise, thank you very much.

02:24:52.935 --> 02:24:55.290
I mean, you're making some
very strong points more broadly

02:24:55.290 --> 02:24:59.320
about the difficulty in handling
particular cases and so on.

02:24:59.320 --> 02:25:01.290
But thank you for your last remark,

02:25:01.290 --> 02:25:04.410
and I just, actually to
Andrew who's our lead

02:25:04.410 --> 02:25:07.920
for the non-executives in
that sort of relationship

02:25:07.920 --> 02:25:09.930
with our guardians.

02:25:09.930 --> 02:25:11.520
But we've also met with you.

02:25:11.520 --> 02:25:13.770
I think a number of us have
met with you very frequently.

02:25:13.770 --> 02:25:16.020
All of the NEDs actually
at various occasions

02:25:16.020 --> 02:25:16.917
have met with the guardians

02:25:16.917 --> 02:25:21.690
and it's been really helpful
to hear your voices directly.

02:25:21.690 --> 02:25:25.860
But Dorota, now would you
like to come in at this stage?

02:25:25.860 --> 02:25:27.533
- Perfect. Thank you very much.

02:25:27.533 --> 02:25:31.770
So, Dorota Mason, Freedom
to Speak Up Guardian.

02:25:31.770 --> 02:25:36.180
And thank you for inviting me
today to this board meeting.

02:25:36.180 --> 02:25:39.660
So the information I shall
be sharing with you relates

02:25:39.660 --> 02:25:41.730
to grievance cases I have supported

02:25:41.730 --> 02:25:44.880
in relation to bullying and harassment.

02:25:44.880 --> 02:25:46.440
It is really positive to hear,

02:25:46.440 --> 02:25:47.790
I need to emphasise this,

02:25:47.790 --> 02:25:49.830
through the papers that have been shared,

02:25:49.830 --> 02:25:52.740
that we're creating an
employee relations team,

02:25:52.740 --> 02:25:56.370
a mediation service, and
a resolution framework.

02:25:56.370 --> 02:25:58.530
It's really key that guardians feed

02:25:58.530 --> 02:26:01.620
into these areas, I believe.

02:26:01.620 --> 02:26:04.740
And I also speak on behalf of my reporters

02:26:04.740 --> 02:26:09.030
that we cannot forget five key
needs as part of this work.

02:26:09.030 --> 02:26:10.470
So number one,

02:26:10.470 --> 02:26:14.103
to produce a comprehensive
grievance policy and procedure.

02:26:15.090 --> 02:26:18.540
Number two, agreed
timeframes for processes.

02:26:18.540 --> 02:26:22.350
Number three, strive to
produce quality investigation

02:26:22.350 --> 02:26:24.270
and outcome reports.

02:26:24.270 --> 02:26:27.240
Four, agreed touchpoints with reporters.

02:26:27.240 --> 02:26:31.350
And number five, follow
through on recommendations.

02:26:31.350 --> 02:26:33.570
By doing that, this shows commitment

02:26:33.570 --> 02:26:37.020
and continuous improvement
that we are actually listening

02:26:37.020 --> 02:26:40.830
and are being a responsive organisation.

02:26:40.830 --> 02:26:43.650
These five points that I've
referred to are important

02:26:43.650 --> 02:26:48.390
because it really impacts
staff and this organisation.

02:26:48.390 --> 02:26:50.550
We know that without these elements

02:26:50.550 --> 02:26:54.030
this can increase to, you
know, prolonged stress,

02:26:54.030 --> 02:26:58.080
it results in a negative
speaking up experience.

02:26:58.080 --> 02:27:00.554
It can lead to sickness levels rising.

02:27:00.554 --> 02:27:03.360
Talent also leaves the organisation

02:27:03.360 --> 02:27:06.390
and our workplace culture is weakened.

02:27:06.390 --> 02:27:07.920
I think we also need to remember

02:27:07.920 --> 02:27:11.130
that nobody wishes to raise a grievance.

02:27:11.130 --> 02:27:13.470
We therefore need to act early,

02:27:13.470 --> 02:27:16.620
continue striving to be
an excellent organisation,

02:27:16.620 --> 02:27:18.480
and we need to care for our workforce

02:27:18.480 --> 02:27:21.720
because our workforce
is our greatest asset

02:27:21.720 --> 02:27:23.910
and we need to support it.

02:27:23.910 --> 02:27:25.539
Thank you for listening.
- Thank you.

02:27:25.539 --> 02:27:29.130
I'm going to give a few
minutes for people to react

02:27:29.130 --> 02:27:30.570
or just ask questions,

02:27:30.570 --> 02:27:32.430
react or ask questions,

02:27:32.430 --> 02:27:34.140
either of the NGO, Jayne,

02:27:34.140 --> 02:27:38.130
and or Annelise, you or Dorota.

02:27:38.130 --> 02:27:39.150
We're going to try and gather them

02:27:39.150 --> 02:27:42.581
because I've failed to run
the agenda appropriately

02:27:42.581 --> 02:27:43.650
and we've got a very important item

02:27:43.650 --> 02:27:47.160
we still haven't done,
dealt with or heard,

02:27:47.160 --> 02:27:49.680
and Chris and Habib and Bola,

02:27:49.680 --> 02:27:53.220
we really need to hear
you on inequalities.

02:27:53.220 --> 02:27:55.950
So I'm going to try and
gather them quite quickly

02:27:55.950 --> 02:27:57.420
just from around the table.

02:27:57.420 --> 02:28:00.030
I do remember though the
last call we were on together

02:28:00.030 --> 02:28:01.290
with Andrew a couple of weeks ago

02:28:01.290 --> 02:28:03.150
and that point about timely resolution

02:28:03.150 --> 02:28:04.890
because of the strain that carries

02:28:04.890 --> 02:28:07.167
on for the individual, if we are not,

02:28:07.167 --> 02:28:09.600
and I realise complexity
sometimes mean, you know,

02:28:09.600 --> 02:28:12.600
these things are not instantaneous
and they will take time

02:28:12.600 --> 02:28:15.578
but sometimes some of
those times to address

02:28:15.578 --> 02:28:19.320
struck us as quite long
in that conversation

02:28:19.320 --> 02:28:20.550
we had a couple of weeks ago.

02:28:20.550 --> 02:28:21.840
So let's just go around and gather.

02:28:21.840 --> 02:28:24.030
So, Amanda's hand.
- And Andrew.

02:28:24.030 --> 02:28:26.640
Well Andrew should definitely also speak.

02:28:26.640 --> 02:28:29.102
So I've got Mary, Amanda, Andrew,

02:28:29.102 --> 02:28:30.240
should we just do that?

02:28:30.240 --> 02:28:31.581
Mary, do you want to go first quickly?

02:28:31.581 --> 02:28:33.510
- Just very quickly, I
wanted to say thank you

02:28:33.510 --> 02:28:34.920
for such great presentations

02:28:34.920 --> 02:28:35.753
and also for the fact

02:28:35.753 --> 02:28:38.820
that Jayne gave me a
tutorial on this recently

02:28:38.820 --> 02:28:42.123
which was very helpful for me
to get the national picture.

02:28:43.080 --> 02:28:46.200
I just wanted to ask whether,

02:28:46.200 --> 02:28:49.620
as you've got independent
providers getting involved,

02:28:49.620 --> 02:28:52.593
whether all independent providers

02:28:52.593 --> 02:28:55.290
that we place NHS patients in,

02:28:55.290 --> 02:28:58.440
and I'm particularly thinking
about mental health patients,

02:28:58.440 --> 02:29:00.300
have this kind of structure,

02:29:00.300 --> 02:29:02.550
and if not whether in the future

02:29:02.550 --> 02:29:05.921
that might be a criteria
for placing contracts.

02:29:05.921 --> 02:29:07.470
- So it's a normal trick.

02:29:07.470 --> 02:29:09.120
I mean you may have to take notes

02:29:09.120 --> 02:29:10.410
as to who might want to answer that.

02:29:10.410 --> 02:29:12.270
I think that Jayne might
have been aimed at you.

02:29:12.270 --> 02:29:13.983
So Jane, and then Amanda.

02:29:15.450 --> 02:29:16.770
- Oh yeah,

02:29:16.770 --> 02:29:19.544
just very quickly just to say thank you.

02:29:19.544 --> 02:29:21.870
I mean, I'm a relatively
new non-executive director

02:29:21.870 --> 02:29:25.290
and I've found the conversations
that I've been part of

02:29:25.290 --> 02:29:28.500
over the last few months
really helpful and insightful.

02:29:28.500 --> 02:29:31.020
So thank you so much for what you do.

02:29:31.020 --> 02:29:33.060
There was a particularly
thoughtful discussion I thought,

02:29:33.060 --> 02:29:34.470
which you just alluded to,

02:29:34.470 --> 02:29:37.500
on the last call about the interaction

02:29:37.500 --> 02:29:41.070
between sort of thoroughness
of investigation

02:29:41.070 --> 02:29:43.410
and length of resolution

02:29:43.410 --> 02:29:45.567
and the stresses and
strains that come with that.

02:29:45.567 --> 02:29:48.180
And I was particularly
interested in the discussion,

02:29:48.180 --> 02:29:49.890
which is alluded to in the paper as well,

02:29:49.890 --> 02:29:52.080
but about the role of mediation

02:29:52.080 --> 02:29:54.780
and how we perhaps boost that.

02:29:54.780 --> 02:29:56.880
And I look forward to returning to

02:29:56.880 --> 02:29:59.010
that theme in future discussions.

02:29:59.010 --> 02:30:00.758
- Thank you, Jane. So
Amanda do you want to?

02:30:00.758 --> 02:30:01.591
- Yeah, thank you.

02:30:01.591 --> 02:30:02.424
I've got just a question,

02:30:02.424 --> 02:30:04.590
so again it was just to
start by saying thank you

02:30:04.590 --> 02:30:09.000
both to Jayne but also
to Dorota and Annelise

02:30:09.000 --> 02:30:13.110
because I think what you have made us do

02:30:13.110 --> 02:30:14.370
is really stop and listen,

02:30:14.370 --> 02:30:19.370
which ease the job you do
so brilliantly, but also,

02:30:19.470 --> 02:30:21.977
stop doing your finger-tapping, Richard.

02:30:24.508 --> 02:30:26.486
- It actually wasn't about your question,

02:30:26.486 --> 02:30:31.123
which was very good actually, I think.

02:30:31.123 --> 02:30:35.880
- But what I think is, if
I reflect on NHS England,

02:30:35.880 --> 02:30:38.310
the truth is we have
not made it easy for you

02:30:38.310 --> 02:30:39.660
to do your jobs.

02:30:39.660 --> 02:30:42.240
Not only have we done what
we've discussed earlier

02:30:42.240 --> 02:30:44.580
about bringing three
organisations into one,

02:30:44.580 --> 02:30:47.010
the complexity of the inheritance

02:30:47.010 --> 02:30:49.560
of some of the cases that we've got,

02:30:49.560 --> 02:30:52.560
the complexity, as you've already said,

02:30:52.560 --> 02:30:57.210
of the interaction with HR and
some of the Speak Up cases.

02:30:57.210 --> 02:30:58.950
I really want to commend the guardians

02:30:58.950 --> 02:31:01.170
because you've shown
extraordinary resilience

02:31:01.170 --> 02:31:05.427
and frankly patience with
us to keep going with this.

02:31:05.427 --> 02:31:08.730
And I'm delighted that things
are beginning to move forward.

02:31:08.730 --> 02:31:10.570
But I'm really struck Jayne,
by your point earlier on,

02:31:10.570 --> 02:31:12.210
which I think is just true,

02:31:12.210 --> 02:31:15.060
which is what we measure matters.

02:31:15.060 --> 02:31:17.250
So, we're doing a lot internally.

02:31:17.250 --> 02:31:19.770
Steve is leading, looking
at our, if you like,

02:31:19.770 --> 02:31:23.160
our corporate health, so
that we review regularly.

02:31:23.160 --> 02:31:24.600
I mean it's everything from, you know,

02:31:24.600 --> 02:31:26.310
what our vacancy rates are like

02:31:26.310 --> 02:31:28.020
through to our sickness levels,

02:31:28.020 --> 02:31:30.723
you know, grievances, Speak Up cases.

02:31:31.680 --> 02:31:33.060
My question is,

02:31:33.060 --> 02:31:38.060
what one thing would you want
us to be looking at internally

02:31:38.400 --> 02:31:40.440
as a way of making sure that this gets

02:31:40.440 --> 02:31:42.150
the right kind of focus going forward?

02:31:42.150 --> 02:31:43.170
And again, I'm really struck by,

02:31:43.170 --> 02:31:44.670
it's the detail, some of this,

02:31:44.670 --> 02:31:47.820
it's the five steps we
talked before this meeting

02:31:47.820 --> 02:31:50.820
about our response to our
own internal staff survey

02:31:50.820 --> 02:31:54.780
and the six big commitments
that we're making on that.

02:31:54.780 --> 02:31:55.613
But again, it's like,

02:31:55.613 --> 02:31:57.660
it's six, it's five, you know,

02:31:57.660 --> 02:32:00.090
how do we make sure we're looking,

02:32:00.090 --> 02:32:03.780
not lost in so much
detail that we sort of,

02:32:03.780 --> 02:32:05.280
you know, miss the wood for the trees,

02:32:05.280 --> 02:32:06.960
but that we are focusing on asking

02:32:06.960 --> 02:32:09.180
the right questions of ourselves?

02:32:09.180 --> 02:32:12.150
And Jayne, it's my same question to you.

02:32:12.150 --> 02:32:12.983
You know, if again,

02:32:12.983 --> 02:32:14.910
I know the answer won't be just one thing,

02:32:14.910 --> 02:32:17.760
but what one thing would
you be saying to us?

02:32:17.760 --> 02:32:19.890
We've just done a piece of work consulting

02:32:19.890 --> 02:32:21.900
on a new oversight framework for the NHS,

02:32:21.900 --> 02:32:26.900
which is trying to be much more
balanced in pulling in data

02:32:27.060 --> 02:32:29.610
and admittedly it is mostly hard data,

02:32:29.610 --> 02:32:31.920
but data that gives a
much more rounded view

02:32:31.920 --> 02:32:34.860
of everything from patient
experience to outcomes

02:32:34.860 --> 02:32:37.650
to staff experience outcomes, et cetera.

02:32:37.650 --> 02:32:41.010
But what would your ask be
of us around this agenda

02:32:41.010 --> 02:32:43.860
and the point you've made
so powerfully about culture?

02:32:43.860 --> 02:32:45.090
- Well, okay Andrew.

02:32:45.090 --> 02:32:46.490
- So, just building on that-

02:32:47.580 --> 02:32:49.560
- Just so you know, I'm going
to get Andrew and Tanuj,

02:32:49.560 --> 02:32:53.290
and then I'm going to refuse
to catch anybody else's eyes

02:32:54.210 --> 02:32:55.350
to enable you to answer,

02:32:55.350 --> 02:32:57.780
to then move on to the
very important next topic.

02:32:57.780 --> 02:32:59.730
So, Andrew and Tanuj.
- So we have brought

02:32:59.730 --> 02:33:01.620
four organisations into one,

02:33:01.620 --> 02:33:04.140
and Amanda was very clear at the onset

02:33:04.140 --> 02:33:07.440
this was about creating a
new and different culture

02:33:07.440 --> 02:33:11.850
and I think the guardians
are absolutely, you know,

02:33:11.850 --> 02:33:13.830
full on in terms of, you know,

02:33:13.830 --> 02:33:16.170
understanding the commitment that exists

02:33:16.170 --> 02:33:18.180
from all of the exec team

02:33:18.180 --> 02:33:21.540
to try and get Speak
Up into a better place.

02:33:21.540 --> 02:33:24.300
However, the one thing
that always comes across

02:33:24.300 --> 02:33:26.600
in any conversation we
have with the guardians

02:33:26.600 --> 02:33:31.260
as a group of non-execs is
it's the first line manager bit

02:33:31.260 --> 02:33:35.520
and are we tooling up
those people appropriately

02:33:35.520 --> 02:33:38.090
to act, listen and resolve matters

02:33:38.090 --> 02:33:40.377
at the lowest possible operational level.

02:33:40.377 --> 02:33:43.080
And I think that's our
challenge going forward

02:33:43.080 --> 02:33:45.270
because it comes up in every conversation

02:33:45.270 --> 02:33:46.670
that we have with guardians.

02:33:48.060 --> 02:33:51.000
- Thank you for all for a
great programme and great,

02:33:51.000 --> 02:33:53.190
I just have two very
precise set of questions,

02:33:53.190 --> 02:33:54.023
Jayne, for you.

02:33:54.023 --> 02:33:59.023
One was the 1,200+ trained guardians.

02:33:59.370 --> 02:34:03.030
Any sense on what's the
demographic profile of that group

02:34:03.030 --> 02:34:07.800
and how closely or not does
it deflect the demographics

02:34:07.800 --> 02:34:09.540
of the workforce, and
does that even matter?

02:34:09.540 --> 02:34:11.310
I think that's the first question.

02:34:11.310 --> 02:34:14.940
The second one is I note
the 27% increase in cases

02:34:14.940 --> 02:34:17.400
and as you rightly said in your briefing,

02:34:17.400 --> 02:34:18.233
it can be a really good thing.

02:34:18.233 --> 02:34:20.370
You know, it's reflective
of the confidence

02:34:20.370 --> 02:34:21.660
of people going up,

02:34:21.660 --> 02:34:24.870
but seeing what you see, the big picture,

02:34:24.870 --> 02:34:26.970
what is your takeaway on,

02:34:26.970 --> 02:34:29.640
is it increased confidence
of the cases going up

02:34:29.640 --> 02:34:31.590
or do you see a sense of a deterioration

02:34:31.590 --> 02:34:34.680
in the conduct environment or culture?

02:34:34.680 --> 02:34:37.650
So, any sort of colour
on that would be useful,

02:34:37.650 --> 02:34:38.760
but really great update.

02:34:38.760 --> 02:34:39.593
Thank you.

02:34:39.593 --> 02:34:41.580
- So can I very quickly, Jayne,

02:34:41.580 --> 02:34:44.160
do you want to take the
specifics to you first,

02:34:44.160 --> 02:34:45.906
and then Dorota and Annelise,

02:34:45.906 --> 02:34:49.590
if you wanted to react
to the comments made,

02:34:49.590 --> 02:34:50.423
that would be good.

02:34:50.423 --> 02:34:51.330
Janye?
- Certainly.

02:34:51.330 --> 02:34:53.580
So in terms of the demographics
of the guardian population,

02:34:53.580 --> 02:34:54.540
really great question.

02:34:54.540 --> 02:34:58.140
In our report that we do
when we look at guardians

02:34:58.140 --> 02:35:00.763
and we do a guardian survey,

02:35:00.763 --> 02:35:02.697
I don't have the figures
right to hand now,

02:35:02.697 --> 02:35:05.730
but I can circulate that for
you after from the last one,

02:35:05.730 --> 02:35:07.140
but no, they're not reflective,

02:35:07.140 --> 02:35:08.940
and we encourage organisations to,

02:35:08.940 --> 02:35:11.610
when they're recruiting,
as with all recruitment,

02:35:11.610 --> 02:35:12.630
to look at that demographic

02:35:12.630 --> 02:35:14.280
and to really try and positively recruit

02:35:14.280 --> 02:35:17.400
to reflect the broad, you know,

02:35:17.400 --> 02:35:19.920
the broad range of of
workers that there is.

02:35:19.920 --> 02:35:22.140
So, it's a no and it's a yes,

02:35:22.140 --> 02:35:24.570
and we need to do more with that.

02:35:24.570 --> 02:35:26.370
The second in terms of
that increased figures,

02:35:26.370 --> 02:35:27.330
what's my take on that?

02:35:27.330 --> 02:35:29.400
My take on that is if I
look at it in conjunction

02:35:29.400 --> 02:35:31.830
with the staff survey,
it's not that great news,

02:35:31.830 --> 02:35:33.900
it's good that they're
coming to the guardians,

02:35:33.900 --> 02:35:35.550
but with the decreased confidence

02:35:35.550 --> 02:35:37.830
and obviously the staff survey asks

02:35:37.830 --> 02:35:40.200
about just speaking up to any route,

02:35:40.200 --> 02:35:42.960
it's not just talking
about the guardian route.

02:35:42.960 --> 02:35:46.423
What it's saying is we are
not as confident as we were

02:35:46.423 --> 02:35:47.850
and so therefore people are obviously,

02:35:47.850 --> 02:35:50.370
so I think it's not a good news story.

02:35:50.370 --> 02:35:52.050
We need to do more.

02:35:52.050 --> 02:35:53.557
And may I just quickly answer

02:35:53.557 --> 02:35:56.040
Amanda's question?
- Yeah, of course.

02:35:56.040 --> 02:35:57.180
- For one thing,

02:35:57.180 --> 02:35:58.470
it's actually going to
be two wrapped up in one,

02:35:58.470 --> 02:35:59.303
which is naughty.

02:35:59.303 --> 02:36:00.720
But there isn't just one thing,

02:36:00.720 --> 02:36:03.120
I've got a shopping list for you.

02:36:03.120 --> 02:36:06.150
I think it's that whole
point about measurement,

02:36:06.150 --> 02:36:10.920
and you as a regulator and
as the leadership of the NHS

02:36:10.920 --> 02:36:14.700
setting that tone for what's
measured, what you measure,

02:36:14.700 --> 02:36:17.070
but also what you are asking of others

02:36:17.070 --> 02:36:19.080
to report on around their culture

02:36:19.080 --> 02:36:20.280
and their improvement plans

02:36:20.280 --> 02:36:22.680
and more looking at the compliance.

02:36:22.680 --> 02:36:24.060
Because that links in with the question

02:36:24.060 --> 02:36:25.170
about private sector.

02:36:25.170 --> 02:36:27.510
And if I can quickly answer
that, and then I'll shut up,

02:36:27.510 --> 02:36:31.320
in terms of anybody providing NHS services

02:36:31.320 --> 02:36:34.770
under the NHS contract
should have a guardian

02:36:34.770 --> 02:36:37.080
and be looking at their culture.

02:36:37.080 --> 02:36:40.184
But again, that comes back to
what's the compliance on that.

02:36:40.184 --> 02:36:42.270
CQC when they're doing
their valid inspections

02:36:42.270 --> 02:36:43.830
should look at that.

02:36:43.830 --> 02:36:45.660
But in terms of capacity

02:36:45.660 --> 02:36:48.570
and that whole compliance
piece from your perspectives,

02:36:48.570 --> 02:36:51.231
I think that we've got more to do on that.

02:36:51.231 --> 02:36:52.447
- Thank you very much.

02:36:52.447 --> 02:36:55.048
And Annelise or Dorota, do
you want to react at all

02:36:55.048 --> 02:36:57.870
to any of the points made?

02:36:57.870 --> 02:36:59.473
- Thank you very much.

02:36:59.473 --> 02:37:01.683
So I think my one thing,

02:37:05.160 --> 02:37:06.630
it's going to be really daft to say,

02:37:06.630 --> 02:37:09.840
I think we need to be more
humanistic in our approach

02:37:09.840 --> 02:37:14.840
and indeed enable our policies,
processes and procedures

02:37:15.720 --> 02:37:19.860
to be a lot more humanistic in navigating

02:37:19.860 --> 02:37:22.470
some of these challenges we face.

02:37:22.470 --> 02:37:27.470
I think that's probably
my biggest reflection.

02:37:27.510 --> 02:37:32.250
I think we have situations
where, for instance,

02:37:32.250 --> 02:37:35.190
one case where we have
10 people speaking up

02:37:35.190 --> 02:37:38.370
about a senior person in a team,

02:37:38.370 --> 02:37:40.963
yet because everybody feels so unsafe

02:37:40.963 --> 02:37:43.713
to be able to step into the space to say,

02:37:45.023 --> 02:37:47.673
I will stand against a grievance,

02:37:48.690 --> 02:37:52.950
we're disabled almost to be
able to push something forward.

02:37:52.950 --> 02:37:54.810
So I think it's being more humanistic

02:37:54.810 --> 02:37:57.660
in enabling our policies,
processes and procedures

02:37:57.660 --> 02:37:59.253
to actually act on things.

02:37:59.253 --> 02:38:02.229
It's time for change on
our policies, I feel.

02:38:02.229 --> 02:38:03.300
Thank you.
- Thank you very much.

02:38:03.300 --> 02:38:04.350
- Thank you.
- So...

02:38:07.096 --> 02:38:09.429
- May I go?
- Your one thing.

02:38:11.021 --> 02:38:12.810
- I would say,

02:38:12.810 --> 02:38:14.400
and we've done some work around this,

02:38:14.400 --> 02:38:15.960
so I need to caveat this,

02:38:15.960 --> 02:38:19.230
is that we need to equip
line managers and staff

02:38:19.230 --> 02:38:20.063
with the skills,

02:38:20.063 --> 02:38:23.040
and I say staff as well, moving forward,

02:38:23.040 --> 02:38:24.640
because it's very difficult

02:38:25.710 --> 02:38:29.040
having these difficult conversations.

02:38:29.040 --> 02:38:30.330
It is a real skill.

02:38:30.330 --> 02:38:34.620
I'm not quite sure whether an
e-learning module covers this

02:38:34.620 --> 02:38:37.200
or whether an hours training session.

02:38:37.200 --> 02:38:39.330
I'm going to go back to the days

02:38:39.330 --> 02:38:43.740
where we used to take out a
full day and it was interactive

02:38:43.740 --> 02:38:46.350
and I know people don't
wish to do that sometimes,

02:38:46.350 --> 02:38:49.260
but it is actually super helpful.

02:38:49.260 --> 02:38:51.270
So I think we just need that commitment

02:38:51.270 --> 02:38:54.090
to support our staff in
relation to behaviours,

02:38:54.090 --> 02:38:57.480
how we listen, how we
take that information in,

02:38:57.480 --> 02:39:00.120
and what do we do with that information.

02:39:00.120 --> 02:39:02.130
And I'm going to be really
cheeky and follow Jayne

02:39:02.130 --> 02:39:03.990
and I'm going to say the other point is

02:39:03.990 --> 02:39:06.600
is that if we do not have enough staff

02:39:06.600 --> 02:39:10.020
to support some of these
mechanisms and processes,

02:39:10.020 --> 02:39:13.050
and potentially outsource the panel,

02:39:13.050 --> 02:39:16.680
potentially outsource the
investigating officers as well.

02:39:16.680 --> 02:39:17.520
- Thank you very much.

02:39:17.520 --> 02:39:20.034
So I'm going to thank Jayne for-

02:39:20.034 --> 02:39:22.410
- May I just endorse that,

02:39:22.410 --> 02:39:23.910
the work and I think, again,

02:39:23.910 --> 02:39:25.200
the work that we need to do

02:39:25.200 --> 02:39:27.900
for preparing managers
to be good managers,

02:39:27.900 --> 02:39:31.500
and that's across the NHS,
is absolutely essential.

02:39:31.500 --> 02:39:33.630
It is for leaders and we
know that works in train

02:39:33.630 --> 02:39:34.920
but I think the management piece,

02:39:34.920 --> 02:39:35.850
we are not born managers.

02:39:35.850 --> 02:39:37.993
We have to learn usually by our mistakes.

02:39:37.993 --> 02:39:40.777
So, I really endorse that. Thank you.

02:39:40.777 --> 02:39:42.960
- So Jayne, can I thank you,

02:39:42.960 --> 02:39:46.560
and can I also thank
Annelise, you and Dorota,

02:39:46.560 --> 02:39:48.567
for your contribution and the discussion.

02:39:48.567 --> 02:39:52.140
And Steve, thank you very
much for taking this on,

02:39:52.140 --> 02:39:54.360
and Andrew as always thank
you on behalf of the NEDs

02:39:54.360 --> 02:39:58.560
for acting as the lead NED
with regard to FTSU Guardians

02:39:58.560 --> 02:39:59.877
and all the feedback and so on.

02:39:59.877 --> 02:40:02.580
So, thank you. That was
a very good conversation.

02:40:02.580 --> 02:40:05.220
We're going to go to the
last substantive item.

02:40:05.220 --> 02:40:06.660
I'm going to apologise and say,

02:40:06.660 --> 02:40:08.610
we're going to go a few minutes over,

02:40:08.610 --> 02:40:11.550
probably 5, 6, 7 minutes
over the scheduled time.

02:40:11.550 --> 02:40:12.883
So, my apologies for that.

02:40:12.883 --> 02:40:14.430
I do know one or two of you

02:40:14.430 --> 02:40:17.343
have to leave on the dot
actually, so I do apologise.

02:40:18.570 --> 02:40:22.290
Delighted to see you again, Habib, here,

02:40:22.290 --> 02:40:23.510
and we've seen each
other a number of times

02:40:23.510 --> 02:40:25.200
in various other areas.

02:40:25.200 --> 02:40:27.630
So, Dr. Habib Naqvi, and also Bola,

02:40:27.630 --> 02:40:29.730
as always good to see you on this topic.

02:40:29.730 --> 02:40:32.408
But Chris do you want to open that topic?

02:40:32.408 --> 02:40:34.470
- So, hello, my name is Chris Hopson,

02:40:34.470 --> 02:40:35.970
I'm the Chief Strategy Officer.

02:40:35.970 --> 02:40:38.340
And Richard, as you know, this
is our regular opportunity

02:40:38.340 --> 02:40:42.060
to discuss our work on reducing
healthcare inequalities,

02:40:42.060 --> 02:40:44.790
including the work of the
Race and Health Observatory.

02:40:44.790 --> 02:40:46.080
I think it's important to note, Richard,

02:40:46.080 --> 02:40:48.390
that this is a priority
for the new government

02:40:48.390 --> 02:40:50.280
and that's come across clearly in a number

02:40:50.280 --> 02:40:53.610
of the ministerial meetings
that we've had so far.

02:40:53.610 --> 02:40:54.570
Worth saying, I think,

02:40:54.570 --> 02:40:57.300
that the manifesto on which
this government was elected

02:40:57.300 --> 02:41:00.810
stated the UK has stark
health inequalities,

02:41:00.810 --> 02:41:03.300
it cannot be right that
life expectancy varies

02:41:03.300 --> 02:41:05.760
so widely across and within communities.

02:41:05.760 --> 02:41:06.990
As part of our health mission,

02:41:06.990 --> 02:41:09.120
we will tackle the social
determinants of health,

02:41:09.120 --> 02:41:11.280
halving the gap in healthy life expectancy

02:41:11.280 --> 02:41:14.370
between the richest and
poorest regions in England.

02:41:14.370 --> 02:41:17.217
The paper that we've circulated
sets out the approach

02:41:17.217 --> 02:41:18.960
and the infrastructure we've developed

02:41:18.960 --> 02:41:20.880
to reduce inequality in the area

02:41:20.880 --> 02:41:24.240
where we in the NHS have direct control

02:41:24.240 --> 02:41:27.480
and that's the actual delivery
of healthcare services.

02:41:27.480 --> 02:41:28.830
But obviously we need to recognise

02:41:28.830 --> 02:41:30.510
that we'll want to join with government,

02:41:30.510 --> 02:41:31.770
other public services,

02:41:31.770 --> 02:41:34.560
our voluntary and community
sector partners in the nation

02:41:34.560 --> 02:41:38.370
as a whole in tackling those
wider determinants of health

02:41:38.370 --> 02:41:41.460
to deliver the government's
wider health mission.

02:41:41.460 --> 02:41:43.860
The paper just sets out
I think really clearly

02:41:43.860 --> 02:41:46.710
how we created the Core20PLUS5 framework,

02:41:46.710 --> 02:41:48.720
which sets out really clearly I think

02:41:48.720 --> 02:41:51.660
for frontline healthcare
delivery organisations.

02:41:51.660 --> 02:41:53.970
Gives them the means of
understanding what actions

02:41:53.970 --> 02:41:56.280
should be being taken
and why it's important.

02:41:56.280 --> 02:41:59.160
It sets out how we've built
a consistent delivery network

02:41:59.160 --> 02:42:02.100
across the NHS to ensure
that local NHS organisations

02:42:02.100 --> 02:42:05.010
have got the capacity and
capability to do what's needed.

02:42:05.010 --> 02:42:07.650
It sets out how we've created
strategic partnerships

02:42:07.650 --> 02:42:08.640
of which our partnership

02:42:08.640 --> 02:42:11.370
with the Race and Health Observatory

02:42:11.370 --> 02:42:13.800
to tackle race and
health inequality is key.

02:42:13.800 --> 02:42:15.720
It also sets out how
we've provided guidance

02:42:15.720 --> 02:42:17.760
to support frontline NHS organisations

02:42:17.760 --> 02:42:21.150
to take action in the required
areas of intervention.

02:42:21.150 --> 02:42:24.540
But this, the next bit
that we now need to do,

02:42:24.540 --> 02:42:26.460
having set up that infrastructure,

02:42:26.460 --> 02:42:28.740
and you might argue is the key challenge,

02:42:28.740 --> 02:42:30.330
it's to ensure that we do

02:42:30.330 --> 02:42:32.970
measurably reduce healthcare inequalities

02:42:32.970 --> 02:42:35.877
and that we have the right
means to measure that.

02:42:35.877 --> 02:42:37.680
And again, we've set out in the paper

02:42:37.680 --> 02:42:39.750
about how we are doing that.

02:42:39.750 --> 02:42:41.190
But look Richard, what
we thought we would do

02:42:41.190 --> 02:42:45.780
is really bring this
to life by asking Habib

02:42:45.780 --> 02:42:48.210
and then Bola to just basically set out

02:42:48.210 --> 02:42:51.930
some concrete examples of
how the work that's been done

02:42:51.930 --> 02:42:55.200
over the last year has really
made an impact on the ground.

02:42:55.200 --> 02:42:56.687
So Habib, I pass over to you.

02:42:56.687 --> 02:42:58.350
- Great, thank you. Thank you, Chris.

02:42:58.350 --> 02:42:59.490
Habib Naqvi, Chief Executive

02:42:59.490 --> 02:43:01.683
of the NHS Race and Health Observatory.

02:43:02.580 --> 02:43:04.110
Over the last year, the Observatory's work

02:43:04.110 --> 02:43:06.515
can be grouped into three areas,

02:43:06.515 --> 02:43:10.140
looking at bringing
together the evidence base

02:43:10.140 --> 02:43:13.920
where it exists or establishing
it where it doesn't exist.

02:43:13.920 --> 02:43:15.270
Secondly, turning that evidence

02:43:15.270 --> 02:43:18.090
into policy recommendations for change.

02:43:18.090 --> 02:43:20.790
And thirdly, supporting
the system with regards

02:43:20.790 --> 02:43:22.680
to improvement and change.

02:43:22.680 --> 02:43:25.740
So I'm just going to give a
very quick couple of examples

02:43:25.740 --> 02:43:28.260
on those three areas.

02:43:28.260 --> 02:43:33.260
Firstly, last year we published
the We Deserve Better Report

02:43:33.480 --> 02:43:35.310
looking at access to healthcare

02:43:35.310 --> 02:43:37.200
for people with learning disabilities.

02:43:37.200 --> 02:43:40.890
You may remember the stark
statistic around life expectancy

02:43:40.890 --> 02:43:43.560
If you're a white person
with learning disability,

02:43:43.560 --> 02:43:45.690
your life expectancy,

02:43:45.690 --> 02:43:48.150
your average life expectancy is 62 years.

02:43:48.150 --> 02:43:50.250
If you're from a black
or Asian background,

02:43:50.250 --> 02:43:52.263
it's only 34 years.

02:43:53.100 --> 02:43:54.420
On the back of that report,

02:43:54.420 --> 02:43:57.240
and since publishing
that report last year,

02:43:57.240 --> 02:43:59.700
we've seen recording
of ethnicity introduced

02:43:59.700 --> 02:44:03.150
into GP learning disability registers

02:44:03.150 --> 02:44:07.800
as part of the quality outcomes
framework for health checks.

02:44:07.800 --> 02:44:10.320
We've seen awareness raising

02:44:10.320 --> 02:44:12.930
within communities around
annual health checks.

02:44:12.930 --> 02:44:14.469
On Tuesday this week I was with

02:44:14.469 --> 02:44:16.080
the Race Equality Foundation

02:44:16.080 --> 02:44:17.670
and Learning Disability England,

02:44:17.670 --> 02:44:19.775
where they produced community videos

02:44:19.775 --> 02:44:22.680
that will be distributed out.

02:44:22.680 --> 02:44:26.370
And thirdly, we're looking at
the nursing workforce as well,

02:44:26.370 --> 02:44:30.300
increasing representation
within the nursing workforce

02:44:30.300 --> 02:44:32.640
and particularly the learning
disability nursing workforce

02:44:32.640 --> 02:44:35.700
from people from a ethnic
minority background.

02:44:35.700 --> 02:44:37.080
Also, last year we published

02:44:37.080 --> 02:44:40.680
the Racial Inequalities in
Neonatal Testing Report,

02:44:40.680 --> 02:44:42.930
looking at the racial bias that exists

02:44:42.930 --> 02:44:45.600
in some clinical tests
such as the APGAR score,

02:44:45.600 --> 02:44:48.270
and in testing for jaundice, for example.

02:44:48.270 --> 02:44:51.250
We've taken that forward
some work that we've

02:44:52.110 --> 02:44:55.890
just commissioned Manchester
University NHS Foundation Trust

02:44:55.890 --> 02:45:00.780
to look at jaundice testing
and in particular looking

02:45:00.780 --> 02:45:04.830
at transcutaneous bilirubin
monitoring devices

02:45:04.830 --> 02:45:07.800
which test and follow the process through

02:45:07.800 --> 02:45:10.470
in terms of diagnosis of jaundice.

02:45:10.470 --> 02:45:13.590
And also earlier this year
the Chief Midwifery Officer

02:45:13.590 --> 02:45:16.290
wrote a letter to all hospital trusts

02:45:16.290 --> 02:45:21.290
offering mannequins
and birthing simulators

02:45:21.570 --> 02:45:24.540
in different skin tones
on the back of our report.

02:45:24.540 --> 02:45:28.590
And within 24 hours of
sending that letter out,

02:45:28.590 --> 02:45:32.010
a hundred maternity
services actually responded

02:45:32.010 --> 02:45:33.870
positively to that offer.

02:45:33.870 --> 02:45:37.170
And now we have nearly every
single maternity service

02:45:37.170 --> 02:45:42.170
across the country that has
simulators and mannequins

02:45:42.360 --> 02:45:46.053
in different skin tone and
different skin colours.

02:45:47.310 --> 02:45:50.700
With regards to policy change,
our very first piece of work

02:45:50.700 --> 02:45:54.780
looked at racial bias in pulse oximetry.

02:45:54.780 --> 02:45:57.540
This was at the peak of the pandemic.

02:45:57.540 --> 02:46:00.453
On the back of that report,

02:46:01.290 --> 02:46:04.542
there were changes made
to the NHS Choices website

02:46:04.542 --> 02:46:07.620
with regards with guidance
on home pulse oximeters.

02:46:07.620 --> 02:46:10.620
NIHR put out a very substantial
call for further evidence,

02:46:10.620 --> 02:46:13.770
particularly from the UK on that area.

02:46:13.770 --> 02:46:15.720
And the Secretary of State
for Health and Social Care

02:46:15.720 --> 02:46:18.780
at the time announced a
government commissioned

02:46:18.780 --> 02:46:22.500
independent review looking
at equity in medical devices,

02:46:22.500 --> 02:46:26.070
which we were grateful to see
published earlier this year

02:46:26.070 --> 02:46:28.200
led by Dame Margaret Whitehead,

02:46:28.200 --> 02:46:30.600
which emphasised the
recommendations that we put

02:46:30.600 --> 02:46:32.673
in our report as well.

02:46:33.644 --> 02:46:35.700
And then implementation,
which is a third area.

02:46:35.700 --> 02:46:36.900
Earlier this year we launched

02:46:36.900 --> 02:46:38.160
the Learning and Action Network,

02:46:38.160 --> 02:46:40.890
looking at the stark
inequalities that we see

02:46:40.890 --> 02:46:43.740
in maternal and neonatal outcomes.

02:46:43.740 --> 02:46:45.780
Working with other organisations

02:46:45.780 --> 02:46:47.190
such as the Health Foundation

02:46:47.190 --> 02:46:50.310
and the Institute for
Healthcare Improvement.

02:46:50.310 --> 02:46:52.470
We've introduced an anti-racism approach

02:46:52.470 --> 02:46:54.750
to quality improvement,
which we're modelling

02:46:54.750 --> 02:46:57.390
with regards to maternal
and neonatal outcomes,

02:46:57.390 --> 02:47:00.600
using that as a kind
of test model or proxy

02:47:00.600 --> 02:47:02.610
to look at other conditions in due course.

02:47:02.610 --> 02:47:06.600
So we will be evaluating
and hopefully scaling

02:47:06.600 --> 02:47:07.800
and spreading the good practise

02:47:07.800 --> 02:47:11.790
that we get from the the
eight ICS pilot sites

02:47:11.790 --> 02:47:14.940
that are undertaking this
programme across the country.

02:47:14.940 --> 02:47:18.000
And we were grateful for
the support of the CNO

02:47:18.000 --> 02:47:19.230
and the Chief Midwifery Officer

02:47:19.230 --> 02:47:21.810
at the launch event in January.

02:47:21.810 --> 02:47:24.300
Looking ahead, we'd be
launching our strategy

02:47:24.300 --> 02:47:27.210
with a new contract and
a new work programme

02:47:27.210 --> 02:47:29.640
for the Observatory in the autumn.

02:47:29.640 --> 02:47:31.560
Not just highlighting, as Amanda says,

02:47:31.560 --> 02:47:32.520
the scale of the challenge,

02:47:32.520 --> 02:47:35.580
but coming up with some
very practical solutions

02:47:35.580 --> 02:47:37.143
to those challenges.

02:47:38.190 --> 02:47:40.170
- Thank you.
- Thank you, Chris.

02:47:40.170 --> 02:47:41.003
- And Bola?

02:47:42.030 --> 02:47:42.870
- Thank you, Chair.

02:47:42.870 --> 02:47:47.130
And thank you again for the
opportunity to update the board

02:47:47.130 --> 02:47:48.570
in terms of the programme of work

02:47:48.570 --> 02:47:52.050
we've been driving forward
over the last year.

02:47:52.050 --> 02:47:55.590
I'm just going to talk through
how we've arranged our work

02:47:55.590 --> 02:47:58.890
and some specific examples
of what's been going on.

02:47:58.890 --> 02:48:02.700
So firstly, we've worked
to focus system effort

02:48:02.700 --> 02:48:06.750
and investment on our
most disadvantaged people

02:48:06.750 --> 02:48:09.420
and communities around three things.

02:48:09.420 --> 02:48:13.110
One, the five strategic
priorities that we've set out,

02:48:13.110 --> 02:48:15.683
the three recovery plans from NHS England,

02:48:15.683 --> 02:48:20.250
and the Core20PLUS5 approach
as has been described.

02:48:20.250 --> 02:48:22.710
And the examples I'm going to talk through

02:48:22.710 --> 02:48:27.000
then talk about those
three themes if you like.

02:48:27.000 --> 02:48:28.740
Peter and David earlier on alluded

02:48:28.740 --> 02:48:32.040
to the targeted lung
cancer check programme,

02:48:32.040 --> 02:48:34.860
which I will hold up as a great example

02:48:34.860 --> 02:48:36.870
within the Core20PLUS5 framework

02:48:36.870 --> 02:48:40.350
that when we focus investment and energy

02:48:40.350 --> 02:48:43.440
in a particular part of the population,

02:48:43.440 --> 02:48:45.660
we can demonstrate impact.

02:48:45.660 --> 02:48:50.660
So the increase to 76% of
early stage cancer diagnosis

02:48:51.060 --> 02:48:54.680
has actually been largely
due to the bottom 10%

02:48:54.680 --> 02:48:56.190
of the population,

02:48:56.190 --> 02:48:58.350
which again is just a great example

02:48:58.350 --> 02:49:01.170
of the targeted investment and effort.

02:49:01.170 --> 02:49:03.150
In relation to sickle cell disorder,

02:49:03.150 --> 02:49:05.070
which affects predominantly people

02:49:05.070 --> 02:49:08.460
of Black African population groups,

02:49:08.460 --> 02:49:11.820
we've had Amanda's mandate
and executive sponsorship

02:49:11.820 --> 02:49:14.160
to drive forward a programme
of quality improvement

02:49:14.160 --> 02:49:15.330
in that regard.

02:49:15.330 --> 02:49:17.550
And I just want to call
out a particular example

02:49:17.550 --> 02:49:19.440
of the Voxeltor drug,

02:49:19.440 --> 02:49:21.390
which is now available to people living

02:49:21.390 --> 02:49:23.190
with sickle cell disorder.

02:49:23.190 --> 02:49:25.650
And when you consider the inequalities

02:49:25.650 --> 02:49:27.540
in the product pipeline,

02:49:27.540 --> 02:49:30.300
it's a really important advancement

02:49:30.300 --> 02:49:31.710
that people with sickle cell

02:49:31.710 --> 02:49:34.620
now have access to this medication

02:49:34.620 --> 02:49:37.830
that reduces their need
for blood transfusion,

02:49:37.830 --> 02:49:39.960
and therefore it's good for the patients,

02:49:39.960 --> 02:49:41.280
it's good for the NHS,

02:49:41.280 --> 02:49:43.800
it reduces the frequency of crisis.

02:49:43.800 --> 02:49:46.350
And so from a patient
experience point of view,

02:49:46.350 --> 02:49:48.543
it's a very significant step forward.

02:49:49.680 --> 02:49:50.700
Also in relation

02:49:50.700 --> 02:49:53.340
to the urgent and emergency
care recovery plan,

02:49:53.340 --> 02:49:55.890
we've been working with the UEC team

02:49:55.890 --> 02:49:59.130
on the high intensity use service

02:49:59.130 --> 02:50:01.500
and a great example is
that new cross hospital

02:50:01.500 --> 02:50:06.330
in Wolverhampton that
I've seen a 58% reduction

02:50:06.330 --> 02:50:08.610
in people using accident and emergency,

02:50:08.610 --> 02:50:13.470
especially amongst the
highest users of A&amp;E.

02:50:13.470 --> 02:50:14.580
They've looked at their data

02:50:14.580 --> 02:50:18.960
and 60% of the people who were
using A&amp;E disproportionately

02:50:18.960 --> 02:50:21.510
were from the most deprived communities.

02:50:21.510 --> 02:50:25.590
And so a 58% reduction in that utilisation

02:50:25.590 --> 02:50:29.250
is a great example again
of how actually action

02:50:29.250 --> 02:50:31.770
to tackle health inequalities can make

02:50:31.770 --> 02:50:34.830
a material contribution to reduction

02:50:34.830 --> 02:50:37.140
in service pressures for the NHS,

02:50:37.140 --> 02:50:39.033
and boost our productivity as well.

02:50:39.930 --> 02:50:43.020
Amanda referenced innovation
in your opening remarks

02:50:43.020 --> 02:50:44.640
and I just wanted to describe

02:50:44.640 --> 02:50:47.640
the Innovation for Health
Inequalities programme,

02:50:47.640 --> 02:50:51.360
which is a significant investment
that was made last year

02:50:51.360 --> 02:50:55.440
in collaboration with the
Accelerated Access Collaborative.

02:50:55.440 --> 02:50:57.630
And an example of where that has worked

02:50:57.630 --> 02:51:02.220
is with health innovation in
Northeast and North Cumbria,

02:51:02.220 --> 02:51:06.090
where they focused on their
South Asian, Black African,

02:51:06.090 --> 02:51:08.130
and the most socioeconomically deprived

02:51:08.130 --> 02:51:10.680
white population group to increase

02:51:10.680 --> 02:51:14.280
the diagnosis of high
cholesterol in these groups.

02:51:14.280 --> 02:51:16.890
The board may be aware
that raised cholesterol

02:51:16.890 --> 02:51:19.710
is associated with heart
attacks and strokes,

02:51:19.710 --> 02:51:22.710
and they were able to
demonstrate an increase

02:51:22.710 --> 02:51:24.570
in the diagnosis of high cholesterol

02:51:24.570 --> 02:51:26.430
in the populations that will otherwise

02:51:26.430 --> 02:51:28.020
not have come forward

02:51:28.020 --> 02:51:31.683
if not for the Innovation for
Health Inequalities programme.

02:51:32.670 --> 02:51:35.850
I also then want to
finally talk about dental.

02:51:35.850 --> 02:51:40.320
So, NHS Suffolk and Northeast Essex ICB

02:51:40.320 --> 02:51:42.030
under the leadership of Ed Garratt,

02:51:42.030 --> 02:51:46.470
and I commend his blog that
came out yesterday to the board.

02:51:46.470 --> 02:51:49.260
They have entered into
a creative partnership

02:51:49.260 --> 02:51:51.840
with Suffolk University

02:51:51.840 --> 02:51:55.380
to deliver an NHS-only dental service

02:51:55.380 --> 02:51:59.490
focusing on homeless people, carers,

02:51:59.490 --> 02:52:01.680
and also care leavers,

02:52:01.680 --> 02:52:03.570
really to focus the investment

02:52:03.570 --> 02:52:06.540
on the most vulnerable people in that ICB.

02:52:06.540 --> 02:52:08.190
And it's achieving two things.

02:52:08.190 --> 02:52:10.140
One, access to dentistry

02:52:10.140 --> 02:52:12.210
for the most vulnerable population groups,

02:52:12.210 --> 02:52:17.010
but more importantly the
training of the dental workforce

02:52:17.010 --> 02:52:20.463
is the thing that is particularly
striking about this model.

02:52:21.300 --> 02:52:25.110
And to reflect on why
some of these initiatives

02:52:25.110 --> 02:52:28.350
are gaining traction and making
a significant difference,

02:52:28.350 --> 02:52:31.110
I just want to talk about the core themes.

02:52:31.110 --> 02:52:36.110
One is the tight definition
of the population of focus,

02:52:38.160 --> 02:52:41.280
tightly defined interventions,

02:52:41.280 --> 02:52:43.770
targeting of the funding,

